WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.420
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:14.940
PRESENTER: Welcome
everyone to 10250.

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:18.220
We also have people
in 4270 and 4370.

00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:21.390
Hello, if you can see me.

00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:23.260
We'd like to welcome everyone.

00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:25.110
This is a really
important occasion.

00:00:25.110 --> 00:00:26.910
We're on the eve of war.

00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:28.960
So thank you for being here.

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:33.600
This is MIT's second evening of
events sponsored by the MIT's

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:35.220
Initiative of Peace.

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:37.770
And yesterday's event
was a tremendous success,

00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:42.810
with over 3,000 people attending
Reverend Jesse Jackson's speech

00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:47.280
in which he urged us to join
our community fellow members

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:50.285
and study war no more.

00:00:50.285 --> 00:00:52.660
[APPLAUSE]

00:00:56.940 --> 00:01:00.610
This was then followed by the
inauguration of the MIT student

00:01:00.610 --> 00:01:06.090
center to be the Martin Luther
King, Jr. Peace Center, where

00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:10.660
we will be having many different
various activities organized.

00:01:10.660 --> 00:01:13.080
For example, we
have discussions,

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:15.690
open discussions, workshops.

00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:18.030
There'll be newspapers,
political art,

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:20.370
political theater, many,
many different things.

00:01:20.370 --> 00:01:21.960
And we urge all
of you to join us

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:24.780
after this talk to continue
the activities that

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:28.590
will be going on in the
peace center this evening.

00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:31.317
With no further
ado, here's Noam.

00:01:31.317 --> 00:01:33.802
[CHEERING]

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:06.980
CHOMSKY: I guess it's not
considered good form to start

00:02:06.980 --> 00:02:10.100
by admonishing your audience.

00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:14.300
But I don't think it's
a time for cheering.

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:19.580
In fact, it's not easy to
talk about this topic at all

00:02:19.580 --> 00:02:23.840
when we're just short
of a decision that's

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:27.150
going to mean the
slaughter of tens

00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:29.300
if not hundreds of
thousands of people

00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:33.890
and that may set a large
part of the world in flames.

00:02:33.890 --> 00:02:39.260
And that's exactly what
is soon going to happen.

00:02:39.260 --> 00:02:41.180
I suppose there are
still possibilities

00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:42.170
that it may be averted.

00:02:42.170 --> 00:02:44.390
But right now, they
look pretty slim.

00:02:44.390 --> 00:02:48.200
Well, as I say, it's not
easy to talk about it calmly.

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:51.620
But since I really can't
think of much else to do,

00:02:51.620 --> 00:02:56.600
that's what I will proceed
to do, for what it's worth.

00:02:56.600 --> 00:02:59.720
At least we can try to
understand what's going on

00:02:59.720 --> 00:03:02.720
and use such
understanding as we can

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:05.780
get as a basis of making some
reasonable decision about how

00:03:05.780 --> 00:03:08.570
to respond to this and many
other things like it that

00:03:08.570 --> 00:03:10.880
are going to follow.

00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:15.770
When Saddam Hussein
invaded Kuwait on August 2,

00:03:15.770 --> 00:03:19.820
there were two responses.

00:03:19.820 --> 00:03:27.170
And they were both very strong
and pretty much unprecedented.

00:03:27.170 --> 00:03:30.170
One response, which went
through the United Nations,

00:03:30.170 --> 00:03:34.250
was sanctions and embargo.

00:03:34.250 --> 00:03:38.430
And those sanctions were
of unprecedented severity.

00:03:38.430 --> 00:03:41.270
I mean, there's been nothing
remotely like them even

00:03:41.270 --> 00:03:45.290
in cases much worse than
this latest crime of Saddam

00:03:45.290 --> 00:03:48.340
Hussein's.

00:03:48.340 --> 00:03:51.520
The other response
was preparation

00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:56.830
for war, quick preparation
for war, within a day or two.

00:03:56.830 --> 00:03:58.720
That preparation
for war had nothing

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:00.370
to do with the
annexation of Kuwait.

00:04:00.370 --> 00:04:03.400
In fact, it took place
well before the annexation.

00:04:03.400 --> 00:04:06.040
So it had to do
with the invasion.

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:07.930
And at the time
that it took place,

00:04:07.930 --> 00:04:10.690
there was essentially nothing
to distinguish Saddam Hussein's

00:04:10.690 --> 00:04:13.760
invasion of Kuwait
from, for example,

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:16.630
George Bush's invasion of
Panama a couple of months

00:04:16.630 --> 00:04:20.980
earlier, which was probably
more bloody and destructive.

00:04:20.980 --> 00:04:24.190
Now, these two responses
separate world opinion

00:04:24.190 --> 00:04:26.530
quite substantially.

00:04:26.530 --> 00:04:29.200
The first response had pretty
broad support, at least

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:30.760
in the industrial world--

00:04:30.760 --> 00:04:31.870
the sanctions.

00:04:31.870 --> 00:04:34.660
And sanctions, of course,
means negotiations.

00:04:34.660 --> 00:04:37.870
Sanctions means that
you put pressure

00:04:37.870 --> 00:04:41.980
on through some economic
means leading to a negotiated

00:04:41.980 --> 00:04:44.350
settlement of some kind.

00:04:44.350 --> 00:04:47.270
And diplomacy-- there's
another word for diplomacy.

00:04:47.270 --> 00:04:48.700
It's called linkage.

00:04:48.700 --> 00:04:51.220
That word is a new one that
was kind of invented here.

00:04:51.220 --> 00:04:52.850
And we're supposed
to be against it.

00:04:52.850 --> 00:04:55.960
But it's just another
word for diplomacy.

00:04:55.960 --> 00:05:01.210
Diplomacy means you
consider relevant issues,

00:05:01.210 --> 00:05:04.590
and you try to reach
some solution to them.

00:05:04.590 --> 00:05:06.340
But since we're supposed
to be against it,

00:05:06.340 --> 00:05:08.950
a new word was necessary, and
that's linkage, which is bad.

00:05:08.950 --> 00:05:12.550
And therefore, everybody's
against linkage.

00:05:12.550 --> 00:05:15.110
So the first response is
sanctions and diplomacy

00:05:15.110 --> 00:05:17.260
equals linkage.

00:05:17.260 --> 00:05:19.600
And that had pretty
broad support.

00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:21.490
And what was surprising
about it is not

00:05:21.490 --> 00:05:23.620
that it took place,
because there's often

00:05:23.620 --> 00:05:28.570
broad opposition to
aggression, but that it could

00:05:28.570 --> 00:05:31.550
get implemented, that
sanctions could be implemented,

00:05:31.550 --> 00:05:33.980
which is rare, and also
that they were so severe,

00:05:33.980 --> 00:05:35.590
which is also very rare.

00:05:35.590 --> 00:05:39.410
The second response, preparation
for war, that was very limited.

00:05:39.410 --> 00:05:42.850
In fact, it was limited to
the United States and England,

00:05:42.850 --> 00:05:45.610
with very weak
support from others.

00:05:45.610 --> 00:05:49.250
Also, the family dictatorships
that run the Gulf states.

00:05:49.250 --> 00:05:50.230
They're in favor of it.

00:05:50.230 --> 00:05:52.840
And Israel's in favor of it.

00:05:52.840 --> 00:05:56.980
But it's basically a two-member
partnership, US and England.

00:05:56.980 --> 00:05:59.830
And if you look at the
military structure,

00:05:59.830 --> 00:06:03.850
you'll notice that two military
forces are under a command

00:06:03.850 --> 00:06:04.780
structure to fight.

00:06:04.780 --> 00:06:06.488
And the others are
kind of hanging around

00:06:06.488 --> 00:06:09.077
the periphery to the
extent that they're at all.

00:06:09.077 --> 00:06:10.660
The other major
countries in the world

00:06:10.660 --> 00:06:12.670
didn't want anything
to do with it.

00:06:12.670 --> 00:06:15.730
The European community
and Japan, the other two

00:06:15.730 --> 00:06:17.920
major power centers--

00:06:17.920 --> 00:06:20.300
European community
outside of England--

00:06:20.300 --> 00:06:22.060
made it pretty clear
at once that this

00:06:22.060 --> 00:06:23.270
was none of their business.

00:06:23.270 --> 00:06:26.590
They were not going to
support it even financially.

00:06:26.590 --> 00:06:29.620
In the third world,
it's very split.

00:06:29.620 --> 00:06:33.640
Much of it is, in fact,
against any of the responses.

00:06:33.640 --> 00:06:37.360
In the Arab world,
there's a sharp division.

00:06:37.360 --> 00:06:40.290
It's been noted, and it's
worth paying attention to,

00:06:40.290 --> 00:06:42.910
that the division
in the Arab world

00:06:42.910 --> 00:06:46.930
pretty much has to do with the
extent to which a country has

00:06:46.930 --> 00:06:50.890
some degree of responsiveness
and openness to public opinion.

00:06:50.890 --> 00:06:55.270
By and large, the more a country
has some form of pluralism

00:06:55.270 --> 00:06:58.240
and elements of
democracy, it tends

00:06:58.240 --> 00:07:01.060
to be opposed to the US
presence in the Gulf.

00:07:01.060 --> 00:07:04.030
The countries that are
more in support of it

00:07:04.030 --> 00:07:07.780
are the ones that are
more repressive, narrower,

00:07:07.780 --> 00:07:10.390
have no openings.

00:07:10.390 --> 00:07:12.820
That's pretty much the case.

00:07:12.820 --> 00:07:14.080
And it's been noticed.

00:07:14.080 --> 00:07:18.790
The New York Times specialist on
the Middle East, Judith Miller,

00:07:18.790 --> 00:07:21.880
pointed out that
the countries that

00:07:21.880 --> 00:07:24.820
have what she called "nascent
democratic movements"

00:07:24.820 --> 00:07:26.770
are opposed to the US presence.

00:07:26.770 --> 00:07:29.680
And administration
spokesmen have also

00:07:29.680 --> 00:07:31.920
noticed it and have
spoken about it

00:07:31.920 --> 00:07:34.180
and have pointed out that
their conclusion from that

00:07:34.180 --> 00:07:40.360
is that we have to strike fast
because especially if you let

00:07:40.360 --> 00:07:44.140
things run into the
Islamic religious periods

00:07:44.140 --> 00:07:48.250
around March, the Ramadan
and the pilgrimage,

00:07:48.250 --> 00:07:51.010
the Hajj, then even
the totalitarian states

00:07:51.010 --> 00:07:54.654
have a tough time keeping
a lid on public opinion.

00:07:54.654 --> 00:07:56.570
And therefore, the whole
thing might collapse.

00:07:56.570 --> 00:07:58.540
So the conclusion
is strike fast.

00:07:58.540 --> 00:08:01.090
And incidentally,
the same conclusions

00:08:01.090 --> 00:08:02.740
are drawn about the home front.

00:08:02.740 --> 00:08:05.530
Unless you strike fast,
there will be an expression

00:08:05.530 --> 00:08:06.830
of public opinion.

00:08:06.830 --> 00:08:09.010
Well, you can draw
your own conclusions

00:08:09.010 --> 00:08:15.970
from that, both the lessons
that it teaches about statecraft

00:08:15.970 --> 00:08:18.290
and the prospects for action.

00:08:18.290 --> 00:08:20.260
Anyhow, there were
those divisions.

00:08:20.260 --> 00:08:21.970
George Bush likes
to talk about how

00:08:21.970 --> 00:08:24.640
it's the world against Saddam
Hussein, which is something

00:08:24.640 --> 00:08:25.530
of an exaggeration.

00:08:25.530 --> 00:08:27.380
It certainly is the
industrial world

00:08:27.380 --> 00:08:29.920
and parts of the rest
against Saddam Hussein.

00:08:29.920 --> 00:08:33.580
But it's also, in this respect,
the world against George

00:08:33.580 --> 00:08:38.740
Bush or, to be more precise,
against George Bush and--

00:08:38.740 --> 00:08:42.250
whoever happens to be sitting in
10 Downing Street at the moment

00:08:42.250 --> 00:08:43.600
varies.

00:08:43.600 --> 00:08:47.020
But it's the
US-British coalition

00:08:47.020 --> 00:08:50.890
that's pretty much breaking
with world opinion on that

00:08:50.890 --> 00:08:52.850
and has been from the beginning.

00:08:52.850 --> 00:08:54.760
Now, that raises a
number of questions.

00:08:54.760 --> 00:08:58.300
We want to understand why the
United Nations was able to act,

00:08:58.300 --> 00:09:00.520
which is very unusual;
why the actions were

00:09:00.520 --> 00:09:03.415
so unprecedented in
severity as they were--

00:09:03.415 --> 00:09:05.770
I can't think of
another case where

00:09:05.770 --> 00:09:08.890
there was an embargo on
food, for example, even

00:09:08.890 --> 00:09:11.780
in much worse cases than this.

00:09:11.780 --> 00:09:13.330
And, of course, the
crucial question

00:09:13.330 --> 00:09:15.760
is why the United States
and England are alone--

00:09:15.760 --> 00:09:18.700
aside from the family
dictatorships in the Gulf,

00:09:18.700 --> 00:09:20.560
pretty much alone.

00:09:20.560 --> 00:09:23.800
Well, let's hold those questions
in the back of our minds

00:09:23.800 --> 00:09:29.180
for a second and be clear about
what in fact happened next.

00:09:29.180 --> 00:09:32.740
What happened next is that
within days of the invasion,

00:09:32.740 --> 00:09:35.290
well before the annexation,
the United States

00:09:35.290 --> 00:09:39.280
moved decisively to undercut
the possibility that sanctions

00:09:39.280 --> 00:09:40.630
might be effective.

00:09:40.630 --> 00:09:42.910
That was done instantly.

00:09:42.910 --> 00:09:45.070
The immediate response
from Washington

00:09:45.070 --> 00:09:50.050
was to announce the sending
of a huge expeditionary force.

00:09:50.050 --> 00:09:55.540
Now, a deterrent force,
largely offshore,

00:09:55.540 --> 00:09:58.660
that could be kept in
place for a long time,

00:09:58.660 --> 00:10:00.670
long enough for
sanctions to take effect.

00:10:00.670 --> 00:10:04.050
And these sanctions are
very likely to take effect.

00:10:04.050 --> 00:10:05.810
It's two major reasons for that.

00:10:05.810 --> 00:10:08.650
One is their
unprecedented severity.

00:10:08.650 --> 00:10:11.740
And the other is that the
usual sanctions busters--

00:10:11.740 --> 00:10:14.260
namely the United States and
England and their allies--

00:10:14.260 --> 00:10:17.650
happen to be onboard this
time, and vigorously onboard.

00:10:17.650 --> 00:10:19.720
So it's not like South Africa.

00:10:19.720 --> 00:10:23.020
So this time, it's very
likely that these sanctions

00:10:23.020 --> 00:10:24.550
would work.

00:10:24.550 --> 00:10:27.490
But of course, it takes
time for sanctions to work.

00:10:27.490 --> 00:10:30.460
And a huge expeditionary
force can't

00:10:30.460 --> 00:10:33.190
be kept there more than a
couple of weeks, or maybe months

00:10:33.190 --> 00:10:34.666
at the outside.

00:10:34.666 --> 00:10:36.040
Furthermore,
there's this problem

00:10:36.040 --> 00:10:38.590
that I mentioned before-- you
can't keep the troops too long

00:10:38.590 --> 00:10:41.320
or the Arab world blows up
because nobody likes it.

00:10:41.320 --> 00:10:43.600
So you've got to do it fast.

00:10:43.600 --> 00:10:46.390
That wouldn't be a problem
with a deterrent force.

00:10:46.390 --> 00:10:49.690
So the purpose--
certainly the effect,

00:10:49.690 --> 00:10:52.660
and the predictable effect,
so I assume the purpose--

00:10:52.660 --> 00:10:54.550
of announcing right
off that there

00:10:54.550 --> 00:11:00.450
would be a huge expeditionary
force, that must have been--

00:11:00.450 --> 00:11:02.200
the effect obviously
was and the purpose

00:11:02.200 --> 00:11:04.510
must have been-- to undercut
sanctions, to make sure

00:11:04.510 --> 00:11:06.130
that they can't work.

00:11:06.130 --> 00:11:07.630
And within the first
couple of days,

00:11:07.630 --> 00:11:10.120
it was clear that sanctions
were not an option.

00:11:10.120 --> 00:11:13.030
Well, what about
diplomacy, the other aspect

00:11:13.030 --> 00:11:17.230
of the peaceful means prescribed
by international law, sanctions

00:11:17.230 --> 00:11:19.495
and diplomacy?

00:11:19.495 --> 00:11:24.340
Diplomatic opportunities
began to crop up very quickly,

00:11:24.340 --> 00:11:27.010
within a week or two.

00:11:27.010 --> 00:11:30.340
By the end of August,
there were several

00:11:30.340 --> 00:11:33.550
that looked pretty significant,
including Iraqi offers that

00:11:33.550 --> 00:11:35.950
were regarded by
high US officials

00:11:35.950 --> 00:11:38.330
as serious and negotiable.

00:11:38.330 --> 00:11:40.030
The offers at that
time basically

00:11:40.030 --> 00:11:42.640
had to do with, as you've
all heard plenty of times

00:11:42.640 --> 00:11:46.150
by now, two uninhabited
islands in the Gulf

00:11:46.150 --> 00:11:49.350
and with this particular oil
field, the Rumaila oil field.

00:11:49.350 --> 00:11:52.045
This oil field's
about 98% inside Iraq.

00:11:52.045 --> 00:11:54.370
It crosses a disputed border.

00:11:54.370 --> 00:11:56.710
And Iraq wanted control
of the whole thing.

00:11:56.710 --> 00:11:59.080
The uninhabited
islands, they have

00:11:59.080 --> 00:12:00.880
to do with access to the Gulf.

00:12:00.880 --> 00:12:03.130
The imperial settlement
imposed by England

00:12:03.130 --> 00:12:06.710
was set up in such a way that
Iraq is basically landlocked.

00:12:06.710 --> 00:12:10.330
And access to the Gulf
would involve control

00:12:10.330 --> 00:12:11.440
over those two islands.

00:12:11.440 --> 00:12:13.570
There could be lots of
ways of working this out--

00:12:13.570 --> 00:12:15.414
leasing or whatever.

00:12:15.414 --> 00:12:17.080
Much harder problems
than that have been

00:12:17.080 --> 00:12:19.150
settled through negotiations.

00:12:19.150 --> 00:12:22.690
And a settlement along
those lines looked feasible.

00:12:22.690 --> 00:12:23.946
Was it feasible?

00:12:23.946 --> 00:12:26.320
Well, we don't know because
the United States rejected it

00:12:26.320 --> 00:12:28.180
flat out, out of hand.

00:12:28.180 --> 00:12:31.090
The only way you can find
out if a proposal is serious

00:12:31.090 --> 00:12:32.920
is to pursue it, obviously.

00:12:32.920 --> 00:12:37.240
And rejecting it out of hand
was a way of ensuring that there

00:12:37.240 --> 00:12:38.440
wouldn't be serious--

00:12:38.440 --> 00:12:40.420
in fact, it was probably
an expression of fear

00:12:40.420 --> 00:12:43.000
that it might be serious.

00:12:43.000 --> 00:12:45.820
The New York Times had a
pretty remarkable story.

00:12:45.820 --> 00:12:48.640
Thomas Friedman, the
diplomatic correspondent,

00:12:48.640 --> 00:12:51.670
which means, essentially,
the State Department

00:12:51.670 --> 00:12:53.710
spokesman at the Times.

00:12:53.710 --> 00:12:55.930
That's the nature
of that office.

00:12:55.930 --> 00:12:58.840
He had quite a perceptive
article, I thought--

00:12:58.840 --> 00:13:01.330
I think it was
around August 22--

00:13:01.330 --> 00:13:05.140
in which he said that
the administration wants

00:13:05.140 --> 00:13:11.430
to prevent the diplomatic track
from being pursued because

00:13:11.430 --> 00:13:14.200
of fear that pursuit
of the diplomatic track

00:13:14.200 --> 00:13:17.710
might defuse the crisis--

00:13:17.710 --> 00:13:21.190
that was his words--

00:13:21.190 --> 00:13:27.839
by offering a few token
gains for Saddam Hussein,

00:13:27.839 --> 00:13:29.380
namely-- and then
he mentioned them--

00:13:29.380 --> 00:13:33.510
a couple of uninhabited islands
and some border rectification.

00:13:33.510 --> 00:13:35.730
And you don't want to
defuse the crisis when

00:13:35.730 --> 00:13:38.470
you can have a neat war with a
couple hundred thousand people

00:13:38.470 --> 00:13:39.640
killed, apparently.

00:13:39.640 --> 00:13:44.470
So the Times actually had
this offer leaked to it

00:13:44.470 --> 00:13:46.070
and suppressed it.

00:13:46.070 --> 00:13:48.950
And it was only released,
and then marginally,

00:13:48.950 --> 00:13:54.320
when it was then leaked to
a suburban newspaper in New

00:13:54.320 --> 00:13:56.030
York, Newsday, which
published it very

00:13:56.030 --> 00:13:57.470
prominently on the front page.

00:13:57.470 --> 00:13:59.690
It was all over the
newsstands in New York,

00:13:59.690 --> 00:14:02.150
and you had to at least have
dismissive mention of it

00:14:02.150 --> 00:14:04.400
in the back pages the next day.

00:14:04.400 --> 00:14:07.370
Well, since that time, there
have been repeated offers.

00:14:07.370 --> 00:14:09.860
The most recent one
that's been made public,

00:14:09.860 --> 00:14:15.860
at least, was disclosed on
January 2, again in Newsday.

00:14:15.860 --> 00:14:17.720
The reporter-- it's
a good reporter

00:14:17.720 --> 00:14:20.150
at Newsday, Newt Royce,
Washington bureau,

00:14:20.150 --> 00:14:23.330
who's been following
this topic very closely.

00:14:23.330 --> 00:14:27.230
On January 2, he reported
that US officials

00:14:27.230 --> 00:14:31.010
had disclosed that there
had been an Iraqi offer

00:14:31.010 --> 00:14:32.900
a couple of days earlier.

00:14:32.900 --> 00:14:36.920
And that offer was to withdraw
completely from Kuwait

00:14:36.920 --> 00:14:42.290
in return for a pledge not to
attack the withdrawing forces

00:14:42.290 --> 00:14:47.300
and to reach arrangements
or settlement--

00:14:47.300 --> 00:14:51.950
or something kind of vague, to
do something about two issues,

00:14:51.950 --> 00:14:53.970
two major regional issues--

00:14:53.970 --> 00:14:57.200
one of them the issue of
the Arab-Israeli conflict,

00:14:57.200 --> 00:14:59.360
particularly the state
of the Palestinians.

00:14:59.360 --> 00:15:04.040
And the second issue was
weapons of mass destruction

00:15:04.040 --> 00:15:05.430
in the region.

00:15:05.430 --> 00:15:09.560
So in return for some kind of
Security Council commitment

00:15:09.560 --> 00:15:12.020
to deal with those
two issues, there

00:15:12.020 --> 00:15:13.670
could be complete withdrawal.

00:15:13.670 --> 00:15:15.260
Well, was that offer serious?

00:15:15.260 --> 00:15:20.150
We don't know because the United
States dismissed it instantly.

00:15:20.150 --> 00:15:23.590
Again, high State
Department officials

00:15:23.590 --> 00:15:26.690
described it as a serious
pre-negotiating position

00:15:26.690 --> 00:15:31.060
as an indication that Iraq
wants to withdraw from Kuwait

00:15:31.060 --> 00:15:34.160
and noted specifically
that it didn't say anything

00:15:34.160 --> 00:15:35.830
about the border issues.

00:15:35.830 --> 00:15:37.310
It dropped them.

00:15:37.310 --> 00:15:41.000
Well, that one didn't
get much publicity.

00:15:41.000 --> 00:15:43.340
The New York Times, however,
which didn't report,

00:15:43.340 --> 00:15:47.660
did report the same day a
meeting between Yasser Arafat

00:15:47.660 --> 00:15:50.300
and Saddam Hussein
in which Arafat said

00:15:50.300 --> 00:15:53.000
that they had agreed that--

00:15:53.000 --> 00:15:56.600
Saddam Hussein had made a
proposal back on August 12

00:15:56.600 --> 00:15:59.530
to withdraw, or
at least to link--

00:15:59.530 --> 00:16:02.960
all these are kind of vague--
to link Iraqi withdrawal

00:16:02.960 --> 00:16:05.960
to withdrawal from other
occupied Arab territories.

00:16:05.960 --> 00:16:09.110
That means Syria and Israel
from Lebanon and Syria

00:16:09.110 --> 00:16:13.700
and Israel from the Syrian
Golan Heights and the West Bank

00:16:13.700 --> 00:16:15.020
and Gaza Strip.

00:16:15.020 --> 00:16:19.790
And according to the January 2
report, January 3 in the Times,

00:16:19.790 --> 00:16:23.810
this was now weakened
to just a commitment

00:16:23.810 --> 00:16:26.150
to deal with those issues,
so withdrawal in response

00:16:26.150 --> 00:16:28.430
to a commitment to
deal with those issues.

00:16:28.430 --> 00:16:30.190
Well, that was also rejected.

00:16:30.190 --> 00:16:32.210
Anyhow, two weeks
before the deadline,

00:16:32.210 --> 00:16:34.340
things looked like this.

00:16:34.340 --> 00:16:36.470
The contours of a
possible settlement

00:16:36.470 --> 00:16:42.980
were Iraqi withdrawal from
Kuwait in return for a pledge

00:16:42.980 --> 00:16:46.850
not to attack the withdrawing
forces and a commitment

00:16:46.850 --> 00:16:48.770
to settle regional issues.

00:16:48.770 --> 00:16:51.830
No settlement of them, just
a commitment to settle them.

00:16:51.830 --> 00:16:53.010
That was rejected.

00:16:53.010 --> 00:16:56.103
So we don't know if
that was serious.

00:16:56.103 --> 00:16:59.300
And all of that is indicative
of a strong opposition

00:16:59.300 --> 00:17:02.330
to diplomacy, very strong
opposition to diplomacy.

00:17:02.330 --> 00:17:04.760
These are certainly
well within the range

00:17:04.760 --> 00:17:07.040
of negotiable positions.

00:17:07.040 --> 00:17:09.560
But the United States
didn't want to hear of them.

00:17:09.560 --> 00:17:11.480
And they were flat out rejected.

00:17:11.480 --> 00:17:14.060
And therefore, we haven't any
idea, and we'll never know,

00:17:14.060 --> 00:17:20.960
whether that offered a way of
avoiding a major catastrophe.

00:17:20.960 --> 00:17:26.240
That has continued up until
just a couple of hours ago.

00:17:26.240 --> 00:17:29.480
Last night, France
made a proposal

00:17:29.480 --> 00:17:34.370
at the Security
Council for Iraq,

00:17:34.370 --> 00:17:37.610
calling for a complete
and rapid Iraqi withdrawal

00:17:37.610 --> 00:17:41.300
from Kuwait in return for--

00:17:41.300 --> 00:17:44.030
and then comes
wording, vague wording,

00:17:44.030 --> 00:17:47.360
about calling an
international conference

00:17:47.360 --> 00:17:50.170
at an appropriate moment.

00:17:50.170 --> 00:17:53.930
Not even saying when, but some
time or other, a commitment

00:17:53.930 --> 00:17:55.640
to call an
international conference

00:17:55.640 --> 00:18:00.470
at an appropriate moment to
consider the Palestinian issue

00:18:00.470 --> 00:18:01.880
in the Arab-Israeli conflict.

00:18:01.880 --> 00:18:03.320
That was the proposal.

00:18:03.320 --> 00:18:07.010
That was flat out rejected by
the United States, instantly.

00:18:07.010 --> 00:18:09.440
The United States and
Britain rejected it outright.

00:18:09.440 --> 00:18:12.280
Also the Soviet Union, but at
this point Soviet leadership

00:18:12.280 --> 00:18:14.660
would stand on their heads
and sing Yankee Doodle

00:18:14.660 --> 00:18:16.940
if they thought they could
get American support.

00:18:16.940 --> 00:18:18.740
So that's irrelevant.

00:18:18.740 --> 00:18:22.610
But England and the United
States instantaneously

00:18:22.610 --> 00:18:26.150
rejected it on the
usual grounds--

00:18:26.150 --> 00:18:29.840
linkage, meaning diplomacy, and
you can't reward aggressors,

00:18:29.840 --> 00:18:31.350
and all this posturing.

00:18:31.350 --> 00:18:34.050
Now, actually, what
the UN ambassador,

00:18:34.050 --> 00:18:37.730
the American UN ambassador
said, Thomas Pickering, he said,

00:18:37.730 --> 00:18:42.350
you can't accept this because
it doesn't keep to the UN

00:18:42.350 --> 00:18:45.650
resolutions on Iraq and Kuwait.

00:18:45.650 --> 00:18:48.230
And he's technically
correct about that.

00:18:48.230 --> 00:18:50.420
The wording is not drawn
from those resolutions.

00:18:50.420 --> 00:18:54.770
It's drawn from a different
UN Security Council decision.

00:18:54.770 --> 00:19:01.670
Namely, it's drawn from a
Security Council decision

00:19:01.670 --> 00:19:05.090
on December 20 of last year,
which had absolutely nothing

00:19:05.090 --> 00:19:05.990
to do with Iraq.

00:19:05.990 --> 00:19:08.870
It had to do with protection
of the rights of Palestinians

00:19:08.870 --> 00:19:10.190
under Israeli occupation.

00:19:10.190 --> 00:19:13.220
It was a resolution--

00:19:13.220 --> 00:19:18.320
I think 681-- which called on
Israel to observe the Geneva

00:19:18.320 --> 00:19:20.990
Conventions and to
stop deporting people

00:19:20.990 --> 00:19:25.003
and to protect the rights of
Palestinians under occupation.

00:19:25.003 --> 00:19:27.560
Now, that resolution
was passed unanimously.

00:19:27.560 --> 00:19:30.020
The United States had
voted for it, too.

00:19:30.020 --> 00:19:32.720
And then came something
kind of unusual.

00:19:32.720 --> 00:19:37.040
Attached to that
resolution was a codicil,

00:19:37.040 --> 00:19:39.740
a statement attached to it--

00:19:39.740 --> 00:19:42.440
not in the resolution, but
attached to it-- which said,

00:19:42.440 --> 00:19:46.160
members of the Security Council
agree that in the interest

00:19:46.160 --> 00:19:51.140
of peace, they will proceed
at an appropriate moment--

00:19:51.140 --> 00:19:52.340
that's the crucial wording.

00:19:52.340 --> 00:19:54.440
They will proceed at
an appropriate moment

00:19:54.440 --> 00:19:57.860
to call for an
international conference

00:19:57.860 --> 00:20:00.640
to deal with the issue
in order to reach peace.

00:20:00.640 --> 00:20:02.697
Now, why wasn't that
in the resolution?

00:20:02.697 --> 00:20:05.030
Well, because if that phrase
had been in the resolution,

00:20:05.030 --> 00:20:07.310
the United States would
have vetoed it, right?

00:20:07.310 --> 00:20:09.880
Remember that this had
nothing to do with Iraq.

00:20:09.880 --> 00:20:11.922
The word Iraq or
Kuwait never got

00:20:11.922 --> 00:20:13.130
mentioned in this resolution.

00:20:13.130 --> 00:20:15.320
It just had to do
with the Palestinians.

00:20:15.320 --> 00:20:17.700
But the United States
was going to veto it

00:20:17.700 --> 00:20:20.030
if it said that at
some appropriate moment

00:20:20.030 --> 00:20:22.010
in the future, there would be--

00:20:22.010 --> 00:20:25.910
unspecified when, some
moment in the future, when

00:20:25.910 --> 00:20:28.010
it's appropriate,
whatever that means--

00:20:28.010 --> 00:20:31.169
there might be an international
conference on the Palestinians.

00:20:31.169 --> 00:20:33.710
If that was the case, the United
States was going to veto it.

00:20:33.710 --> 00:20:35.600
Nothing there about linkage.

00:20:35.600 --> 00:20:40.160
And the French offer
simply drew that wording

00:20:40.160 --> 00:20:43.100
and tried to work it into
a resolution on Iraq.

00:20:43.100 --> 00:20:45.074
So Pickering, the
ambassador, was

00:20:45.074 --> 00:20:46.490
right in saying
the wording wasn't

00:20:46.490 --> 00:20:48.980
drawn from Iraq resolutions.

00:20:48.980 --> 00:20:50.830
But it was drawn from
another resolution,

00:20:50.830 --> 00:20:54.920
or an addition to a resolution,
just a couple of weeks earlier.

00:20:54.920 --> 00:21:00.770
Well, that indicates the depth
of the commitment of the United

00:21:00.770 --> 00:21:04.560
States not to allow
diplomacy to work.

00:21:04.560 --> 00:21:08.390
You can't allow it to work,
even in any fashion whatsoever.

00:21:08.390 --> 00:21:12.600
Even the weakest gesture toward
diplomacy has to be blocked.

00:21:12.600 --> 00:21:16.100
The US from the start insisted
that diplomatic contacts

00:21:16.100 --> 00:21:19.700
be limited to delivery
of an ultimatum.

00:21:19.700 --> 00:21:22.040
You're allowed to deliver
them an ultimatum saying,

00:21:22.040 --> 00:21:23.450
capitulate or die.

00:21:23.450 --> 00:21:24.980
That's diplomacy.

00:21:24.980 --> 00:21:26.780
And that's what
George Bush calls

00:21:26.780 --> 00:21:29.480
going the extra mile for peace.

00:21:29.480 --> 00:21:32.870
And most of the press,
including even people who

00:21:32.870 --> 00:21:35.540
ought to know better, like
Fred Kaplan of the Globe,

00:21:35.540 --> 00:21:38.090
who's a good reporter,
have described

00:21:38.090 --> 00:21:41.840
this as a tremendous,
forthcoming effort

00:21:41.840 --> 00:21:44.270
to explore every
possible diplomatic path

00:21:44.270 --> 00:21:45.620
and so on and so forth.

00:21:45.620 --> 00:21:48.350
Fact is, it was flat
rejection of diplomacy

00:21:48.350 --> 00:21:51.160
from the first instant.

00:21:51.160 --> 00:21:56.030
Now, looking back, we have
the following situation--

00:21:56.030 --> 00:21:59.120
there were
unprecedented sanctions.

00:21:59.120 --> 00:22:00.770
There were diplomatic options.

00:22:00.770 --> 00:22:03.770
The United States moved at
once to undercut the sanctions

00:22:03.770 --> 00:22:06.170
and moved at once to
undercut the diplomacy

00:22:06.170 --> 00:22:08.690
and has kept to that
position ever since.

00:22:08.690 --> 00:22:10.580
Well, what are the
reasons for this?

00:22:10.580 --> 00:22:12.440
You've heard them 10,000 times.

00:22:12.440 --> 00:22:14.180
We can't reward aggressors.

00:22:14.180 --> 00:22:16.710
You can't have linkage.

00:22:16.710 --> 00:22:17.550
And so on.

00:22:17.550 --> 00:22:19.940
It just offends our
moral sensibilities

00:22:19.940 --> 00:22:22.310
to allow our aggressor
to get anything.

00:22:22.310 --> 00:22:27.080
So therefore, we got to
smash them in the face.

00:22:27.080 --> 00:22:29.000
Now, it really doesn't--

00:22:29.000 --> 00:22:32.220
we don't have to waste
any time discussing this.

00:22:32.220 --> 00:22:35.300
The only reason for even
spending three seconds on it

00:22:35.300 --> 00:22:38.450
is that this has been
accepted at face value

00:22:38.450 --> 00:22:40.222
virtually across the spectrum.

00:22:40.222 --> 00:22:42.680
So, for example, if you listen
to the congressional debate,

00:22:42.680 --> 00:22:44.210
you'll notice that--

00:22:44.210 --> 00:22:45.860
I mean, I didn't
hear the whole thing.

00:22:45.860 --> 00:22:49.740
But just about everybody, maybe
everybody-- every word I heard,

00:22:49.740 --> 00:22:52.290
at least-- accepted
the legitimacy of this.

00:22:52.290 --> 00:22:54.300
And most of the
press has accepted

00:22:54.300 --> 00:22:55.710
the legitimacy of this.

00:22:55.710 --> 00:22:57.210
There's been very
little questioning

00:22:57.210 --> 00:22:59.060
of the rhetorical stance.

00:22:59.060 --> 00:23:02.790
There's debate, but the
debate is over tactics.

00:23:02.790 --> 00:23:04.710
Is it good for us
or is it bad for us

00:23:04.710 --> 00:23:07.380
to pursue this just course?

00:23:07.380 --> 00:23:12.360
Now, because the rhetorical
stance was never challenged,

00:23:12.360 --> 00:23:17.010
the debate was pretty much
restricted to a tactical frame.

00:23:17.010 --> 00:23:19.160
Is it good for us or is
it bad for us to proceed?

00:23:19.160 --> 00:23:20.784
And in that frame,
the administration's

00:23:20.784 --> 00:23:23.080
going to win, obviously.

00:23:23.080 --> 00:23:24.300
I mean, it's debatable.

00:23:24.300 --> 00:23:27.180
And so you go along with power.

00:23:27.180 --> 00:23:28.704
Of course, if the
rhetorical stance

00:23:28.704 --> 00:23:30.120
had ever been
challenged, it would

00:23:30.120 --> 00:23:31.672
have collapsed instantaneously.

00:23:31.672 --> 00:23:33.630
And that would have been
the end of the debate.

00:23:33.630 --> 00:23:35.520
Because it's
impossible to uphold

00:23:35.520 --> 00:23:41.490
even one fragment of this
position for three seconds.

00:23:41.490 --> 00:23:43.420
And the reason for
that is very simple.

00:23:43.420 --> 00:23:46.380
In fact, the reason is
one of logic, basically.

00:23:46.380 --> 00:23:49.530
The point of logic is that
you can't uphold principles

00:23:49.530 --> 00:23:50.250
selectively.

00:23:50.250 --> 00:23:52.560
If you uphold some
doctrine selectively,

00:23:52.560 --> 00:23:54.300
it's not a principle.

00:23:54.300 --> 00:23:55.890
That's transparent.

00:23:55.890 --> 00:23:57.300
We don't ever doubt that.

00:23:57.300 --> 00:24:02.430
So, for example, when
Saddam Hussein tells us that

00:24:02.430 --> 00:24:07.070
he's agonizing over the human
rights abuses of the Israelis

00:24:07.070 --> 00:24:10.710
in the occupied territories
and he clutches to his breast

00:24:10.710 --> 00:24:13.720
the Amnesty International
report on Israeli atrocities--

00:24:13.720 --> 00:24:16.350
which is pretty
awful, incidentally--

00:24:16.350 --> 00:24:18.990
or when he tells us he's
against annexation-- namely,

00:24:18.990 --> 00:24:21.420
he's against annexation
of the Golan Heights--

00:24:21.420 --> 00:24:23.340
we don't say he's
a man of principle.

00:24:23.340 --> 00:24:24.780
You laugh.

00:24:24.780 --> 00:24:27.780
And exactly the same
reasoning holds true

00:24:27.780 --> 00:24:31.500
when George Bush tells us
that he clutches to his breast

00:24:31.500 --> 00:24:34.800
the Amnesty International report
on Iraqi atrocities in Kuwait,

00:24:34.800 --> 00:24:36.360
but not a long
list of others you

00:24:36.360 --> 00:24:38.620
might think of and that his--

00:24:38.620 --> 00:24:39.944
[APPLAUSE]

00:24:41.760 --> 00:24:44.070
--cant tolerate,
can't go to sleep

00:24:44.070 --> 00:24:46.890
at night because of this
thought that an aggressor's not

00:24:46.890 --> 00:24:49.150
being punished and
so on and so forth.

00:24:49.150 --> 00:24:51.020
I mean, these are jokes.

00:24:51.020 --> 00:24:53.416
Incidentally, in the third
world, my impression--

00:24:53.416 --> 00:24:55.540
we don't get much information
from the third world.

00:24:55.540 --> 00:24:57.800
But my impression is that
they were regarded as jokes.

00:24:57.800 --> 00:25:01.770
It's kind of striking that
until the last couple of days,

00:25:01.770 --> 00:25:03.810
even, people in the
third world didn't

00:25:03.810 --> 00:25:05.670
seem to think that this
was going to happen.

00:25:05.670 --> 00:25:09.330
I was down at a conference
in Mexico last weekend.

00:25:09.330 --> 00:25:11.790
And nobody took it-- from
people around Latin America,

00:25:11.790 --> 00:25:13.170
nobody thought
this was for real.

00:25:13.170 --> 00:25:17.340
They all thought, this
is never gonna happen.

00:25:17.340 --> 00:25:20.670
People from the Middle East
have had the same reaction,

00:25:20.670 --> 00:25:22.080
from right in the region.

00:25:22.080 --> 00:25:24.600
And I think the reason
for that is pretty simple.

00:25:24.600 --> 00:25:28.230
When we hear Saddam Hussein's
posturing, we laugh.

00:25:28.230 --> 00:25:31.554
When they hear George Bush's
posturing, they laugh.

00:25:31.554 --> 00:25:33.720
What they don't realize is
that in the United States

00:25:33.720 --> 00:25:36.030
and England, people don't laugh.

00:25:36.030 --> 00:25:37.830
They take it seriously.

00:25:37.830 --> 00:25:41.160
They don't understand the
deep totalitarian streak

00:25:41.160 --> 00:25:43.620
in Western culture,
which means that you

00:25:43.620 --> 00:25:45.300
can get by with this
kind of posturing

00:25:45.300 --> 00:25:48.069
and everybody takes it very
seriously and somberly.

00:25:48.069 --> 00:25:50.610
And that's the kind of mistake
that people in the third world

00:25:50.610 --> 00:25:54.490
have been making for a long
time, in fact for 500 years,

00:25:54.490 --> 00:25:58.070
ever since the European
conquest of the world began.

00:25:58.070 --> 00:26:00.180
And they've paid
quite a cost for it,

00:26:00.180 --> 00:26:03.540
and they're continuing
to pay a price.

00:26:03.540 --> 00:26:06.870
Well, now it's clear
that it's for real.

00:26:11.490 --> 00:26:13.870
It's instantaneous-- I'm not
gonna waste your time on it.

00:26:13.870 --> 00:26:16.470
But the United States is
one of the leading violators

00:26:16.470 --> 00:26:19.290
of all the principles that
are grandly proclaimed.

00:26:19.290 --> 00:26:20.910
That's obvious.

00:26:20.910 --> 00:26:24.480
Anybody who's opened
their eyes in the last 50

00:26:24.480 --> 00:26:26.910
years or 100 years knows that.

00:26:26.910 --> 00:26:28.889
In the last year, in fact.

00:26:28.889 --> 00:26:30.930
And therefore, we don't
have to argue any further

00:26:30.930 --> 00:26:31.860
about the stance.

00:26:31.860 --> 00:26:34.710
What about the matter
of linkage or sanctions?

00:26:34.710 --> 00:26:37.050
Well, supposedly, we
can't be for sanctions

00:26:37.050 --> 00:26:38.280
because it'll take too long.

00:26:38.280 --> 00:26:40.110
Why will it take too long?

00:26:40.110 --> 00:26:46.380
Well, there's the argument
given by the administration

00:26:46.380 --> 00:26:50.070
and by people in
Congress is that--

00:26:50.070 --> 00:26:53.250
one argument is, well, if you
wait for sanctions to hold,

00:26:53.250 --> 00:26:54.956
the coalition will fall apart.

00:26:54.956 --> 00:26:56.442
Yeah, that's interesting.

00:26:56.442 --> 00:26:57.900
That's possibly
true, incidentally.

00:26:57.900 --> 00:27:00.420
But that tells you something
about the kind of support

00:27:00.420 --> 00:27:03.270
that there is behind
the US position.

00:27:03.270 --> 00:27:11.940
The second argument
is that sanctions

00:27:11.940 --> 00:27:15.330
will reward the aggressor.

00:27:15.330 --> 00:27:17.460
It just hurts our
moral sensibilities

00:27:17.460 --> 00:27:21.210
to sit by while we
watch the aggressor

00:27:21.210 --> 00:27:22.810
benefit from what he's done.

00:27:22.810 --> 00:27:27.270
We've got to move fast to stop
our sensitive souls for being

00:27:27.270 --> 00:27:28.770
seared by this sight.

00:27:28.770 --> 00:27:31.590
Again, that's not one you can
take very seriously, either.

00:27:31.590 --> 00:27:34.690
There are other cases where
there have been sanctions--

00:27:34.690 --> 00:27:37.620
very porous sanctions because,
as I say, the United States

00:27:37.620 --> 00:27:41.130
and England and France and
others regularly violate them.

00:27:41.130 --> 00:27:43.290
But take, say, South Africa.

00:27:43.290 --> 00:27:46.290
The occupation of
Namibia was declared

00:27:46.290 --> 00:27:49.600
illegal by the United
Nations and the World Court

00:27:49.600 --> 00:27:52.900
back around late '60s.

00:27:52.900 --> 00:27:55.780
For years, the United
States carried out

00:27:55.780 --> 00:28:00.040
what it called quiet diplomacy
and constructive engagement--

00:28:00.040 --> 00:28:01.120
20 years, in fact.

00:28:01.120 --> 00:28:05.860
Meanwhile, South Africa robbed
the place, terrorized it,

00:28:05.860 --> 00:28:08.620
used it as a base for
attacking its neighbors

00:28:08.620 --> 00:28:10.180
at a terrific cost.

00:28:10.180 --> 00:28:12.790
The UN Economic
Commission on Africa

00:28:12.790 --> 00:28:15.460
estimated the cost to
the neighbors alone--

00:28:15.460 --> 00:28:17.170
forget South Africa
and Namibia--

00:28:17.170 --> 00:28:19.450
in the last decade,
in the '80s, they

00:28:19.450 --> 00:28:25.310
estimated it at over $60 billion
and a million and a half lives.

00:28:25.310 --> 00:28:28.660
Well, meanwhile, our sensitive
souls weren't seared.

00:28:28.660 --> 00:28:32.280
We went along with-- nobody
proposed bombing South Africa.

00:28:32.280 --> 00:28:35.860
Nobody proposed
withholding food.

00:28:35.860 --> 00:28:40.480
The sanctions, they were
violated all over the place.

00:28:40.480 --> 00:28:41.890
We went on with diplomacy.

00:28:41.890 --> 00:28:44.320
There was finally a settlement
with plenty of reward

00:28:44.320 --> 00:28:47.140
for the aggressor,
plenty of linkage,

00:28:47.140 --> 00:28:49.450
linkage all the way up to Cuba.

00:28:49.450 --> 00:28:52.090
And everybody thought
that was great,

00:28:52.090 --> 00:28:54.310
a great achievement
of diplomacy.

00:28:54.310 --> 00:28:57.040
And the same is true in
case after case after case.

00:28:57.040 --> 00:29:01.030
When George Shultz tried to
broker Israel's withdrawal

00:29:01.030 --> 00:29:04.630
from Lebanon after a completely
unprovoked attack in which

00:29:04.630 --> 00:29:08.950
maybe 20,000
civilians were killed,

00:29:08.950 --> 00:29:11.310
that was a partial
withdrawal from Lebanon.

00:29:11.310 --> 00:29:13.630
Plenty of reward for
the aggressor and plenty

00:29:13.630 --> 00:29:14.840
of linkage.

00:29:14.840 --> 00:29:18.000
So Syria was ordered to get out.

00:29:18.000 --> 00:29:20.740
No negotiations with Syria,
just give 'em orders.

00:29:20.740 --> 00:29:22.930
That's linkage with a vengeance.

00:29:22.930 --> 00:29:27.434
Same was true after Israel's
invasion of Sinai back in 1956,

00:29:27.434 --> 00:29:29.350
which the United States
didn't like that time.

00:29:29.350 --> 00:29:30.580
So it kicked them
out, but, again,

00:29:30.580 --> 00:29:32.410
with plenty of rewards
for the aggressor.

00:29:32.410 --> 00:29:33.530
That's just standard.

00:29:33.530 --> 00:29:35.810
So this just can't
be taken seriously.

00:29:35.810 --> 00:29:38.440
I mean, serious people can't
waste a moment on this.

00:29:38.440 --> 00:29:39.100
So let's not.

00:29:41.840 --> 00:29:43.810
There's a conclusion
that follows from this,

00:29:43.810 --> 00:29:44.410
incidentally.

00:29:44.410 --> 00:29:46.930
The conclusion that follows
is that no reason has

00:29:46.930 --> 00:29:48.730
been offered for going to war.

00:29:48.730 --> 00:29:50.170
And I mean no reason.

00:29:50.170 --> 00:29:51.100
I mean none.

00:29:51.100 --> 00:29:53.142
[APPLAUSE]

00:29:57.420 --> 00:29:58.560
None whatsoever.

00:29:58.560 --> 00:30:01.340
There's been simply no argument.

00:30:01.340 --> 00:30:03.320
The reason is that
every single argument

00:30:03.320 --> 00:30:05.390
is transparently fraudulent.

00:30:05.390 --> 00:30:10.130
Not complicated
fraud, trivial fraud.

00:30:10.130 --> 00:30:11.776
And that means
whatever's going on

00:30:11.776 --> 00:30:13.400
has nothing to do
with the reasons that

00:30:13.400 --> 00:30:14.233
are being presented.

00:30:14.233 --> 00:30:16.070
Some other thing.

00:30:16.070 --> 00:30:18.720
Well, let's come back to
the original questions.

00:30:23.360 --> 00:30:26.150
Why were the
sanctions implemented?

00:30:26.150 --> 00:30:30.589
Well, one reason-- the answer to
that is also trivially simple.

00:30:30.589 --> 00:30:32.630
The sanctions were
implemented because the United

00:30:32.630 --> 00:30:35.150
States, for once, permitted it.

00:30:35.150 --> 00:30:36.860
Usually, the United
States doesn't

00:30:36.860 --> 00:30:40.040
permit the UN to carry
out its peacekeeping role.

00:30:40.040 --> 00:30:42.530
That's why it
doesn't carry it out.

00:30:42.530 --> 00:30:44.900
For the last 20 years,
the United States

00:30:44.900 --> 00:30:48.800
is far in the lead in vetoing
Security Council resolutions.

00:30:48.800 --> 00:30:51.980
In second place is the
other one of the righteous

00:30:51.980 --> 00:30:54.470
avengers in the
Gulf, Britain, mainly

00:30:54.470 --> 00:30:56.810
because of their protection
of the racist states

00:30:56.810 --> 00:30:58.430
of southern Africa.

00:30:58.430 --> 00:31:01.520
Between them, the United
States and England,

00:31:01.520 --> 00:31:03.410
which are now standing
on high principle,

00:31:03.410 --> 00:31:07.580
account for about 4/5 of
the Security Council vetoes

00:31:07.580 --> 00:31:09.530
during these last 20 years.

00:31:09.530 --> 00:31:12.530
In third place, well
behind, is France.

00:31:12.530 --> 00:31:16.400
And in fourth is
the Soviet Union.

00:31:16.400 --> 00:31:19.340
Well, we're now told
over and over again

00:31:19.340 --> 00:31:21.470
that one of the really
important things

00:31:21.470 --> 00:31:25.790
is this great new world order,
which will finally have peace,

00:31:25.790 --> 00:31:28.130
and the UN will be able to
function because the Soviet

00:31:28.130 --> 00:31:31.130
Union has lost the Cold War.

00:31:31.130 --> 00:31:33.870
And therefore, they're not
going to block the UN anymore.

00:31:33.870 --> 00:31:36.110
Well, it takes three
minutes to figure out

00:31:36.110 --> 00:31:37.520
who's been blocking the UN.

00:31:37.520 --> 00:31:41.000
I should say the same is
true in the General Assembly.

00:31:41.000 --> 00:31:42.500
And there's not the
slightest reason

00:31:42.500 --> 00:31:45.260
to suppose that with the
Soviet Union out of the game,

00:31:45.260 --> 00:31:47.810
the United States and Britain
will suddenly call off

00:31:47.810 --> 00:31:49.730
their campaign against
international law

00:31:49.730 --> 00:31:51.130
and against the United Nations.

00:31:51.130 --> 00:31:52.260
It doesn't make any sense.

00:31:52.260 --> 00:31:52.710
Why should they?

00:31:52.710 --> 00:31:54.626
If fact, they'll carry
it out more vigorously.

00:31:54.626 --> 00:31:56.600
There's less in the way.

00:31:56.600 --> 00:31:59.910
There's no prospects for a new
world order or a great new era.

00:31:59.910 --> 00:32:02.420
In fact, quite the contrary.

00:32:02.420 --> 00:32:05.330
I should say, incidentally,
that in this case, too,

00:32:05.330 --> 00:32:08.040
I think if you take
a careful look,

00:32:08.040 --> 00:32:09.795
you'll notice that
the United States also

00:32:09.795 --> 00:32:12.170
undercut the peacekeeping
function of the United Nations,

00:32:12.170 --> 00:32:14.000
but in a more complex way.

00:32:14.000 --> 00:32:17.150
Usually, it just does it
by vetoes, straight vetoes

00:32:17.150 --> 00:32:18.900
so nothing can happen.

00:32:18.900 --> 00:32:22.670
In this case, the United Nations
did move toward sanctions,

00:32:22.670 --> 00:32:26.120
and the United States, as I
said, undercut that instantly,

00:32:26.120 --> 00:32:29.090
undercut the sanctions option
and undercut the diplomacy

00:32:29.090 --> 00:32:29.840
option.

00:32:29.840 --> 00:32:33.170
That's undercutting the
peacekeeping mission of the UN.

00:32:33.170 --> 00:32:36.050
There were lots of threats
and pressure and cajolery.

00:32:36.050 --> 00:32:37.640
And finally, what
in effect happened

00:32:37.640 --> 00:32:40.310
is that the UN washed
its hands of the matter.

00:32:40.310 --> 00:32:43.280
This famous UN resolution
that was passed basically

00:32:43.280 --> 00:32:44.540
says nothing.

00:32:44.540 --> 00:32:46.400
It says, use any
necessary means.

00:32:46.400 --> 00:32:48.230
Doesn't tell you what
necessary means are.

00:32:48.230 --> 00:32:49.994
They can be diplomacy
or sanctions.

00:32:49.994 --> 00:32:52.160
Or, for those guys who want
to kill a lot of people,

00:32:52.160 --> 00:32:53.820
they can be kill
a lot of people.

00:32:53.820 --> 00:32:57.200
But that's a decision for the
individual states to take.

00:32:57.200 --> 00:32:59.150
There's no UN
resolution that uses

00:32:59.150 --> 00:33:01.160
any phrase like
"minimal use of force"

00:33:01.160 --> 00:33:02.480
or anything of that kind.

00:33:02.480 --> 00:33:04.490
The UN has mechanisms,
Security Council

00:33:04.490 --> 00:33:07.550
has mechanisms to call
for military force.

00:33:07.550 --> 00:33:09.500
It specifically didn't use them.

00:33:09.500 --> 00:33:11.000
And the United
States, incidentally,

00:33:11.000 --> 00:33:14.120
didn't want it to use them
because those UN resolutions

00:33:14.120 --> 00:33:17.570
were intended as nothing more
than a weapon against Congress

00:33:17.570 --> 00:33:20.780
so that you could turn
to people in Congress

00:33:20.780 --> 00:33:23.570
and say, you guys aren't
as patriotic as Malaysia

00:33:23.570 --> 00:33:24.980
or something.

00:33:24.980 --> 00:33:27.120
And that's it.

00:33:27.120 --> 00:33:31.820
Now, this whole record is one of
simply undercutting the United

00:33:31.820 --> 00:33:35.930
Nations in slightly different
fashion than the usual one.

00:33:35.930 --> 00:33:38.720
Why were the sanctions of
such unprecedented severity?

00:33:38.720 --> 00:33:40.190
Well, that's easy
to answer, too.

00:33:40.190 --> 00:33:42.020
It's because of
tremendous US pressure.

00:33:42.020 --> 00:33:43.910
US is a scary place.

00:33:43.910 --> 00:33:46.200
And people don't
stand up against it.

00:33:46.200 --> 00:33:48.590
It's dangerous and frightening.

00:33:48.590 --> 00:33:51.099
And if the US is putting a
lot of-- it's also powerful.

00:33:51.099 --> 00:33:52.640
And if the US is
putting pressure on,

00:33:52.640 --> 00:33:54.240
people tend to go along.

00:33:54.240 --> 00:33:56.210
Not gonna stand up against--

00:33:56.210 --> 00:33:58.670
you don't stand up
against a violent, lawless

00:33:58.670 --> 00:33:59.960
terrorist state.

00:33:59.960 --> 00:34:03.350
And what happened is the
sanctions were imposed.

00:34:03.350 --> 00:34:04.592
Again, it's not explicit.

00:34:04.592 --> 00:34:06.050
There's nothing
explicit in the UN.

00:34:06.050 --> 00:34:08.150
In fact, the UN
resolutions don't even

00:34:08.150 --> 00:34:11.389
call for enforcing sanctions, if
you actually read the wording.

00:34:11.389 --> 00:34:13.639
But it was interpreted
the US way.

00:34:13.639 --> 00:34:15.560
Block everything,
including force.

00:34:15.560 --> 00:34:17.630
So that answers the
first set of questions.

00:34:17.630 --> 00:34:19.400
Very simple.

00:34:19.400 --> 00:34:21.770
The UN could act because
the United States for once

00:34:21.770 --> 00:34:24.679
permitted it to, instead of
blocking it, as is usual.

00:34:24.679 --> 00:34:26.201
The reaction was
particularly harsh

00:34:26.201 --> 00:34:28.159
because the United States
twisted a lot of arms

00:34:28.159 --> 00:34:29.300
and scared a lot of people.

00:34:32.279 --> 00:34:34.070
And that's about all
there is to it, as far

00:34:34.070 --> 00:34:35.940
as the first questions
are concerned.

00:34:35.940 --> 00:34:38.300
What about the more
interesting ones--

00:34:38.300 --> 00:34:42.830
why are the United States and
England isolated on this issue?

00:34:42.830 --> 00:34:45.699
Well, that's
important to look at.

00:34:45.699 --> 00:34:47.270
Here, you have to
look a little more

00:34:47.270 --> 00:34:49.590
carefully at what's going on.

00:34:49.590 --> 00:34:53.239
So let's start with this
latest Iraqi offer, disclosed

00:34:53.239 --> 00:34:57.510
by US officials, remember,
to withdraw from Kuwait

00:34:57.510 --> 00:35:02.400
in return for agreements on two
major issues, regional issues--

00:35:02.400 --> 00:35:07.290
the Arab-Israeli conflict and
weapons of mass destruction.

00:35:07.290 --> 00:35:09.900
Why won't the US permit that?

00:35:09.900 --> 00:35:13.080
Well, I've already mentioned
that the US is opposed

00:35:13.080 --> 00:35:17.040
to an international conference
quite independently of anything

00:35:17.040 --> 00:35:19.320
having to do with Iraq.

00:35:19.320 --> 00:35:21.270
And in fact, we know
that because the US

00:35:21.270 --> 00:35:23.970
has been opposed to an
international conference

00:35:23.970 --> 00:35:26.664
long before Iraq invaded Kuwait.

00:35:26.664 --> 00:35:28.830
So the argument that we're
now opposed to it because

00:35:28.830 --> 00:35:31.200
of linkage, that can't be true.

00:35:31.200 --> 00:35:33.540
We were opposed to it before,
quite apart from the fact

00:35:33.540 --> 00:35:36.320
that we standardly
reward aggressors, even

00:35:36.320 --> 00:35:37.920
much worse cases than this.

00:35:37.920 --> 00:35:39.550
So that's not the answer.

00:35:39.550 --> 00:35:40.920
What is the answer?

00:35:40.920 --> 00:35:43.789
Well, the answer to that
is also painfully simple.

00:35:43.789 --> 00:35:46.080
The United States is opposed
to a diplomatic settlement

00:35:46.080 --> 00:35:47.610
of the Arab-Israeli conflict.

00:35:47.610 --> 00:35:49.950
It's been opposed to
it for a long time.

00:35:49.950 --> 00:35:51.790
And it's isolated on that.

00:35:51.790 --> 00:35:55.080
The last vote at the
United Nations, last year,

00:35:55.080 --> 00:35:57.390
was 151 to 3.

00:35:57.390 --> 00:36:00.840
The three were the United
States, Israel, and Dominica.

00:36:00.840 --> 00:36:02.880
Now, that's the
kind of unanimity

00:36:02.880 --> 00:36:05.130
you never get at the
United-- all the NATO allies

00:36:05.130 --> 00:36:06.300
voted against us.

00:36:06.300 --> 00:36:07.710
Everybody did.

00:36:07.710 --> 00:36:09.510
I mean, maybe one
or two countries

00:36:09.510 --> 00:36:10.860
were sleeping or something.

00:36:10.860 --> 00:36:15.510
But it was basically the
world against George Bush.

00:36:15.510 --> 00:36:21.660
And again, by a large, much
larger majority than the world

00:36:21.660 --> 00:36:23.550
against Saddam Hussein.

00:36:23.550 --> 00:36:26.370
Well, that's the way
it's been for 20 years.

00:36:26.370 --> 00:36:29.700
The United States and Israel
have been essentially alone

00:36:29.700 --> 00:36:31.380
in blocking a
political settlement

00:36:31.380 --> 00:36:35.250
of the Arab-Israeli conflict,
which has been supported

00:36:35.250 --> 00:36:36.059
by the whole world.

00:36:36.059 --> 00:36:37.725
Well, that tells you
why they're against

00:36:37.725 --> 00:36:39.210
an international conference.

00:36:39.210 --> 00:36:42.480
What's gonna happen at an
international conference?

00:36:42.480 --> 00:36:44.250
No matter how you
pick the people--

00:36:44.250 --> 00:36:46.666
you pick them as you like--
there's no way to-- unless you

00:36:46.666 --> 00:36:48.110
pick just Dominica.

00:36:48.110 --> 00:36:51.870
At an international conference,
there's going to be pressure--

00:36:51.870 --> 00:36:53.760
and that'll probably
change the day

00:36:53.760 --> 00:36:55.920
after the vote, when
they paid them off.

00:36:58.770 --> 00:37:00.750
At any international
conference, there's

00:37:00.750 --> 00:37:03.540
going to be pressure for
a diplomatic settlement.

00:37:03.540 --> 00:37:06.240
And the United States doesn't
want a diplomatic settlement.

00:37:06.240 --> 00:37:08.850
Therefore, there can't be
an international conference.

00:37:08.850 --> 00:37:10.230
Period.

00:37:10.230 --> 00:37:11.700
Nothing to do with linkage.

00:37:11.700 --> 00:37:13.320
Nothing to do with Iraq.

00:37:13.320 --> 00:37:14.520
Nothing to do with anything.

00:37:14.520 --> 00:37:16.810
That's why it's the way
it's been for years.

00:37:16.810 --> 00:37:19.110
The United States has been
vetoing Security Council

00:37:19.110 --> 00:37:22.590
resolutions, voting virtually
alone against General Assembly

00:37:22.590 --> 00:37:25.710
resolutions, undercutting
every possible initiative.

00:37:25.710 --> 00:37:29.370
This has been going
on since 1970-'71.

00:37:29.370 --> 00:37:32.460
Nothing new about it.

00:37:32.460 --> 00:37:36.720
Our blocking of the
diplomatic process on this

00:37:36.720 --> 00:37:38.880
even has a technical name
in the United States.

00:37:38.880 --> 00:37:41.370
It's called the peace process.

00:37:41.370 --> 00:37:42.780
We're pursuing
the peace process,

00:37:42.780 --> 00:37:45.300
meaning blocking every
conceivable possibility

00:37:45.300 --> 00:37:46.710
to get peace.

00:37:46.710 --> 00:37:50.910
And in fact, occasionally,
you'll get an article.

00:37:50.910 --> 00:37:55.475
It will say somewhere,
well, the Russians are--

00:37:55.475 --> 00:37:57.600
I remember an article in
the New York Times saying,

00:37:57.600 --> 00:38:00.000
the Russians are
trying to come aboard.

00:38:00.000 --> 00:38:03.510
They're trying to get to
join the peace process,

00:38:03.510 --> 00:38:05.460
meaning maybe they're
going to accept the US

00:38:05.460 --> 00:38:08.080
position in isolation from
the rest of the world.

00:38:08.080 --> 00:38:11.070
If they do that, they'll be
on the team, team being us,

00:38:11.070 --> 00:38:12.040
by definition.

00:38:12.040 --> 00:38:14.220
Everyone else is off the team.

00:38:14.220 --> 00:38:16.350
Well, that's the way it's
been for a long time.

00:38:16.350 --> 00:38:18.725
So therefore, there can't be
an international conference.

00:38:18.725 --> 00:38:20.340
And therefore, we go to war.

00:38:20.340 --> 00:38:23.160
What about the second
issue, the issue

00:38:23.160 --> 00:38:25.110
of weapons of mass destruction?

00:38:25.110 --> 00:38:27.240
Well, refusal of
linkage on this one

00:38:27.240 --> 00:38:30.960
is particularly remarkable
because this is a disarmament

00:38:30.960 --> 00:38:31.740
issue.

00:38:31.740 --> 00:38:33.630
And disarmament
issues are always

00:38:33.630 --> 00:38:36.240
settled in a regional
or global context.

00:38:36.240 --> 00:38:39.570
I mean, that's not
even in dispute, right?

00:38:39.570 --> 00:38:43.700
Of course disarmament issues are
settled in a broader context,

00:38:43.700 --> 00:38:45.180
regional or maybe global.

00:38:45.180 --> 00:38:46.350
Everyone is.

00:38:46.350 --> 00:38:48.490
But we reject it in this case.

00:38:48.490 --> 00:38:50.490
And once again, that
rejection, though it's

00:38:50.490 --> 00:38:52.260
claimed to be based
on linkage, has

00:38:52.260 --> 00:38:54.720
nothing to do with Iraq's
conquest of Kuwait.

00:38:54.720 --> 00:38:56.760
And it's easy to prove that.

00:38:56.760 --> 00:38:59.700
We can prove it very easily by
simply noticing that the United

00:38:59.700 --> 00:39:02.070
States also opposed the
diplomatic settlement

00:39:02.070 --> 00:39:04.860
of that problem before the
Iraq invasion of Kuwait.

00:39:04.860 --> 00:39:08.400
In fact, last April, when
Saddam Hussein was still

00:39:08.400 --> 00:39:11.040
George Bush's great
friend and favored trading

00:39:11.040 --> 00:39:14.940
partner, at that
last April, Iraq

00:39:14.940 --> 00:39:18.750
made an offer to destroy
its nonconventional weapons,

00:39:18.750 --> 00:39:21.380
to destroy its biological
and chemical weapons.

00:39:21.380 --> 00:39:23.940
And that was turned down
by the United States.

00:39:23.940 --> 00:39:26.970
The United States responded
and turned it down

00:39:26.970 --> 00:39:29.430
because Saddam Hussein
insisted that this

00:39:29.430 --> 00:39:31.410
be done in a regional context--

00:39:31.410 --> 00:39:34.710
that is, that Israel
destroy its nuclear weapons

00:39:34.710 --> 00:39:37.190
and its biological
and chemical weapons.

00:39:37.190 --> 00:39:40.760
Well, that's not entirely
unreasonable, one might think.

00:39:40.760 --> 00:39:43.020
But the United
States rejected it.

00:39:43.020 --> 00:39:46.080
And the terms of the rejection
are worth listening to.

00:39:46.080 --> 00:39:48.570
The official State
Department rejection said,

00:39:48.570 --> 00:39:50.580
they welcomed Saddam
Hussein's offer

00:39:50.580 --> 00:39:52.350
to destroy his own weapons.

00:39:52.350 --> 00:39:55.480
But that can't be
linked, United States

00:39:55.480 --> 00:39:59.616
rejects any link to
other weapons issues.

00:39:59.616 --> 00:40:03.631
Now, the other weapons
issues were left unspecified.

00:40:03.631 --> 00:40:05.630
But, of course, everybody
knows what that means.

00:40:05.630 --> 00:40:07.340
That means Israel's
nuclear weapons,

00:40:07.340 --> 00:40:09.756
which they've had for at least
20 years, probably a couple

00:40:09.756 --> 00:40:10.870
hundred of them.

00:40:10.870 --> 00:40:12.430
Now, you can't mention that.

00:40:12.430 --> 00:40:14.320
If you look at the
US diplomatic record,

00:40:14.320 --> 00:40:17.770
you'll notice that no
spokesperson for the government

00:40:17.770 --> 00:40:20.260
ever refers to Israeli
nuclear weapons.

00:40:20.260 --> 00:40:22.090
I mean, everyone knows
they've got them.

00:40:22.090 --> 00:40:23.354
That's not even a question.

00:40:23.354 --> 00:40:24.520
But you can't refer to them.

00:40:24.520 --> 00:40:25.720
Why can't you refer to them?

00:40:25.720 --> 00:40:28.540
Well, there's a reason
for that in American law.

00:40:28.540 --> 00:40:32.330
According to amendments to the
foreign aid legislation back

00:40:32.330 --> 00:40:35.920
in the '70s, it's illegal
under American law

00:40:35.920 --> 00:40:38.770
to give any aid at all,
including economic aid,

00:40:38.770 --> 00:40:41.560
to any country that's engaged
in clandestine nuclear weapons

00:40:41.560 --> 00:40:42.770
development.

00:40:42.770 --> 00:40:44.890
So therefore, if you
concede that Israel

00:40:44.890 --> 00:40:46.990
has nuclear weapons,
all aid to Israel

00:40:46.990 --> 00:40:49.220
has been illegal for years.

00:40:49.220 --> 00:40:50.380
And you can't concede that.

00:40:50.380 --> 00:40:53.590
So therefore, you don't mention
Israeli nuclear weapons.

00:40:53.590 --> 00:40:55.750
But in order to
preserve Israel's right

00:40:55.750 --> 00:40:59.920
to continue nuclear
weapons development--

00:40:59.920 --> 00:41:03.130
without mentioning
it, of course--

00:41:03.130 --> 00:41:07.000
we reject the possibility
of treating Saddam Hussein's

00:41:07.000 --> 00:41:10.120
military arsenals through
diplomatic means, which

00:41:10.120 --> 00:41:11.800
is the right way to do it.

00:41:11.800 --> 00:41:13.030
And we go to war.

00:41:16.430 --> 00:41:17.560
That's the other element.

00:41:17.560 --> 00:41:20.050
Notice there are three
issues here that are linked.

00:41:20.050 --> 00:41:22.040
One is Iraqi
withdrawal from Kuwait.

00:41:22.040 --> 00:41:25.030
Another, settlement of
the Arab-Israeli conflict

00:41:25.030 --> 00:41:26.800
and other regional
security problems.

00:41:26.800 --> 00:41:29.140
And a third, the problem
of Saddam Hussein's

00:41:29.140 --> 00:41:31.120
threatening military arsenals.

00:41:31.120 --> 00:41:33.280
There appears to be
a diplomatic approach

00:41:33.280 --> 00:41:35.290
to every one of
those three issues.

00:41:35.290 --> 00:41:37.930
The United States is opposed
to a diplomatic settlement

00:41:37.930 --> 00:41:39.730
of each of those issues.

00:41:39.730 --> 00:41:41.740
And therefore, it's
opposed to linking them.

00:41:41.740 --> 00:41:42.447
Very simple.

00:41:42.447 --> 00:41:44.530
That's the answer to why
we're opposed to linkage,

00:41:44.530 --> 00:41:47.050
meaning diplomacy,
and why we go to war.

00:41:47.050 --> 00:41:49.240
Well, that gives you
more understanding

00:41:49.240 --> 00:41:50.490
of what's going on.

00:41:50.490 --> 00:41:53.920
Britain is with us on this
alone, virtually alone.

00:41:53.920 --> 00:41:55.870
Except on the
Arab-Israeli conflict,

00:41:55.870 --> 00:41:57.580
even Britain's not with us.

00:41:57.580 --> 00:42:00.080
In fact, nobody
is except Israel.

00:42:00.080 --> 00:42:03.911
Well, that leaves, basically,
really, the serious

00:42:03.911 --> 00:42:06.160
question-- why are the United
States and Britain alone

00:42:06.160 --> 00:42:08.790
on this issue?

00:42:08.790 --> 00:42:11.770
Why is it the world versus
the United States and England,

00:42:11.770 --> 00:42:13.547
which it pretty much is?

00:42:13.547 --> 00:42:15.130
That's a serious
question because it's

00:42:15.130 --> 00:42:17.260
going to blow up a
large part of the world

00:42:17.260 --> 00:42:21.910
very shortly, maybe
getting started right now.

00:42:21.910 --> 00:42:24.340
So it's worth trying
to understand it.

00:42:24.340 --> 00:42:27.217
Well, the other questions
are really trivial.

00:42:27.217 --> 00:42:29.050
I mean, every question
I've mentioned so far

00:42:29.050 --> 00:42:31.600
shouldn't strain the
intelligence of a 10-year-old.

00:42:31.600 --> 00:42:33.700
If it's not in the front
pages of the newspaper,

00:42:33.700 --> 00:42:35.950
it's because they're trying
hard not to understand it.

00:42:35.950 --> 00:42:37.639
There's nothing
subtle about this.

00:42:37.639 --> 00:42:40.180
And you don't have to have any
complicated knowledge of world

00:42:40.180 --> 00:42:42.541
affairs or be able to think
through hard arguments

00:42:42.541 --> 00:42:43.040
or anything.

00:42:43.040 --> 00:42:44.373
You don't have to know anything.

00:42:44.373 --> 00:42:47.770
Everything that I've just
said is basically elementary.

00:42:47.770 --> 00:42:50.920
And there shouldn't be any
much confusion about it.

00:42:50.920 --> 00:42:52.540
However, this
other question, why

00:42:52.540 --> 00:42:55.570
the United States and England
are alone, that's harder.

00:42:55.570 --> 00:42:57.580
There you really
have to speculate.

00:42:57.580 --> 00:43:00.880
You have to try to figure out
what's behind policy decisions

00:43:00.880 --> 00:43:02.380
and so on.

00:43:02.380 --> 00:43:03.869
And it is speculation.

00:43:03.869 --> 00:43:04.660
So you could argue.

00:43:04.660 --> 00:43:08.520
But speculation doesn't have
to be totally uninformed.

00:43:08.520 --> 00:43:11.780
There's evidence.

00:43:11.780 --> 00:43:13.850
There's evidence from
history, and there's

00:43:13.850 --> 00:43:18.575
evidence from the
current world situation.

00:43:18.575 --> 00:43:20.340
How am I doing on time?

00:43:20.340 --> 00:43:21.810
AUDIENCE: Quarter after 8:00.

00:43:21.810 --> 00:43:23.870
CHOMSKY: Should I go
three more minutes?

00:43:23.870 --> 00:43:24.990
OK.

00:43:24.990 --> 00:43:26.680
Tell me if I'm--

00:43:26.680 --> 00:43:30.310
so let's take a look at those
two questions, those two

00:43:30.310 --> 00:43:30.880
issues.

00:43:30.880 --> 00:43:32.610
We know something about those.

00:43:32.610 --> 00:43:34.680
On the matter of
history, there's

00:43:34.680 --> 00:43:37.810
some very, again,
pretty obvious points.

00:43:37.810 --> 00:43:40.300
There was an imperial
settlement in that region.

00:43:40.300 --> 00:43:42.970
And that imperial settlement
was imposed by the United States

00:43:42.970 --> 00:43:44.530
and England.

00:43:44.530 --> 00:43:46.300
It was, in fact,
imposed in such a way

00:43:46.300 --> 00:43:49.390
that they would be the
beneficiaries of it.

00:43:49.390 --> 00:43:52.600
Given that fact, it's
not too surprising to see

00:43:52.600 --> 00:43:55.184
that those are the two
countries that are girding up

00:43:55.184 --> 00:43:57.100
for war in the Gulf while
everybody else keeps

00:43:57.100 --> 00:43:58.270
their distance.

00:43:58.270 --> 00:43:59.935
Now, the history
of this goes back

00:43:59.935 --> 00:44:01.310
to the early part
of the century.

00:44:01.310 --> 00:44:03.070
Originally, it was
England and France.

00:44:03.070 --> 00:44:06.970
They sort of ran the region.

00:44:06.970 --> 00:44:08.920
By the First World
War, the United States

00:44:08.920 --> 00:44:11.650
was becoming a big actor
in international affairs,

00:44:11.650 --> 00:44:13.090
and it wanted to get in, too.

00:44:13.090 --> 00:44:18.000
So there was a British,
French, US division

00:44:18.000 --> 00:44:20.860
around by the '20s and '30s.

00:44:20.860 --> 00:44:24.310
After the Second World
War, the United States

00:44:24.310 --> 00:44:26.020
essentially took over.

00:44:26.020 --> 00:44:31.180
The relations of power were such
that the United States was just

00:44:31.180 --> 00:44:33.340
overwhelmingly powerful.

00:44:33.340 --> 00:44:36.250
France was kicked in
the pants immediately.

00:44:36.250 --> 00:44:38.560
They were kicked out of the
arrangement on the grounds

00:44:38.560 --> 00:44:41.920
that France was an enemy state,
and therefore the agreements

00:44:41.920 --> 00:44:44.290
with it, the treaties and
so on, didn't mean anything.

00:44:44.290 --> 00:44:45.789
It was an enemy
state because it had

00:44:45.789 --> 00:44:47.110
been under German occupation.

00:44:47.110 --> 00:44:50.050
So that got rid of France and
left England and the United

00:44:50.050 --> 00:44:51.920
States.

00:44:51.920 --> 00:44:54.990
The British tried to hang on.

00:44:54.990 --> 00:44:59.029
And the United States was
not allowing them very much.

00:44:59.029 --> 00:45:00.820
There was a lot of
conflict between Britain

00:45:00.820 --> 00:45:03.810
and the United States in the
late '40s and the early '50s.

00:45:03.810 --> 00:45:08.500
It was pretty much resolved by
the mid-'50s under the dictates

00:45:08.500 --> 00:45:10.470
of the stronger partner.

00:45:10.470 --> 00:45:14.140
I mean, the United States wanted
to keep Britain in the game.

00:45:14.140 --> 00:45:19.480
The reason for that
was stated uncautiously

00:45:19.480 --> 00:45:24.380
in a secret discussion by
a high Kennedy official,

00:45:24.380 --> 00:45:25.750
which later got declassified.

00:45:25.750 --> 00:45:29.110
He described England
as "our lieutenant."

00:45:29.110 --> 00:45:31.780
And then he said, the
fashionable word is "partner."

00:45:31.780 --> 00:45:33.640
Now, you don't tell
the British about this.

00:45:33.640 --> 00:45:36.190
They're supposed to have
delusions of partnership.

00:45:36.190 --> 00:45:39.250
But in secret, they're
our lieutenants.

00:45:39.250 --> 00:45:41.140
So we want to keep them viable.

00:45:41.140 --> 00:45:44.150
That's called the
special relationship.

00:45:44.150 --> 00:45:50.080
And by the '50s, it was a
Anglo-American condominium

00:45:50.080 --> 00:45:53.930
with the US calling most
of the shots, of course.

00:45:53.930 --> 00:45:57.160
Now, there's an earlier
history, needless to say.

00:45:57.160 --> 00:45:58.840
And even if nobody
remembers it here,

00:45:58.840 --> 00:46:00.950
they probably
remember it elsewhere.

00:46:00.950 --> 00:46:03.370
So just in the
case of that, right

00:46:03.370 --> 00:46:05.620
after the First World
War, the British

00:46:05.620 --> 00:46:07.870
were having some problems
holding together the empire.

00:46:07.870 --> 00:46:10.300
Britain was weakened
by the First World War.

00:46:10.300 --> 00:46:13.750
And that meant it was kind of
hard to keep the lid on things.

00:46:13.750 --> 00:46:16.330
And they had to get pretty
violent, in fact, to do it.

00:46:16.330 --> 00:46:19.120
That's when the Amritsar
massacre took place in India

00:46:19.120 --> 00:46:24.460
in 1919, with about 400
unarmed protesters massacred.

00:46:24.460 --> 00:46:26.500
There were similar
problems in Iraq.

00:46:26.500 --> 00:46:29.971
And England didn't have the
manpower to put it down.

00:46:29.971 --> 00:46:31.720
They didn't have the
troops at that point.

00:46:31.720 --> 00:46:34.540
So they decided to
innovate and turn

00:46:34.540 --> 00:46:38.860
to terror bombing of villages
for the first time, which

00:46:38.860 --> 00:46:39.610
is cheaper.

00:46:39.610 --> 00:46:41.234
You don't need a lot
of force for that.

00:46:44.170 --> 00:46:47.050
Also, terror bombing of
villages was used even

00:46:47.050 --> 00:46:51.890
to collect taxes from tribesmen
and that sort of thing.

00:46:51.890 --> 00:46:54.370
And it was pretty effective,
but it wasn't quite enough.

00:46:54.370 --> 00:46:59.200
The RAF, Royal Air Force,
Middle East command in 1919

00:46:59.200 --> 00:47:05.210
requested authorization for the
use of chemical weapons as--

00:47:05.210 --> 00:47:08.470
I'm quoting-- "against
recalcitrant Arabs

00:47:08.470 --> 00:47:09.970
as experiment."

00:47:09.970 --> 00:47:12.250
Now, chemical weapons,
that means poison gas.

00:47:12.250 --> 00:47:15.880
At the time, that was regarded
with the ultimate horror.

00:47:15.880 --> 00:47:18.670
This was right after
the First World War.

00:47:18.670 --> 00:47:20.065
It was ultimate revulsion.

00:47:20.065 --> 00:47:21.250
It was poison gas.

00:47:21.250 --> 00:47:24.610
So they requested
authorization to do that.

00:47:24.610 --> 00:47:27.100
The India office didn't think
it was a very bright idea.

00:47:27.100 --> 00:47:29.390
They thought it would have
negative consequences.

00:47:29.390 --> 00:47:32.560
But they were overruled
by the Secretary of State

00:47:32.560 --> 00:47:35.650
at the war office,
who dismissed what

00:47:35.650 --> 00:47:38.780
he called this squeamishness
over the use of gas.

00:47:38.780 --> 00:47:42.550
And he declared
himself to be strongly

00:47:42.550 --> 00:47:48.750
in favor of the use of poison
gas against uncivilized tribes.

00:47:48.750 --> 00:47:50.620
That was Winston
Churchill, incidentally,

00:47:50.620 --> 00:47:53.530
one of the great moral
heroes of the West.

00:47:53.530 --> 00:47:57.100
And it kind of
went on from there.

00:47:57.100 --> 00:47:58.990
So there is an earlier history.

00:47:58.990 --> 00:48:02.140
But let's put that aside and
turn to the post Second World

00:48:02.140 --> 00:48:04.940
War period.

00:48:04.940 --> 00:48:08.094
The US and Britain pretty
much had it under control.

00:48:08.094 --> 00:48:09.010
But there was a break.

00:48:09.010 --> 00:48:12.070
There was a break in
1953, when Iran tried

00:48:12.070 --> 00:48:13.480
to take control of its own oil.

00:48:13.480 --> 00:48:17.410
That was taken care of
with a CIA-backed coup.

00:48:17.410 --> 00:48:19.180
The big break, the
serious one, was

00:48:19.180 --> 00:48:24.840
in 1958, when Iraq had
a nationalist officers

00:48:24.840 --> 00:48:28.380
coup, which overthrew
a dependent regime

00:48:28.380 --> 00:48:31.890
and took Iraq
partially, at least,

00:48:31.890 --> 00:48:34.110
out of the Anglo-American
condominium.

00:48:34.110 --> 00:48:38.220
Well, that caused a furor
in Washington and London.

00:48:38.220 --> 00:48:42.510
One response was a marine
landing in Lebanon the next day

00:48:42.510 --> 00:48:44.200
to prop up the regime there.

00:48:44.200 --> 00:48:47.340
A second response was
apparent authorization

00:48:47.340 --> 00:48:51.030
by Eisenhower of the use
of nuclear weapons in case

00:48:51.030 --> 00:48:55.110
any unfriendly forces
went into Kuwait.

00:48:55.110 --> 00:48:57.660
Third response was a set
of decisions by Britain

00:48:57.660 --> 00:49:00.510
and the United States about
how to handle the situation.

00:49:00.510 --> 00:49:03.015
And Kuwait at that time
was more or less a colony,

00:49:03.015 --> 00:49:05.460
although they called
it independent.

00:49:05.460 --> 00:49:09.060
And the British decided
that it would be a wise idea

00:49:09.060 --> 00:49:11.940
to give Kuwait at least
nominal independence

00:49:11.940 --> 00:49:13.620
to try to undercut
the possibility

00:49:13.620 --> 00:49:16.710
that a nationalist revolt
might spread there,

00:49:16.710 --> 00:49:21.030
but, however, reserving the
right to intervene by force,

00:49:21.030 --> 00:49:24.960
or, as they put it, ruthlessly
to intervene if anything goes

00:49:24.960 --> 00:49:28.150
wrong, no matter
who's responsible.

00:49:28.150 --> 00:49:31.170
And the United States
reserved the same right

00:49:31.170 --> 00:49:33.250
for the rest of the region.

00:49:33.250 --> 00:49:35.280
In fact, they jointly
agreed that the oil

00:49:35.280 --> 00:49:39.970
fields of Kuwait,
Saudi Arabia, Bahrain,

00:49:39.970 --> 00:49:42.210
and the other
emirates, that they

00:49:42.210 --> 00:49:44.970
be kept in US-British hands.

00:49:44.970 --> 00:49:46.990
They were very clear
about the reasons.

00:49:46.990 --> 00:49:49.350
The reasons were-- and they're
important because they're

00:49:49.350 --> 00:49:52.200
still operative and, in fact,
more operative than ever.

00:49:52.200 --> 00:49:55.140
The reasons were
first, Britain, which

00:49:55.140 --> 00:49:58.110
had an ailing economy then--

00:49:58.110 --> 00:50:00.540
worse now, but an
ailing economy--

00:50:00.540 --> 00:50:04.710
needed privileged access
to the oil of Kuwait

00:50:04.710 --> 00:50:07.980
and, crucially, to
investments from Kuwait

00:50:07.980 --> 00:50:09.570
in order to prop up
its economy, which

00:50:09.570 --> 00:50:11.970
had to prop up sterling,
which was extremely weak.

00:50:11.970 --> 00:50:14.650
So that was a major condition.

00:50:14.650 --> 00:50:19.080
Now, by the early 1970s,
those very same considerations

00:50:19.080 --> 00:50:21.090
were applying to
the United States.

00:50:21.090 --> 00:50:25.260
The United States was
losing its world dominance--

00:50:25.260 --> 00:50:30.070
in fact, seriously losing it
from an economic point of view.

00:50:30.070 --> 00:50:33.630
And it began to need
not the oil, so much,

00:50:33.630 --> 00:50:35.290
but the profits from oil.

00:50:35.290 --> 00:50:37.860
Oil creates huge profits.

00:50:37.860 --> 00:50:41.150
And control over those profits
gives you plenty of power.

00:50:41.150 --> 00:50:43.320
And the United States
started needing 'em.

00:50:43.320 --> 00:50:45.990
Also, of course,
control over oil

00:50:45.990 --> 00:50:47.520
gives you a good
deal of influence

00:50:47.520 --> 00:50:49.790
over people who need oil.

00:50:49.790 --> 00:50:54.270
It's a lever of world
control, a significant lever.

00:50:54.270 --> 00:50:56.020
So you've got several reasons.

00:50:56.020 --> 00:50:58.440
One, access to the oil
on preferential terms.

00:50:58.440 --> 00:51:00.510
Two, a lever of world control.

00:51:00.510 --> 00:51:03.070
But three, just
the profits alone.

00:51:03.070 --> 00:51:05.040
They're necessary.

00:51:05.040 --> 00:51:09.090
And by the early '70s, that
was beginning to be serious.

00:51:09.090 --> 00:51:12.630
Going back to 1958 for a moment,
the National Security Council,

00:51:12.630 --> 00:51:16.440
the highest planning body
in decisions that have now

00:51:16.440 --> 00:51:18.800
been partially declassified--

00:51:18.800 --> 00:51:21.420
it isn't too much
declassification of US records,

00:51:21.420 --> 00:51:24.630
incidentally, because the
Reagan administration, which

00:51:24.630 --> 00:51:26.670
had a kind of a
fascist element to it,

00:51:26.670 --> 00:51:30.030
imposed very heavy restrictions
on declassification.

00:51:30.030 --> 00:51:32.830
They didn't want the public to
be able to see what's going on.

00:51:32.830 --> 00:51:37.140
So things get declassified now
with very heavy restrictions,

00:51:37.140 --> 00:51:39.360
so much so that the State
Department historians all

00:51:39.360 --> 00:51:40.800
quit a few months ago.

00:51:40.800 --> 00:51:43.800
But anyhow, enough
went through so you

00:51:43.800 --> 00:51:45.390
can see what was going on.

00:51:45.390 --> 00:51:47.700
The National Security
Council decided in '58,

00:51:47.700 --> 00:51:50.100
after the Iraqi
coup, that the US

00:51:50.100 --> 00:51:53.550
should be prepared to use
force if needed in order

00:51:53.550 --> 00:51:56.220
to secure British and American
interests in the region,

00:51:56.220 --> 00:51:58.680
in Kuwait and other
parts of the region.

00:51:58.680 --> 00:52:00.660
That tells us a lot about
what's going on now.

00:52:00.660 --> 00:52:03.090
As I say, by the
early '70s, the US

00:52:03.090 --> 00:52:06.070
needed the investments
and beginning

00:52:06.070 --> 00:52:08.550
to need the access to the oil
as much as the British did

00:52:08.550 --> 00:52:09.240
at that time.

00:52:09.240 --> 00:52:10.970
We're a little
behind them in this.

00:52:10.970 --> 00:52:13.020
In fact, this is one of the--

00:52:13.020 --> 00:52:17.760
there's been a very substantial
capital flow from the Gulf oil

00:52:17.760 --> 00:52:20.100
producers to England
and the United States.

00:52:20.100 --> 00:52:23.450
Other places, too, but primarily
England and the United States.

00:52:23.450 --> 00:52:27.720
And that's propped up their
financial institutions,

00:52:27.720 --> 00:52:30.690
their corporations, and
their general economies.

00:52:30.690 --> 00:52:33.600
Just purchase of Treasury
securities by Saudi Arabia

00:52:33.600 --> 00:52:35.770
props up the American economy.

00:52:35.770 --> 00:52:40.680
In fact, when the oil price
goes up, what in fact happens

00:52:40.680 --> 00:52:44.340
is dollars shift from one
bank to another in New York,

00:52:44.340 --> 00:52:47.460
and pounds shift from one
bank to another in London,

00:52:47.460 --> 00:52:49.710
to a large extent.

00:52:49.710 --> 00:52:52.310
Prices go up, a lot
of petrodollars.

00:52:52.310 --> 00:52:54.630
But they get spent,
and they get spent

00:52:54.630 --> 00:52:59.880
on exports and
construction firms

00:52:59.880 --> 00:53:03.660
and so on, primarily in
England and the United States.

00:53:03.660 --> 00:53:06.060
And a lot of it goes straight
to-- they purchase up

00:53:06.060 --> 00:53:10.500
parts of ailing banks and
Treasury securities and so on.

00:53:10.500 --> 00:53:13.920
That's been a big shot in the
arm for the US and British

00:53:13.920 --> 00:53:15.900
economies, British
for a long time

00:53:15.900 --> 00:53:18.040
and the United States
for the last 20 years.

00:53:18.040 --> 00:53:21.180
That's a good reason for keeping
control over this system.

00:53:21.180 --> 00:53:23.132
These are all good reasons.

00:53:23.132 --> 00:53:25.590
That's one of the reasons why
the United States and England

00:53:25.590 --> 00:53:27.120
have not been
particularly opposed

00:53:27.120 --> 00:53:28.670
to rises in the oil price.

00:53:28.670 --> 00:53:30.300
In fact, sometimes
they've favored it.

00:53:30.300 --> 00:53:33.090
After the big
near-quadrupling of oil

00:53:33.090 --> 00:53:35.610
prices in the early
'70s, that actually

00:53:35.610 --> 00:53:38.490
improved the British
and American economies,

00:53:38.490 --> 00:53:39.450
helped them.

00:53:39.450 --> 00:53:42.660
The balance of trade between
the United States and the oil

00:53:42.660 --> 00:53:47.040
producers improved
in the US favor

00:53:47.040 --> 00:53:51.510
after the oil price
went up, quadrupled,

00:53:51.510 --> 00:53:54.330
for exactly the
reasons I mentioned.

00:53:54.330 --> 00:53:59.670
Also, the rise in oil
prices helps countries

00:53:59.670 --> 00:54:01.842
that produce high-cost oil.

00:54:01.842 --> 00:54:03.550
There are two such
countries, primarily--

00:54:03.550 --> 00:54:05.370
the United States and England.

00:54:05.370 --> 00:54:09.960
The oil corporations knew about
Alaska oil a long time ago.

00:54:09.960 --> 00:54:11.640
But it wasn't profitable enough.

00:54:11.640 --> 00:54:14.250
After the price quadrupled
in the early '70s,

00:54:14.250 --> 00:54:16.920
it became very profitable,
so they started producing it.

00:54:16.920 --> 00:54:18.790
Same with North Sea oil.

00:54:18.790 --> 00:54:19.830
All of that is good.

00:54:19.830 --> 00:54:22.830
And also, quite apart from
that, the energy corporations

00:54:22.830 --> 00:54:24.240
are all US--

00:54:24.240 --> 00:54:25.710
I mean, with some
minor exceptions,

00:54:25.710 --> 00:54:27.660
they're basically US-British.

00:54:27.660 --> 00:54:31.240
And their profits go up
as the oil price goes up.

00:54:31.240 --> 00:54:34.260
So these are all parts of
the system, important parts.

00:54:34.260 --> 00:54:36.870
Well, without going on, you
put all these things together,

00:54:36.870 --> 00:54:40.050
you see some pretty good reasons
why two countries are getting

00:54:40.050 --> 00:54:42.240
ready to go to war,
just as they said

00:54:42.240 --> 00:54:44.250
they were going to do
back in the late '50s,

00:54:44.250 --> 00:54:47.190
and probably since, but we
don't have documents since.

00:54:47.190 --> 00:54:49.680
We do have documents up to
then, and we know what they say,

00:54:49.680 --> 00:54:51.030
and they make a lot of sense.

00:54:51.030 --> 00:54:52.529
And you can look
at the record since

00:54:52.529 --> 00:54:54.390
and see it follows
the same lines.

00:54:54.390 --> 00:54:56.730
Well, there's one final
point to make about this.

00:54:56.730 --> 00:54:59.790
And here, we really
do speculate.

00:54:59.790 --> 00:55:02.440
That has to do with the
current world system.

00:55:02.440 --> 00:55:04.910
And take a look at what the
current world system is like.

00:55:04.910 --> 00:55:07.410
There's a lot of talk about
this fabled new world order.

00:55:07.410 --> 00:55:09.300
And there's something to it.

00:55:09.300 --> 00:55:11.079
There are changes in the world.

00:55:11.079 --> 00:55:13.620
In fact, there have been changes
in the world for the last 20

00:55:13.620 --> 00:55:15.500
years, and important ones.

00:55:15.500 --> 00:55:18.030
About 20 years ago, it
was becoming quite clear

00:55:18.030 --> 00:55:23.390
that economic power was
diffusing in US domains.

00:55:23.390 --> 00:55:26.400
It was diffusing into
three major power centers--

00:55:26.400 --> 00:55:30.060
German-led Europe,
Japan and its region,

00:55:30.060 --> 00:55:32.290
and the United States
and its region.

00:55:32.290 --> 00:55:36.210
United States is still the
biggest, but not very much

00:55:36.210 --> 00:55:37.150
anymore.

00:55:37.150 --> 00:55:40.260
And in fact, the Reaganite
policies severely

00:55:40.260 --> 00:55:42.660
harmed the American
economy, and those costs

00:55:42.660 --> 00:55:43.800
have not yet come due.

00:55:43.800 --> 00:55:46.080
But they're serious.

00:55:46.080 --> 00:55:48.360
The Vietnam War harmed
the American economy.

00:55:48.360 --> 00:55:50.400
It was very costly
to the United States,

00:55:50.400 --> 00:55:53.580
very beneficial to the
industrial rivals of the United

00:55:53.580 --> 00:55:54.420
States.

00:55:54.420 --> 00:55:56.280
And all of that stuff
changed the structure

00:55:56.280 --> 00:55:57.420
of the world economy.

00:55:57.420 --> 00:55:59.910
That's a major part of the
new world order-- three

00:55:59.910 --> 00:56:02.990
major powers, the US,
especially after Reagan,

00:56:02.990 --> 00:56:06.810
now deeply in debt
and much weakened.

00:56:06.810 --> 00:56:08.790
That's part of the
new world order.

00:56:08.790 --> 00:56:10.297
What about the Russian--

00:56:10.297 --> 00:56:11.700
five minutes, yeah.

00:56:11.700 --> 00:56:12.620
I'm just about done.

00:56:12.620 --> 00:56:14.970
What about the disappearance
of the Soviet Union?

00:56:14.970 --> 00:56:17.670
Well, by the mid-'70s, it was
pretty clear that the Russians

00:56:17.670 --> 00:56:18.960
were in bad trouble.

00:56:18.960 --> 00:56:20.670
In fact, their
military expenditures

00:56:20.670 --> 00:56:24.360
started leveling off,
exactly the opposite of what

00:56:24.360 --> 00:56:25.680
we were told at the time.

00:56:25.680 --> 00:56:27.610
But the CIA has now conceded it.

00:56:27.610 --> 00:56:30.090
And by a couple of years
later, it's pretty clear

00:56:30.090 --> 00:56:31.502
that they're finished.

00:56:31.502 --> 00:56:32.960
And the effect of
that is-- there's

00:56:32.960 --> 00:56:34.620
several effects to that.

00:56:34.620 --> 00:56:37.140
One effect is rhetorical.

00:56:37.140 --> 00:56:41.610
Since 1917, every
American intervention

00:56:41.610 --> 00:56:44.550
anywhere has been in defense
against the Russians.

00:56:44.550 --> 00:56:47.670
Like when the US began to
support fascist Italy back

00:56:47.670 --> 00:56:50.965
in 1922, as they did,
that was in defense

00:56:50.965 --> 00:56:51.840
against the Russians.

00:56:51.840 --> 00:56:53.840
And everything up until
then has been in defense

00:56:53.840 --> 00:56:54.960
against the Russians.

00:56:54.960 --> 00:56:57.600
By about 1988, that
becomes impossible.

00:56:57.600 --> 00:56:59.910
I mean, even the most fertile
imagination can't say,

00:56:59.910 --> 00:57:02.160
we're defending ourselves
against the Russians.

00:57:02.160 --> 00:57:03.990
So you need other excuses.

00:57:03.990 --> 00:57:07.020
That's one change-- new
rhetorical framework.

00:57:07.020 --> 00:57:09.792
The invasion of Panama was
historic in this respect.

00:57:09.792 --> 00:57:11.250
I mean, it was just
like everything

00:57:11.250 --> 00:57:14.090
that's been going on for a
couple of hundred years in most

00:57:14.090 --> 00:57:15.840
respects, but it was
different in that you

00:57:15.840 --> 00:57:18.630
couldn't say we were defending
ourselves against the Russians.

00:57:18.630 --> 00:57:21.060
So one change is
rhetorical, and that's

00:57:21.060 --> 00:57:22.660
happening again in the Gulf.

00:57:22.660 --> 00:57:25.020
A second change is
more significant.

00:57:25.020 --> 00:57:29.160
The Soviet Union and its
empire are now opened up freely

00:57:29.160 --> 00:57:30.690
to Western exploitation.

00:57:30.690 --> 00:57:34.260
That is, they're reverting to
what they were before 1917--

00:57:34.260 --> 00:57:37.680
quasi-colonial dependencies
of the industrial West.

00:57:37.680 --> 00:57:39.510
And that's a big change.

00:57:39.510 --> 00:57:41.430
But of course, the
United States and Britain

00:57:41.430 --> 00:57:43.530
are not ahead in that game.

00:57:43.530 --> 00:57:44.700
Germany's ahead.

00:57:44.700 --> 00:57:47.820
And Japan, when it decides to
get into it, will be ahead.

00:57:47.820 --> 00:57:49.260
But Britain and
the United States

00:57:49.260 --> 00:57:50.640
are not capital-rich countries.

00:57:50.640 --> 00:57:52.150
They can't do that.

00:57:52.150 --> 00:57:54.960
So that's a second development,
not good for the United States

00:57:54.960 --> 00:57:56.010
and Britain.

00:57:56.010 --> 00:57:59.610
Third major development is
that although the world is

00:57:59.610 --> 00:58:03.540
tripolar economically, it's
got one military power.

00:58:03.540 --> 00:58:05.250
And furthermore,
that military power

00:58:05.250 --> 00:58:07.980
is now a lot more free to
use military force than ever

00:58:07.980 --> 00:58:10.300
before because there's
no deterrent any longer.

00:58:10.300 --> 00:58:12.930
Soviet deterrent is gone.

00:58:12.930 --> 00:58:14.640
10, 15 years ago,
the United States

00:58:14.640 --> 00:58:18.390
could not have sent
massive conventional armies

00:58:18.390 --> 00:58:19.200
to the Middle East.

00:58:19.200 --> 00:58:21.670
Now it can do anything
it feels like.

00:58:21.670 --> 00:58:24.581
That gives the US a lot
more freedom to use force.

00:58:24.581 --> 00:58:26.080
Well, you put these
things together,

00:58:26.080 --> 00:58:27.684
and you get a certain picture.

00:58:27.684 --> 00:58:29.350
Everything I say about
the United States

00:58:29.350 --> 00:58:31.705
is also true of England,
but at a much lower scale,

00:58:31.705 --> 00:58:34.840
so I'll put them behind,
keep the lieutenant out

00:58:34.840 --> 00:58:35.410
of the story.

00:58:38.980 --> 00:58:43.720
We have three major
economic powers,

00:58:43.720 --> 00:58:45.810
one major military power.

00:58:45.810 --> 00:58:49.270
That military power doesn't any
longer have the economic base

00:58:49.270 --> 00:58:50.980
to carry off intervention.

00:58:50.980 --> 00:58:53.830
Well, some consequences
follow, and they're drawn.

00:58:53.830 --> 00:58:56.980
So, for example, when Deputy
Secretary of State Lawrence

00:58:56.980 --> 00:58:59.500
Eagleburger was
testifying to Congress

00:58:59.500 --> 00:59:01.940
a couple of weeks ago
about the new world order,

00:59:01.940 --> 00:59:03.550
he said the new
world order will be

00:59:03.550 --> 00:59:05.950
based on what he called
a kind of new invention

00:59:05.950 --> 00:59:07.730
in the art of diplomacy.

00:59:07.730 --> 00:59:09.850
Namely, we carry
out intervention,

00:59:09.850 --> 00:59:11.590
other people pay for it.

00:59:11.590 --> 00:59:14.110
Well, who's going to pay for it?

00:59:14.110 --> 00:59:15.760
The Germans and the
Japanese are not

00:59:15.760 --> 00:59:17.142
that happy about paying for it.

00:59:17.142 --> 00:59:19.600
They're quite happy to see the
United States and England go

00:59:19.600 --> 00:59:22.210
down the tubes, so they
win the Second World War.

00:59:22.210 --> 00:59:23.840
They're not gonna pay for it.

00:59:23.840 --> 00:59:29.770
And there's only one other big
source of capital in the world.

00:59:29.770 --> 00:59:31.447
That's the Gulf oil producers.

00:59:31.447 --> 00:59:33.280
In fact, that's the way
you make the Germans

00:59:33.280 --> 00:59:34.960
and the Japanese pay for it--

00:59:34.960 --> 00:59:37.461
via the oil price
coming back here.

00:59:37.461 --> 00:59:37.960
OK?

00:59:37.960 --> 00:59:42.560
So that's the way
you pay for it.

00:59:42.560 --> 00:59:44.320
However, notice that
there is a shared

00:59:44.320 --> 00:59:47.500
interest with Germany and Japan
and the whole industrial world.

00:59:47.500 --> 00:59:51.080
Namely, somebody has to keep
the third world under control.

00:59:51.080 --> 00:59:52.510
This war against
the world that's

00:59:52.510 --> 00:59:56.070
been going on for the last 500
years, it still has to go on.

00:59:56.070 --> 00:59:59.620
And somebody's got to be
there to make sure it works.

00:59:59.620 --> 01:00:01.690
You try with the
IMF all that stuff.

01:00:01.690 --> 01:00:02.800
That's cost effective.

01:00:02.800 --> 01:00:04.940
But sooner or later,
you need the Marines

01:00:04.940 --> 01:00:06.804
or the B-52s or something.

01:00:06.804 --> 01:00:09.220
And the United States is the
only country that can do it--

01:00:09.220 --> 01:00:12.370
Britain secondarily, but
primarily the United States.

01:00:12.370 --> 01:00:15.820
So somebody has to
play the enforcer role.

01:00:15.820 --> 01:00:18.100
And that's got to be us.

01:00:18.100 --> 01:00:20.530
And that is now being
openly discussed

01:00:20.530 --> 01:00:22.660
in the international
business press,

01:00:22.660 --> 01:00:24.640
for example, where
it's pointed out

01:00:24.640 --> 01:00:28.480
that the US must play even
more than before what's

01:00:28.480 --> 01:00:30.640
called the mercenary role.

01:00:30.640 --> 01:00:36.160
The United States has to be
the Hessians, the guys who

01:00:36.160 --> 01:00:38.020
keep the third
world under control,

01:00:38.020 --> 01:00:39.670
and other people pay for it.

01:00:39.670 --> 01:00:42.420
If there's a
diplomatic settlement,

01:00:42.420 --> 01:00:45.250
it's of no particular
advantage to the United States.

01:00:45.250 --> 01:00:47.290
If there's a
capitulation to force,

01:00:47.290 --> 01:00:49.960
it's of considerable advantage
to the United States.

01:00:49.960 --> 01:00:54.190
It shows that our comparative
advantage is important.

01:00:54.190 --> 01:00:56.740
Our comparative advantage
is in the use of force,

01:00:56.740 --> 01:00:58.140
certainly not in diplomacy.

01:00:58.140 --> 01:01:00.760
We've always been against
diplomacy, but not even anymore

01:01:00.760 --> 01:01:01.870
economically.

01:01:01.870 --> 01:01:04.660
However, force, we rank supreme.

01:01:04.660 --> 01:01:07.360
If you can show that force
is the crucial device

01:01:07.360 --> 01:01:10.690
for controlling the
world, you're better off.

01:01:10.690 --> 01:01:12.250
And I think that's
another reason why

01:01:12.250 --> 01:01:14.590
you've got two powers
sitting there in the Gulf.

01:01:14.590 --> 01:01:17.500
Incidentally, the oil
producers recognize this

01:01:17.500 --> 01:01:19.900
from their own point of view.

01:01:19.900 --> 01:01:21.640
A couple of weeks ago--

01:01:21.640 --> 01:01:25.060
or days ago, in fact-- there was
an article in the Wall Street

01:01:25.060 --> 01:01:28.182
Journal which quoted a high
Gulf official as saying

01:01:28.182 --> 01:01:29.890
that he didn't see
any reason for his son

01:01:29.890 --> 01:01:33.880
to die in Kuwait because we have
our white slaves to do that.

01:01:33.880 --> 01:01:35.950
He hadn't looked very
carefully at the skin

01:01:35.950 --> 01:01:38.350
color of the mercenaries there.

01:01:38.350 --> 01:01:40.240
And he'd also
forgotten for a moment

01:01:40.240 --> 01:01:42.890
that the guys with the guns
are gonna call the shots.

01:01:42.890 --> 01:01:45.700
And if he gets out of line,
he'll learn that very fast.

01:01:45.700 --> 01:01:48.640
But apart from that,
it's sort of true.

01:01:48.640 --> 01:01:51.040
Last comment-- there are
domestic developments

01:01:51.040 --> 01:01:54.140
in the United States that are
tending in the same direction.

01:01:54.140 --> 01:01:57.290
It's not a big secret that
the so-called infrastructure

01:01:57.290 --> 01:01:58.450
is collapsing.

01:01:58.450 --> 01:02:00.280
The educational
system's collapsing.

01:02:00.280 --> 01:02:02.800
The health system's collapsing.

01:02:02.800 --> 01:02:05.339
The inner cities
are being destroyed.

01:02:05.339 --> 01:02:06.880
You may have seen
the statistics that

01:02:06.880 --> 01:02:08.338
were published a
couple of days ago

01:02:08.338 --> 01:02:10.920
on prison population
in the United States.

01:02:10.920 --> 01:02:13.390
The United States is way
ahead of anybody else

01:02:13.390 --> 01:02:16.300
in per capita prison
population, way ahead

01:02:16.300 --> 01:02:18.160
of South Africa
and Russia, which

01:02:18.160 --> 01:02:19.900
used to be up ahead of us.

01:02:19.900 --> 01:02:22.205
For a black male,
the probability

01:02:22.205 --> 01:02:23.830
of being in prison
in the United States

01:02:23.830 --> 01:02:27.910
is four times as high as in
South Africa under apartheid.

01:02:27.910 --> 01:02:29.170
That tells you something.

01:02:29.170 --> 01:02:33.700
All of this reflects an
internal third world.

01:02:36.930 --> 01:02:37.847
Businesses is worried.

01:02:37.847 --> 01:02:39.971
I mean, they're not worried
because too many people

01:02:39.971 --> 01:02:40.490
are in jail.

01:02:40.490 --> 01:02:43.420
They're worried because half the
jobs in the next decade or so

01:02:43.420 --> 01:02:45.940
have to be filled by
blacks and Hispanics.

01:02:45.940 --> 01:02:51.160
And if you keep them in jail and
in concentration camps called

01:02:51.160 --> 01:02:52.690
cities and so on,
they're not going

01:02:52.690 --> 01:02:55.180
to be able to do any of the
work that you need done.

01:02:55.180 --> 01:02:58.120
So they're expecting a big
shortage of skilled labor.

01:02:58.120 --> 01:03:01.870
And that means that
transnational corporations

01:03:01.870 --> 01:03:03.670
are going to shift
those operations that

01:03:03.670 --> 01:03:06.190
involve skilled labor
overseas to where

01:03:06.190 --> 01:03:10.160
there is skilled labor,
educated populations and so on.

01:03:10.160 --> 01:03:13.090
That means everything from
research and management

01:03:13.090 --> 01:03:16.450
and so on to product
development and guys

01:03:16.450 --> 01:03:20.870
to push buttons on computers
and so on and so forth.

01:03:20.870 --> 01:03:22.910
What happens to
the United States?

01:03:22.910 --> 01:03:27.350
Well, maybe these guys
can't do research,

01:03:27.350 --> 01:03:29.690
but they can truck
around the desert.

01:03:29.690 --> 01:03:31.490
So they can be Hessians.

01:03:31.490 --> 01:03:35.662
That's another factor that's
pressing in the same direction.

01:03:35.662 --> 01:03:37.370
Now, that's pretty
much what the future's

01:03:37.370 --> 01:03:40.310
going to look like if it
goes on the way it does.

01:03:40.310 --> 01:03:42.180
It's not for the usual victims.

01:03:42.180 --> 01:03:43.400
It's like always.

01:03:43.400 --> 01:03:46.170
For the citizens of
the mercenary states,

01:03:46.170 --> 01:03:47.990
it's also not
particularly attractive

01:03:47.990 --> 01:03:48.937
if they let it happen.

01:03:48.937 --> 01:03:49.770
And it might happen.

01:03:49.770 --> 01:03:50.900
That's the way
things are moving.

01:03:50.900 --> 01:03:52.941
That's another thing that's
going on in the Gulf.

01:03:52.941 --> 01:03:55.370
Well, what about policy choices?

01:03:55.370 --> 01:03:58.910
Your policy choices depend
on what your goals are.

01:03:58.910 --> 01:04:02.680
If the goal had been to
get Iraq out of Kuwait

01:04:02.680 --> 01:04:06.680
and to settle, to
reduce regional tensions

01:04:06.680 --> 01:04:11.630
and to move make a move towards
a slightly more decent world,

01:04:11.630 --> 01:04:13.580
you would use
sanctions and diplomacy

01:04:13.580 --> 01:04:16.730
and the peaceful means
required by international law.

01:04:16.730 --> 01:04:22.790
If the goal, however, is to firm
up the mercenary enforcer role

01:04:22.790 --> 01:04:25.460
and to establish the rule
of force in the world--

01:04:25.460 --> 01:04:27.616
if that's the goal,
then the policy choices

01:04:27.616 --> 01:04:29.240
that the Bush
administration has picked

01:04:29.240 --> 01:04:31.652
have a certain
chilling logic to them.

01:04:31.652 --> 01:04:32.636
Thanks.

01:04:32.636 --> 01:04:35.096
[APPLAUSE]

01:04:57.250 --> 01:04:58.840
PRESENTER: Thank
you very much, Noam.

01:04:58.840 --> 01:05:01.610
Just for your information,
I'd like everybody in 10250

01:05:01.610 --> 01:05:04.480
to realize there are
actually more people that

01:05:04.480 --> 01:05:07.420
are outside of 10250
who are watching

01:05:07.420 --> 01:05:08.920
this event this evening.

01:05:08.920 --> 01:05:12.250
And for those who are outside
and are still concerned about

01:05:12.250 --> 01:05:14.830
whether they can be able to
make the necessary questions

01:05:14.830 --> 01:05:18.070
or comments, we will allow
these to be written down.

01:05:18.070 --> 01:05:20.350
And these can be
passed to the usher,

01:05:20.350 --> 01:05:22.480
and then will be brought
here and randomly selected

01:05:22.480 --> 01:05:26.040
for anyone who's outside 10250.

01:05:26.040 --> 01:05:28.540
The second announcement I'd
like to make before getting on

01:05:28.540 --> 01:05:30.250
with our second
speaker is that we

01:05:30.250 --> 01:05:32.470
have the specific
list of events that

01:05:32.470 --> 01:05:34.960
will be taking place in the
peace center this evening.

01:05:34.960 --> 01:05:36.932
And Rich Cohen will
read those out.

01:05:41.767 --> 01:05:42.350
COHEN: Thanks.

01:05:42.350 --> 01:05:45.020
This is a continuation in
some cases of the workshops

01:05:45.020 --> 01:05:46.760
that took place yesterday.

01:05:46.760 --> 01:05:49.590
There will be a table on the
third floor of the student

01:05:49.590 --> 01:05:52.910
center when the group moves
over to the student center

01:05:52.910 --> 01:05:56.450
after the beginning of the
question and answer session.

01:05:56.450 --> 01:05:57.950
The question and
answer session will

01:05:57.950 --> 01:06:00.500
continue in the student center.

01:06:00.500 --> 01:06:03.830
And sometime toward
the beginning

01:06:03.830 --> 01:06:05.960
to the middle of that
question and answer session,

01:06:05.960 --> 01:06:08.810
people will break up into these
working groups, organizing

01:06:08.810 --> 01:06:09.890
groups.

01:06:09.890 --> 01:06:12.470
There will be three
small group discussions,

01:06:12.470 --> 01:06:15.740
one led by Geetha from MIT,
one led by David from MIT,

01:06:15.740 --> 01:06:19.160
one led by Marcy from
Brandeis, probably

01:06:19.160 --> 01:06:22.280
in the private dining
rooms in the third floor.

01:06:22.280 --> 01:06:24.590
The gender gap working
group by real men

01:06:24.590 --> 01:06:27.230
of Boston and ecological
transportation group

01:06:27.230 --> 01:06:29.036
will not be meeting tonight.

01:06:29.036 --> 01:06:30.410
But there will be
a sign-up sheet

01:06:30.410 --> 01:06:33.860
for those activities,
a sign-up sheet

01:06:33.860 --> 01:06:36.974
so that you can get connected
with the next meeting

01:06:36.974 --> 01:06:37.640
of those groups.

01:06:37.640 --> 01:06:39.770
The ecological group
is meeting Friday.

01:06:39.770 --> 01:06:43.370
The newspaper creation workshop
of a daily anti-war organizing

01:06:43.370 --> 01:06:47.780
newspaper, that will take
place with Steve Penn from MIT.

01:06:47.780 --> 01:06:51.900
Political art and painting,
Marianne Donnelly from MIT.

01:06:55.900 --> 01:06:58.674
Political organizing,
that will be me.

01:06:58.674 --> 01:07:00.840
And that will be combined
with some other people who

01:07:00.840 --> 01:07:04.470
are working on the
direct action group.

01:07:04.470 --> 01:07:11.170
The letter writing and computer
communications task force,

01:07:11.170 --> 01:07:14.060
Erica from Brandeis
and John from Harvard.

01:07:19.207 --> 01:07:21.790
The direct action meeting will
be announced a little bit later

01:07:21.790 --> 01:07:22.810
when we get over there.

01:07:22.810 --> 01:07:24.610
And then there's going
to be an MIT caucus.

01:07:24.610 --> 01:07:27.700
Not all the people who
are here are from MIT.

01:07:27.700 --> 01:07:31.572
But those of you who are, Penlow
will have a meeting over there.

01:07:31.572 --> 01:07:33.280
We're still here in
this room, everybody.

01:07:33.280 --> 01:07:34.580
We're not going over there yet.

01:07:34.580 --> 01:07:36.580
We still have one more
speaker before we go over

01:07:36.580 --> 01:07:38.380
to the student center.

01:07:38.380 --> 01:07:39.940
And I'll introduce
Michael Albert.

01:07:39.940 --> 01:07:43.596
And here, [INAUDIBLE].

01:07:43.596 --> 01:07:45.470
PRESENTER: Our second
speaker for the evening

01:07:45.470 --> 01:07:48.800
is perhaps probably the
most notorious student

01:07:48.800 --> 01:07:50.490
organizer from MIT.

01:07:50.490 --> 01:07:53.990
He was actually at one point the
MIT Undergraduate Association

01:07:53.990 --> 01:07:54.920
president.

01:07:54.920 --> 01:07:59.630
He cofounded the South End
Press and the Z Magazine.

01:07:59.630 --> 01:08:02.611
And here he is, Michael Albert.

01:08:02.611 --> 01:08:05.497
[APPLAUSE]

01:08:14.650 --> 01:08:16.930
ALBERT: When I came
here 25 years ago,

01:08:16.930 --> 01:08:20.450
it was to be a physicist.

01:08:20.450 --> 01:08:21.370
I loved it.

01:08:21.370 --> 01:08:22.370
I was pretty good at it.

01:08:22.370 --> 01:08:24.140
I was comfortable with it.

01:08:24.140 --> 01:08:27.050
This was probably the best place
in the country, or at least one

01:08:27.050 --> 01:08:28.466
of the best places
in the country,

01:08:28.466 --> 01:08:31.590
to become educated
as a physicist.

01:08:31.590 --> 01:08:37.569
When I left here, I had been
educated to be something else.

01:08:37.569 --> 01:08:41.189
I was a person instead
of a physicist.

01:08:41.189 --> 01:08:44.319
I was just a feeling,
thinking person.

01:08:44.319 --> 01:08:46.375
I wound up a revolutionary.

01:08:46.375 --> 01:08:47.500
That's what I left here as.

01:08:47.500 --> 01:08:49.000
That's what I was taught to be.

01:08:49.000 --> 01:08:54.100
I was taught by Noam and by the
social movements of the time.

01:08:54.100 --> 01:08:55.899
A revolutionary
meaning a person who

01:08:55.899 --> 01:09:00.196
felt that the reason why we
had the atrocity of Vietnam,

01:09:00.196 --> 01:09:02.529
the reason why we had the
atrocity that the civil rights

01:09:02.529 --> 01:09:05.028
movement confronted and that
the women's movement confronted

01:09:05.028 --> 01:09:08.122
and that the other movements
of that time confronted,

01:09:08.122 --> 01:09:09.580
was because of the
basic underlying

01:09:09.580 --> 01:09:13.220
institutions of our society and
that they had to be changed.

01:09:13.220 --> 01:09:16.792
The reason I was
notorious while I was here

01:09:16.792 --> 01:09:18.250
is because of my
response to things

01:09:18.250 --> 01:09:21.882
that went on in this
institution at that time.

01:09:21.882 --> 01:09:23.590
And there are undoubtedly
parallel things

01:09:23.590 --> 01:09:25.300
that go on today.

01:09:25.300 --> 01:09:28.000
One of those
things, for example,

01:09:28.000 --> 01:09:30.689
was a project to
stabilize helicopters.

01:09:30.689 --> 01:09:33.510
It was ostensibly to
stabilize helicopters

01:09:33.510 --> 01:09:36.260
so that they could
fly over Detroit

01:09:36.260 --> 01:09:40.170
and evaluate traffic patterns.

01:09:40.170 --> 01:09:43.920
When we broke onto the airport
where those helicopters were

01:09:43.920 --> 01:09:46.620
and photographed them and came
back with the photographs,

01:09:46.620 --> 01:09:49.380
we were able to prove what
we said all along, which

01:09:49.380 --> 01:09:51.569
was that the helicopters
were gunships

01:09:51.569 --> 01:09:53.399
and that the
stabilization process was

01:09:53.399 --> 01:09:57.570
to be able to shoot the peasants
in the fields in Vietnam

01:09:57.570 --> 01:10:00.100
more accurately.

01:10:00.100 --> 01:10:04.150
Jesse said studying more,
know more last night.

01:10:04.150 --> 01:10:05.810
And we tried to do that.

01:10:05.810 --> 01:10:09.250
Another project that was going
on here at MIT at the time

01:10:09.250 --> 01:10:12.310
was to develop a radar
system, a radar system that

01:10:12.310 --> 01:10:14.740
could guide bombs so that
the bombs would literally

01:10:14.740 --> 01:10:16.450
be smart and find
their target better.

01:10:16.450 --> 01:10:18.010
We didn't know about that.

01:10:18.010 --> 01:10:19.840
We found out about it later.

01:10:19.840 --> 01:10:21.970
And those smart
bombs were a part

01:10:21.970 --> 01:10:26.340
of the worst bombing run in the
Vietnam War at its conclusion.

01:10:26.340 --> 01:10:31.150
Other projects included
ideological projects associated

01:10:31.150 --> 01:10:33.740
with justifying the war.

01:10:33.740 --> 01:10:36.280
One of the faculty people
here, Ithiel de Sola Pool,

01:10:36.280 --> 01:10:40.630
would regularly interrogate
Vietnamese prisoners.

01:10:40.630 --> 01:10:43.870
My response to all
of this and much more

01:10:43.870 --> 01:10:47.501
was a great deal of anger,
anger which you can probably

01:10:47.501 --> 01:10:48.750
already perceive here tonight.

01:10:51.280 --> 01:10:54.490
And at that time, I
allowed that anger to show.

01:10:54.490 --> 01:10:56.140
I expressed it.

01:10:56.140 --> 01:10:58.770
I screamed it when need be.

01:10:58.770 --> 01:11:00.490
And I demonstrated with it.

01:11:00.490 --> 01:11:02.370
And I was considered
moderately insane,

01:11:02.370 --> 01:11:05.910
and it made me notorious.

01:11:05.910 --> 01:11:11.551
And in the 25 years since then,
I've kept that anger down.

01:11:11.551 --> 01:11:13.300
You know how when you
walk down the street

01:11:13.300 --> 01:11:16.810
over on Boylston Street, and you
see somebody grabbing food out

01:11:16.810 --> 01:11:20.110
of a garbage can, or somebody
asks you for a quarter

01:11:20.110 --> 01:11:22.900
and they're in pretty
decrepit shape,

01:11:22.900 --> 01:11:27.500
and you try and keep going,
and you try and get by it,

01:11:27.500 --> 01:11:30.280
and you close yourself
off to it a little bit.

01:11:30.280 --> 01:11:32.450
Well, for the 25 years
between then and now,

01:11:32.450 --> 01:11:34.340
I've been a revolutionary.

01:11:34.340 --> 01:11:35.720
I've been an activist.

01:11:35.720 --> 01:11:38.540
I've been involved all
the time with addressing

01:11:38.540 --> 01:11:39.440
the same questions.

01:11:39.440 --> 01:11:44.110
But I've also
closed off a little

01:11:44.110 --> 01:11:46.180
to the emotions and the
anger and the energy

01:11:46.180 --> 01:11:48.610
that you feel because
of those phenomena.

01:11:48.610 --> 01:11:51.250
And the reason for that is
because of the relative absence

01:11:51.250 --> 01:11:54.100
of large social movements
which were present

01:11:54.100 --> 01:11:56.850
when I was here at MIT
and which gave an outlet.

01:11:56.850 --> 01:11:58.870
And the reason I'm in
quite a bit of turmoil

01:11:58.870 --> 01:12:01.240
tonight is because
some of those shields

01:12:01.240 --> 01:12:04.057
are starting to come down.

01:12:04.057 --> 01:12:05.890
And that's because, for
example, today there

01:12:05.890 --> 01:12:07.990
were 50,000 people on
the streets of Seattle

01:12:07.990 --> 01:12:09.743
protesting what's going on.

01:12:09.743 --> 01:12:12.158
[CHEERING]

01:12:17.970 --> 01:12:21.840
It's because, if the reports
I'm hearing are correct,

01:12:21.840 --> 01:12:24.840
there were 3,000 or
4,000 people occupying

01:12:24.840 --> 01:12:27.070
the main postal center
in the city of Chicago.

01:12:27.070 --> 01:12:29.545
[APPLAUSE]

01:12:33.020 --> 01:12:37.730
What I want to do tonight is to
try and talk about the broader

01:12:37.730 --> 01:12:41.230
context and the
causes and what we

01:12:41.230 --> 01:12:44.520
can do about it
with the intention

01:12:44.520 --> 01:12:46.770
that in Boston in
the coming days,

01:12:46.770 --> 01:12:49.530
we'll have the militants
of those 3,000 people

01:12:49.530 --> 01:12:52.050
who are in the post
office in Chicago

01:12:52.050 --> 01:12:55.740
and the numbers of the
50,000 or 75,000 people

01:12:55.740 --> 01:12:59.100
who are in the streets
of Seattle marching.

01:12:59.100 --> 01:13:01.980
And we will be able to
have an impact on this city

01:13:01.980 --> 01:13:04.050
and on the nation
in that manner.

01:13:07.400 --> 01:13:10.010
For me, what's
happening right now,

01:13:10.010 --> 01:13:14.700
as bad as it is, as
unbelievably surreal

01:13:14.700 --> 01:13:17.610
as it is with this
deadline, is really

01:13:17.610 --> 01:13:22.980
just one part of a phenomenon
that exists all the time.

01:13:22.980 --> 01:13:26.860
And it's the war of
the developed West,

01:13:26.860 --> 01:13:30.156
basically the United States
on the whole third world.

01:13:33.620 --> 01:13:36.560
And that's the thing that
I turned off my sensitivity

01:13:36.560 --> 01:13:38.790
to a little bit
in those 25 years,

01:13:38.790 --> 01:13:41.660
and which roused me so
much then and is again.

01:13:41.660 --> 01:13:45.260
I'm going to have little trouble
reading this, but I will.

01:13:45.260 --> 01:13:50.780
It is an attempt to
try and encapsulate

01:13:50.780 --> 01:13:53.300
the scale and the
magnitude of the crimes

01:13:53.300 --> 01:13:58.700
that Noam has been documenting
for the last 25 years.

01:13:58.700 --> 01:14:01.840
I, over the course of that
time, have been Noam's publisher

01:14:01.840 --> 01:14:03.440
and have not only
read his words,

01:14:03.440 --> 01:14:06.840
but have often typeset
them and laid them out.

01:14:06.840 --> 01:14:10.410
And the process often
leaves me throwing rocks

01:14:10.410 --> 01:14:12.150
at the sides of buildings--

01:14:12.150 --> 01:14:13.170
my own building.

01:14:13.170 --> 01:14:16.020
Regrettably, there's no social
movement powerful enough

01:14:16.020 --> 01:14:20.390
during that period of time to
channel that kind of energy.

01:14:20.390 --> 01:14:24.290
Recently, I wrote something
to try and encapsulate

01:14:24.290 --> 01:14:27.150
the scale of the crimes that
I feel go on in the world

01:14:27.150 --> 01:14:29.060
and that I feel we
have to address.

01:14:29.060 --> 01:14:31.550
Suppose a hypothetical
god got tired of what

01:14:31.550 --> 01:14:35.390
we humans do to one another and
decided that from January 1,

01:14:35.390 --> 01:14:40.940
1999 onward, all corpses
created by violence or neglect

01:14:40.940 --> 01:14:45.290
anywhere in the free world
would cease to decompose.

01:14:45.290 --> 01:14:48.710
Anyone dying for want of
food or medicine, anyone

01:14:48.710 --> 01:14:52.190
hung or garroted to death,
shot or beaten to death,

01:14:52.190 --> 01:14:56.735
raped or bombed to death, anyone
dying unjustly and inhumanely

01:14:56.735 --> 01:15:00.140
in countries of the free
world would as a corpse

01:15:00.140 --> 01:15:03.279
persist without decomposing.

01:15:03.279 --> 01:15:05.320
And the permanent corpse
would then automatically

01:15:05.320 --> 01:15:07.870
enter a glass-walled
cattle car attached

01:15:07.870 --> 01:15:10.900
to an ethereal train traveling
across the United States,

01:15:10.900 --> 01:15:13.850
state by state, never stopping.

01:15:13.850 --> 01:15:17.030
One by one, the corpses would
be loaded on the killing train.

01:15:17.030 --> 01:15:19.790
And after every 1,000
corpses piled in,

01:15:19.790 --> 01:15:23.270
a new car would hitch up
and begin filling also.

01:15:23.270 --> 01:15:26.570
Mile after mile, the killing
train would roll along,

01:15:26.570 --> 01:15:29.870
each corpse viewed through
walls of transparent glass,

01:15:29.870 --> 01:15:35.440
200 new corpses a minute, one
new car every five minutes,

01:15:35.440 --> 01:15:36.880
day and night, without pause.

01:15:36.880 --> 01:15:40.720
By the end of 1991,
on its first birthday,

01:15:40.720 --> 01:15:44.810
the killing train would
measure over 2,000 miles long.

01:15:44.810 --> 01:15:46.230
Traveling at 20
miles an hour, it

01:15:46.230 --> 01:15:50.190
would take about five days to
pass through an intersection.

01:15:50.190 --> 01:15:53.470
By the year 2000, assuming
no dramatic change

01:15:53.470 --> 01:15:55.960
in institutions and
behavior, the train

01:15:55.960 --> 01:15:59.559
would stretch from coast
to coast seven times.

01:15:59.559 --> 01:16:02.100
It would take about six weeks
from the time its engine passed

01:16:02.100 --> 01:16:04.058
the Statue of Liberty to
when its caboose would

01:16:04.058 --> 01:16:08.490
go by, full of corpses,
preventable deaths, every death

01:16:08.490 --> 01:16:11.010
in there preventable.

01:16:11.010 --> 01:16:12.980
Think how young
children sometimes

01:16:12.980 --> 01:16:14.840
point to a picture in
a book or a magazine

01:16:14.840 --> 01:16:17.630
and ask for an explanation.

01:16:17.630 --> 01:16:22.640
Tell me about a tree, a car,
a boat, a train, a big train,

01:16:22.640 --> 01:16:23.740
killing train.

01:16:23.740 --> 01:16:26.589
Try and answer that.

01:16:26.589 --> 01:16:28.880
Think about the pain that
radiates from the Vietnam War

01:16:28.880 --> 01:16:34.240
monument with its 50,000 names.

01:16:34.240 --> 01:16:36.340
Imagine a lost
opportunity and lost love

01:16:36.340 --> 01:16:37.930
and the network of
negative influences

01:16:37.930 --> 01:16:40.570
that radiate from the
unnecessary deaths engraved

01:16:40.570 --> 01:16:42.710
on that monument.

01:16:42.710 --> 01:16:44.629
Now think about
the killing train,

01:16:44.629 --> 01:16:46.670
stretching from coast to
coast and back and forth

01:16:46.670 --> 01:16:49.170
and back and forth
and back and forth.

01:16:49.170 --> 01:16:52.170
Consider its impact, not
only on those on board,

01:16:52.170 --> 01:16:54.060
but on every person that
any of those corpses

01:16:54.060 --> 01:16:56.309
ever loved or would have
loved, fed or would have fed,

01:16:56.309 --> 01:16:58.760
taught or would have taught.

01:16:58.760 --> 01:17:01.150
Who rides the killing train?

01:17:01.150 --> 01:17:05.107
Citizens of the third world,
selling their organs for food,

01:17:05.107 --> 01:17:06.940
selling their babies
to save their families,

01:17:06.940 --> 01:17:10.180
suffering disappearances,
diseases, starvation,

01:17:10.180 --> 01:17:11.260
mass murder.

01:17:11.260 --> 01:17:13.630
They live in Brazil, the
Philippines, El Salvador, South

01:17:13.630 --> 01:17:16.850
Africa, and New York City.

01:17:16.850 --> 01:17:18.920
They are headed for the
killing train every day,

01:17:18.920 --> 01:17:21.690
millions of them.

01:17:21.690 --> 01:17:23.930
When 10 million
children die yearly

01:17:23.930 --> 01:17:27.050
for lack of basic medical
care that the US could provide

01:17:27.050 --> 01:17:29.210
at almost no cost in
countries whose economies

01:17:29.210 --> 01:17:31.870
Exxon and the Bank of
America have looted,

01:17:31.870 --> 01:17:35.320
what can you call it
other than mass murder?

01:17:35.320 --> 01:17:36.880
Bloated, diseased
bodies are victims

01:17:36.880 --> 01:17:39.760
of murder just as surely as
bullet-riddled bodies tossed

01:17:39.760 --> 01:17:42.240
into rivers by death squads.

01:17:42.240 --> 01:17:43.800
Denying medicine
is no less criminal

01:17:43.800 --> 01:17:46.800
than supplying torture rocks
and stealing resources.

01:17:49.900 --> 01:17:52.720
That's the background of
international relations,

01:17:52.720 --> 01:17:57.009
at least as I see it, that
made me revolutionary.

01:18:03.840 --> 01:18:06.310
Why does Bush want war?

01:18:06.310 --> 01:18:11.750
It seems to me that Bush
wants war, partly, minimally--

01:18:11.750 --> 01:18:13.682
I suppose I should
say it's obvious

01:18:13.682 --> 01:18:15.140
that he doesn't
want war because we

01:18:15.140 --> 01:18:19.430
oppose violation of
national sovereignty,

01:18:19.430 --> 01:18:20.900
as Noam made clear.

01:18:20.900 --> 01:18:22.920
If we oppose violation
of national sovereignty,

01:18:22.920 --> 01:18:25.329
we wouldn't support the
Israelis in their violation

01:18:25.329 --> 01:18:26.370
of Lebanon's sovereignty.

01:18:26.370 --> 01:18:29.146
We wouldn't support Syria
in their violation of it.

01:18:29.146 --> 01:18:30.270
We wouldn't support Turkey.

01:18:30.270 --> 01:18:33.780
We wouldn't support
the United States

01:18:33.780 --> 01:18:35.790
in its violation of
Panama's sovereignty,

01:18:35.790 --> 01:18:40.250
of Grenada's sovereignty, of
South Africa, of many countries

01:18:40.250 --> 01:18:41.220
throughout the world.

01:18:41.220 --> 01:18:43.000
That's not the issue.

01:18:43.000 --> 01:18:45.520
Price of oil isn't the issue.

01:18:45.520 --> 01:18:48.790
The control of the price
of oil is a serious issue.

01:18:48.790 --> 01:18:50.770
Insofar as the United
States is no longer

01:18:50.770 --> 01:18:55.180
a viable and a serious
economic force in the world

01:18:55.180 --> 01:18:58.030
as it once was,
the control of oil

01:18:58.030 --> 01:19:00.310
is one economic card
that we can hold,

01:19:00.310 --> 01:19:02.160
and we want to keep holding it.

01:19:02.160 --> 01:19:03.660
That's a powerful reason.

01:19:03.660 --> 01:19:06.150
But it's not sufficient to
explain the drive toward war.

01:19:06.150 --> 01:19:07.900
It's only sufficient
to explain the desire

01:19:07.900 --> 01:19:10.270
to get Hussein out of Kuwait.

01:19:10.270 --> 01:19:14.620
I think the drive toward war
is explained by something else,

01:19:14.620 --> 01:19:17.590
which Noam also brought up.

01:19:17.590 --> 01:19:21.980
And that's the desire,
as he described,

01:19:21.980 --> 01:19:24.680
to have a new world order
in which American might is

01:19:24.680 --> 01:19:27.830
a function no longer of
in part economic power

01:19:27.830 --> 01:19:30.541
and in part military power,
but basically entirely military

01:19:30.541 --> 01:19:31.040
power.

01:19:31.040 --> 01:19:33.590
For those who are old
enough, Paladin, Have Gun--

01:19:33.590 --> 01:19:34.910
Will Travel.

01:19:34.910 --> 01:19:36.620
The difference is that
the people who pay

01:19:36.620 --> 01:19:38.360
will pay because we
have the gun also.

01:19:38.360 --> 01:19:41.528
We will force them to pay.

01:19:41.528 --> 01:19:43.436
That's the future
Bush has in mind.

01:19:48.550 --> 01:19:49.840
What do people do about this?

01:19:49.840 --> 01:19:51.790
Now, I know that you all
know that what you do

01:19:51.790 --> 01:19:52.720
is you protest.

01:19:52.720 --> 01:19:54.070
What you do is you demonstrate.

01:19:54.070 --> 01:19:55.900
What you do is you
march, you rally,

01:19:55.900 --> 01:19:59.050
you do civil disobedience, you
educate, you hold teach-ins,

01:19:59.050 --> 01:20:00.700
you talk to people.

01:20:00.700 --> 01:20:04.320
I'd like to provide what I hope
will be some useful lessons.

01:20:04.320 --> 01:20:06.530
They're very simple.

01:20:06.530 --> 01:20:09.320
They're almost transparently
obvious, I think.

01:20:09.320 --> 01:20:11.360
But they're hard
to keep in mind.

01:20:11.360 --> 01:20:13.770
And they can inform our
activity and give us

01:20:13.770 --> 01:20:18.280
the strength that can help
us in the period ahead.

01:20:18.280 --> 01:20:21.850
The first lesson, the
most trivial and obvious,

01:20:21.850 --> 01:20:23.530
and in many ways
the most important

01:20:23.530 --> 01:20:27.540
and the hardest to keep in
mind, is that organizing works.

01:20:27.540 --> 01:20:28.840
It really does work.

01:20:28.840 --> 01:20:31.090
[APPLAUSE]

01:20:34.390 --> 01:20:38.860
When I came here in 1965, there
were anti-war demonstrations

01:20:38.860 --> 01:20:39.400
in Boston.

01:20:39.400 --> 01:20:40.070
They were small.

01:20:40.070 --> 01:20:41.278
They were held at the Common.

01:20:41.278 --> 01:20:44.410
They were minuscule compared
to what's happening right now.

01:20:44.410 --> 01:20:47.430
And MIT actually participated,
to a small extent.

01:20:47.430 --> 01:20:49.960
A small number of MIT students
would go over and throw rocks

01:20:49.960 --> 01:20:52.200
at the demonstrators.

01:20:52.200 --> 01:20:53.190
No lie.

01:20:53.190 --> 01:20:56.250
That was about the extent
of MIT student participation

01:20:56.250 --> 01:21:01.320
in anti-war demonstrations
in the year 1964 and 1965.

01:21:01.320 --> 01:21:04.690
In 1969, as mentioned, I was
elected the Undergraduate

01:21:04.690 --> 01:21:06.970
Association President at MIT.

01:21:06.970 --> 01:21:12.420
The platform included no more
war research, no more grades

01:21:12.420 --> 01:21:17.770
at MIT, no more
requirements, open admissions

01:21:17.770 --> 01:21:20.320
for working-class
people and people

01:21:20.320 --> 01:21:24.150
of color, $100,000
indemnity payment

01:21:24.150 --> 01:21:27.840
to the Black Panther Party.

01:21:27.840 --> 01:21:30.000
There were about 10
more similar planks

01:21:30.000 --> 01:21:32.700
on the platform on which I was
elected in an election which

01:21:32.700 --> 01:21:36.300
I suspect until this time
is still the one that

01:21:36.300 --> 01:21:38.760
involved more students
than any other,

01:21:38.760 --> 01:21:40.560
probably, in MIT's history.

01:21:40.560 --> 01:21:42.450
It certainly involved
the administration

01:21:42.450 --> 01:21:43.890
in a significant
way, campaigning

01:21:43.890 --> 01:21:46.690
for the other candidates.

01:21:46.690 --> 01:21:50.080
The difference
between 1965 and 1969

01:21:50.080 --> 01:21:52.720
was that in the interim,
students on this campus,

01:21:52.720 --> 01:21:55.690
like the ones who set up the
Peace and Justice Center--

01:21:55.690 --> 01:21:58.510
in fact, identical to them--

01:21:58.510 --> 01:22:04.330
had gone into the dorms and
talked one-to-one with people.

01:22:04.330 --> 01:22:06.940
They had held meetings
with people in which people

01:22:06.940 --> 01:22:11.470
were entreated and helped
to express what they really

01:22:11.470 --> 01:22:13.700
felt about what was going on.

01:22:13.700 --> 01:22:16.780
We'll come a bit later to what,
in fact, they really did feel

01:22:16.780 --> 01:22:18.880
and what our response was.

01:22:18.880 --> 01:22:20.530
We did all sorts
of demonstrations.

01:22:20.530 --> 01:22:22.930
We painted the walls
of MIT at other schools

01:22:22.930 --> 01:22:24.400
that might not be so relevant.

01:22:24.400 --> 01:22:27.860
But at MIT, I'll tell you one
more-- every once in a while,

01:22:27.860 --> 01:22:29.590
I'll do a little
anecdote about MIT.

01:22:29.590 --> 01:22:34.720
MIT produces technocrats.

01:22:34.720 --> 01:22:40.610
Harvard produces ruling class
elite who make decisions.

01:22:40.610 --> 01:22:44.090
BU produces people who
feel middle-level strata.

01:22:44.090 --> 01:22:45.300
I didn't know that at first.

01:22:45.300 --> 01:22:47.150
But when I was here
early on, I went over,

01:22:47.150 --> 01:22:50.860
and I sat in on a
few classes at BU.

01:22:50.860 --> 01:22:52.390
And I was astounded.

01:22:52.390 --> 01:22:54.940
I was astounded by
their magnitude,

01:22:54.940 --> 01:22:57.790
by the absolute lack
of participation

01:22:57.790 --> 01:23:01.030
of anybody in anything having to
do with education, any student,

01:23:01.030 --> 01:23:05.590
by the utter dissociation of
the faculty from the students,

01:23:05.590 --> 01:23:07.750
by a lot of characteristics.

01:23:07.750 --> 01:23:09.610
Then I went up to Harvard.

01:23:09.610 --> 01:23:11.600
There, I was blown away.

01:23:11.600 --> 01:23:14.080
I was blown away by the fact
that they had plush rugs

01:23:14.080 --> 01:23:18.700
in the classrooms, that they
lived in suites that were

01:23:18.700 --> 01:23:22.972
small-scale, miniature
models of boardrooms,

01:23:22.972 --> 01:23:24.055
that they were literally--

01:23:24.055 --> 01:23:27.170
[LAUGHTER]

01:23:27.170 --> 01:23:29.830
--that everything
about the school

01:23:29.830 --> 01:23:33.100
was structured in such
a way as to produce

01:23:33.100 --> 01:23:35.590
a certain kind of product.

01:23:35.590 --> 01:23:41.790
Not a person, mind you, but
an elite CEO, politician,

01:23:41.790 --> 01:23:43.150
et cetera.

01:23:43.150 --> 01:23:44.440
Then I came back to MIT.

01:23:44.440 --> 01:23:46.870
Before this, I had no notion
that MIT was anything other

01:23:46.870 --> 01:23:49.430
than a school, good school.

01:23:49.430 --> 01:23:52.750
I came back to MIT,
and I looked around.

01:23:52.750 --> 01:23:56.050
And it was quite a contrast.

01:23:56.050 --> 01:24:00.470
I saw gray walls as
far as you could see.

01:24:00.470 --> 01:24:05.020
I saw faculty who would
come in and write textbooks

01:24:05.020 --> 01:24:06.430
on the wall.

01:24:06.430 --> 01:24:11.412
I saw an utter lack, a
complete absence of emotion,

01:24:11.412 --> 01:24:13.870
such an absence of emotion that
probably the most important

01:24:13.870 --> 01:24:16.750
thing I did as an organizer
was exhibit emotion.

01:24:16.750 --> 01:24:18.160
That's how bad it was.

01:24:18.160 --> 01:24:20.460
No lie.

01:24:20.460 --> 01:24:23.340
And then I began
to understand why.

01:24:23.340 --> 01:24:28.140
And what I understood was
it's because this place means

01:24:28.140 --> 01:24:30.290
to produce a certain product.

01:24:30.290 --> 01:24:36.000
And the product is a very, very
well-trained, a very capable,

01:24:36.000 --> 01:24:39.690
a very brilliant, in
many cases, scientist

01:24:39.690 --> 01:24:45.240
or engineer who will solve
any problem, any problem,

01:24:45.240 --> 01:24:47.580
so long as it's of some
interest and so long as somebody

01:24:47.580 --> 01:24:49.170
will pay.

01:24:49.170 --> 01:24:52.350
If somebody would pay MIT
graduates to create weapons

01:24:52.350 --> 01:24:56.980
to shoot down B-52s, and it
was an interesting problem,

01:24:56.980 --> 01:24:59.280
they'd rush to it.

01:24:59.280 --> 01:25:01.140
But nobody will pay that.

01:25:01.140 --> 01:25:03.840
And so instead, they create
stabilization programs

01:25:03.840 --> 01:25:07.740
for helicopters and smart bombs
and MIRVs, and all the rest.

01:25:07.740 --> 01:25:12.432
And they do it without so much
as a wrinkle of moral question

01:25:12.432 --> 01:25:14.140
because for them, it's
not a moral issue.

01:25:14.140 --> 01:25:16.020
It's simply a technical,
scientific issue.

01:25:16.020 --> 01:25:17.353
That's what this place produces.

01:25:19.880 --> 01:25:22.720
So the kind of organizing that
we did was relevant to that.

01:25:22.720 --> 01:25:24.370
We did things like
painting the walls.

01:25:24.370 --> 01:25:26.305
We took all the couches,
what few of them

01:25:26.305 --> 01:25:28.430
there were, and put
them in public places

01:25:28.430 --> 01:25:30.555
so people could sit in them
and talk to each other.

01:25:30.555 --> 01:25:31.808
[LAUGHTER]

01:25:34.170 --> 01:25:37.740
We went into classes and we
confronted people loudly,

01:25:37.740 --> 01:25:39.540
exhibiting anger and emotion.

01:25:39.540 --> 01:25:42.240
And we marched, and we held
teach-ins, and we had rallies,

01:25:42.240 --> 01:25:44.760
and we had explanations
and information,

01:25:44.760 --> 01:25:46.600
and then we had occupations.

01:25:46.600 --> 01:25:51.000
And then we had even more
militant demonstrations.

01:25:51.000 --> 01:25:54.570
And in 1969, most of this campus
moved over to the Boston Common

01:25:54.570 --> 01:25:58.397
not to throw rocks at the
demonstration, but to join it.

01:25:58.397 --> 01:25:59.980
So the first lesson
is that organizing

01:25:59.980 --> 01:26:02.140
can move people,
even people who are

01:26:02.140 --> 01:26:03.910
subject to the kind
of socialization

01:26:03.910 --> 01:26:07.370
that this place can generate.

01:26:07.370 --> 01:26:08.900
The second lesson
is that people can

01:26:08.900 --> 01:26:12.200
be moved for the long- or
only for the short-term.

01:26:12.200 --> 01:26:14.660
It's a hard lesson to get to,
although it's, on the face

01:26:14.660 --> 01:26:16.110
of it, very simple.

01:26:16.110 --> 01:26:18.380
Some of you who were there
will remember a May Day

01:26:18.380 --> 01:26:20.545
demonstration held back then.

01:26:20.545 --> 01:26:21.920
This was a
demonstration in which

01:26:21.920 --> 01:26:24.620
people were supposed to go
to Washington to shut down

01:26:24.620 --> 01:26:27.420
the city and end the war.

01:26:27.420 --> 01:26:30.710
The idea was to disrupt
traffic and disrupt

01:26:30.710 --> 01:26:33.450
the functioning of the city.

01:26:33.450 --> 01:26:36.480
The problem was that
the organizing--

01:26:36.480 --> 01:26:37.710
not the conception.

01:26:37.710 --> 01:26:40.480
The conception of going to
Washington and disrupting,

01:26:40.480 --> 01:26:42.420
that was fine.

01:26:42.420 --> 01:26:44.810
The problem was the way
the organizing was done.

01:26:44.810 --> 01:26:47.190
It was an example of
what I like to call

01:26:47.190 --> 01:26:49.830
the apocalyptic organizing.

01:26:49.830 --> 01:26:54.680
People like Rennie Davis,
Tom Hayden, Jerry Rubin--

01:26:54.680 --> 01:26:58.790
as a sidebar, Rennie Davis
wound up an insurance salesman.

01:26:58.790 --> 01:26:59.840
Tom Hayden wound up--

01:27:02.860 --> 01:27:04.720
well, Tom Hayden
at one time thought

01:27:04.720 --> 01:27:08.529
liberals were the lowest
form of human life,

01:27:08.529 --> 01:27:10.070
if he would deign
to call them human.

01:27:10.070 --> 01:27:13.280
And he wound up
barely a liberal.

01:27:13.280 --> 01:27:18.640
And Jerry Rubin is some
kind of a neo-capitalist

01:27:18.640 --> 01:27:19.780
on Wall Street.

01:27:19.780 --> 01:27:22.330
These three folks,
among some others,

01:27:22.330 --> 01:27:24.190
organized for this
May Day demonstration.

01:27:24.190 --> 01:27:27.550
And what they would do is they
would come to places like this.

01:27:27.550 --> 01:27:29.880
And they would speak
to large audiences.

01:27:29.880 --> 01:27:31.307
And they were very
good speakers.

01:27:31.307 --> 01:27:33.390
In fact, Rennie Davis was
perhaps the best speaker

01:27:33.390 --> 01:27:34.056
I've ever heard.

01:27:37.290 --> 01:27:41.240
And they would motivate
powerfully, but often

01:27:41.240 --> 01:27:45.440
with hyperbole, and often
without information,

01:27:45.440 --> 01:27:50.390
and often with a promise that
bore no semblance of reality.

01:27:50.390 --> 01:27:51.950
Come to Washington.

01:27:51.950 --> 01:27:54.920
We'll shut down the city,
and we'll end the war.

01:27:57.700 --> 01:28:01.310
People went to Washington,
disrupted the city,

01:28:01.310 --> 01:28:02.800
but the war went on.

01:28:02.800 --> 01:28:05.510
And when they went home,
having been organized

01:28:05.510 --> 01:28:08.270
without any real fundamental
understanding of the tenacity

01:28:08.270 --> 01:28:12.470
of the war and of the basic
institutions that caused it,

01:28:12.470 --> 01:28:16.220
many of them would become
disgruntled and dismayed

01:28:16.220 --> 01:28:20.960
and lose their commitment.

01:28:20.960 --> 01:28:24.740
Other people, among
them Noam, Leslie Kagan,

01:28:24.740 --> 01:28:28.860
who's currently the chief
organizer, 25 years later,

01:28:28.860 --> 01:28:33.390
of the event on January
26, Dave Dellinger,

01:28:33.390 --> 01:28:35.740
who undoubtedly will
be a speaker there

01:28:35.740 --> 01:28:37.740
and is still doing that,
and many, many others--

01:28:37.740 --> 01:28:39.890
notice all of them
still involved,

01:28:39.890 --> 01:28:41.320
still committed
in the same way--

01:28:41.320 --> 01:28:44.160
would organize in
a different way.

01:28:44.160 --> 01:28:48.750
They would try to motivate
and to arouse and to confront.

01:28:48.750 --> 01:28:51.540
But they would also provide
information and analysis

01:28:51.540 --> 01:28:54.450
and an honest perception
of what was possible

01:28:54.450 --> 01:28:56.360
and what it meant
to build a movement,

01:28:56.360 --> 01:29:00.502
that success was building a
larger movement, that success

01:29:00.502 --> 01:29:02.460
was raising consciousness,
that you didn't have

01:29:02.460 --> 01:29:05.010
to come home, turn on the
TV, and see an end of a war

01:29:05.010 --> 01:29:08.220
to feel success, to feel
that what you were doing

01:29:08.220 --> 01:29:12.540
was part of a process that was
building something worthwhile.

01:29:12.540 --> 01:29:15.960
You can organize for the
short-term and get a big bang,

01:29:15.960 --> 01:29:18.290
or you can organize
for the long-term

01:29:18.290 --> 01:29:21.560
and generally get just as large
an immediate demonstration

01:29:21.560 --> 01:29:23.210
and way more commitment.

01:29:23.210 --> 01:29:26.465
The third lesson is that you can
organize people around symbols

01:29:26.465 --> 01:29:29.810
or around reality.

01:29:29.810 --> 01:29:32.036
At MIT, one of the
things we did is

01:29:32.036 --> 01:29:33.660
we organized around
the instrumentation

01:29:33.660 --> 01:29:36.360
labs, where much of that
research was going on.

01:29:36.360 --> 01:29:39.300
At Princeton, they organized
around a math building--

01:29:39.300 --> 01:29:40.440
I forget the name of it--

01:29:40.440 --> 01:29:42.180
where research was going on.

01:29:42.180 --> 01:29:45.240
At MIT, the culmination of our
work around the instrumentation

01:29:45.240 --> 01:29:48.420
labs, which included a citywide
coalition that came here

01:29:48.420 --> 01:29:51.540
for a demonstration to block
those labs, which were actually

01:29:51.540 --> 01:29:55.830
partly a military installation,
and so there were troops here.

01:29:55.830 --> 01:30:01.644
There were literally people on
top of the buildings with guns

01:30:01.644 --> 01:30:03.060
and all of the
other paraphernalia

01:30:03.060 --> 01:30:03.810
that you'd expect.

01:30:03.810 --> 01:30:07.230
The resolution to this was
that they redefined the I-labs

01:30:07.230 --> 01:30:08.989
to be not on the campus.

01:30:08.989 --> 01:30:10.530
They just called
them something else,

01:30:10.530 --> 01:30:12.392
and they named them after--

01:30:12.392 --> 01:30:13.600
I forget the name of the guy.

01:30:13.600 --> 01:30:15.690
But anyway, they moved
them off the campus.

01:30:15.690 --> 01:30:20.580
And some of the people
who had been organized

01:30:20.580 --> 01:30:23.640
were lost because of that,
because the organizing focused

01:30:23.640 --> 01:30:25.980
so strongly-- not that
it shouldn't have been

01:30:25.980 --> 01:30:30.030
concerned with the I-labs--
but so strongly with the I-labs

01:30:30.030 --> 01:30:31.860
and not enough
with the underlying

01:30:31.860 --> 01:30:34.130
issues and the
underlying structure

01:30:34.130 --> 01:30:35.130
that they could be lost.

01:30:35.130 --> 01:30:38.610
At Princeton, the building's
in the middle of the campus.

01:30:38.610 --> 01:30:41.400
They redefined the
borders of the campus

01:30:41.400 --> 01:30:44.040
so that that plot of ground was
no longer a part of Princeton.

01:30:44.040 --> 01:30:46.110
The thing still sat there.

01:30:46.110 --> 01:30:48.348
It was still functioning
in the exact same way.

01:30:48.348 --> 01:30:49.841
AUDIENCE: What does I stand for?

01:30:49.841 --> 01:30:50.840
ALBERT: Instrumentation.

01:30:50.840 --> 01:30:51.842
Sorry.

01:30:51.842 --> 01:30:54.050
That's where the helicopter
stuff and the smart bombs

01:30:54.050 --> 01:30:55.784
were done.

01:30:55.784 --> 01:30:57.950
So you can organize around
that, or you can organize

01:30:57.950 --> 01:30:59.310
around the basic institutions.

01:30:59.310 --> 01:31:02.680
People can be moved
narrowly or broadly.

01:31:02.680 --> 01:31:04.720
We could go out, and
we can organize now--

01:31:04.720 --> 01:31:08.610
and there's a tremendous desire
to do it, and I feel it, too--

01:31:08.610 --> 01:31:14.660
about war and peace, and
not bother with justice.

01:31:14.660 --> 01:31:17.480
We could skip the
links to racism.

01:31:17.480 --> 01:31:19.640
We could skip the
links to sexism.

01:31:19.640 --> 01:31:22.130
We could skip the
links to poverty.

01:31:22.130 --> 01:31:23.750
We could skip the
links to homophobia.

01:31:23.750 --> 01:31:29.210
We could skip all that
because hey, in two hours,

01:31:29.210 --> 01:31:31.560
we may have bombs
raining from the sky.

01:31:31.560 --> 01:31:33.080
It would be a mistake.

01:31:33.080 --> 01:31:35.570
It is not the way to
organize a movement.

01:31:35.570 --> 01:31:38.750
It is not the way to make the
most powerful statement that

01:31:38.750 --> 01:31:39.792
can be made.

01:31:39.792 --> 01:31:41.750
And it is not the way to
create a movement that

01:31:41.750 --> 01:31:44.170
can sustain itself.

01:31:44.170 --> 01:31:47.492
We're gonna come in a bit to
why I think that's the case.

01:31:51.180 --> 01:31:53.940
Last lesson is that moving
people really does matter.

01:31:56.620 --> 01:32:01.600
If you look back at the history
of the opposition among elites

01:32:01.600 --> 01:32:05.230
to the war-- after
all, if we do get out,

01:32:05.230 --> 01:32:08.920
if we do decide not to start
the war in Iraq, or if we go in

01:32:08.920 --> 01:32:11.740
and then we stop the
war, it will be elites,

01:32:11.740 --> 01:32:14.410
after all, who make
that decision, who put

01:32:14.410 --> 01:32:16.870
their name on that decision.

01:32:16.870 --> 01:32:19.670
So you have to look at
how do they respond?

01:32:19.670 --> 01:32:22.330
What do they respond
to in making decisions

01:32:22.330 --> 01:32:25.330
about war and peace
or, for that matter,

01:32:25.330 --> 01:32:28.260
about any other social issue?

01:32:28.260 --> 01:32:32.280
If you look at the record of
the speeches of the senators

01:32:32.280 --> 01:32:37.540
and the Congresspeople from
the period of the Vietnam War

01:32:37.540 --> 01:32:39.670
and of the CEOs and
lawyers and doctors

01:32:39.670 --> 01:32:42.130
and so on who were
public enough figures

01:32:42.130 --> 01:32:45.790
to go public, when they became
doves, as it was called--

01:32:45.790 --> 01:32:47.830
when they decided they
were against the war--

01:32:47.830 --> 01:32:50.200
you find over and over
again that they give one

01:32:50.200 --> 01:32:51.870
variant or another
of the same speech.

01:32:55.410 --> 01:32:57.150
That speech doesn't
talk about the lives

01:32:57.150 --> 01:32:59.150
of the Vietnamese people.

01:32:59.150 --> 01:33:02.120
It doesn't talk about the
lives of American GIs.

01:33:02.120 --> 01:33:07.010
It does not talk about resources
taken away from the United

01:33:07.010 --> 01:33:10.520
States, which cannot go to
bettering situation here.

01:33:10.520 --> 01:33:12.566
It doesn't mention those things.

01:33:12.566 --> 01:33:13.940
The Congresspeople,
the senators,

01:33:13.940 --> 01:33:16.420
they didn't mention any of that.

01:33:16.420 --> 01:33:17.950
When they changed
sides, when they

01:33:17.950 --> 01:33:20.170
said that they were no
longer against the war,

01:33:20.170 --> 01:33:23.110
they said, our streets
are in turmoil.

01:33:23.110 --> 01:33:25.390
We're losing the
next generation.

01:33:25.390 --> 01:33:28.690
There's the possibility
that all these people who

01:33:28.690 --> 01:33:31.570
are demonstrating are
going to soon challenge

01:33:31.570 --> 01:33:34.630
the basic institutions
in addition to the war.

01:33:34.630 --> 01:33:38.980
What they said was, for me,
the social cost of pursuing

01:33:38.980 --> 01:33:41.260
a policy I want to
pursue-- the war--

01:33:41.260 --> 01:33:42.490
is too high.

01:33:42.490 --> 01:33:44.200
These movements
are raising costs

01:33:44.200 --> 01:33:47.110
that are so high that I can
no longer pursue what I really

01:33:47.110 --> 01:33:48.850
want to pursue.

01:33:48.850 --> 01:33:53.380
George Bush does not give a
shit about dead American GIs.

01:33:53.380 --> 01:33:55.420
He does not care.

01:33:55.420 --> 01:33:57.550
And it's important to
understand that if we're

01:33:57.550 --> 01:33:59.350
going to understand
what we have to do

01:33:59.350 --> 01:34:04.160
to get him and the powers
behind him to react.

01:34:04.160 --> 01:34:05.530
He does not care--

01:34:05.530 --> 01:34:08.260
he doesn't even conceive--

01:34:08.260 --> 01:34:11.525
that there's any
issue to care about

01:34:11.525 --> 01:34:15.350
of dead Iraqis or dead
Jordanians or dead

01:34:15.350 --> 01:34:16.820
Palestinians.

01:34:16.820 --> 01:34:19.830
That isn't even a
concept for him.

01:34:19.830 --> 01:34:24.000
He does not care about
huge sums of money

01:34:24.000 --> 01:34:28.260
going into the military and
not into dealing with racism

01:34:28.260 --> 01:34:30.981
or crumbling school systems
or crumbling infrastructure

01:34:30.981 --> 01:34:32.730
or a lack of housing
in the United States.

01:34:32.730 --> 01:34:34.349
Not only doesn't
he care about that,

01:34:34.349 --> 01:34:36.390
I think that's a significant
part of the purpose.

01:34:39.160 --> 01:34:43.150
He does care about the
possibility of social movements

01:34:43.150 --> 01:34:47.710
challenging capital,
challenging his rule,

01:34:47.710 --> 01:34:49.780
challenging the
two-party system,

01:34:49.780 --> 01:34:52.480
challenging the basic
institutions of patriarchy.

01:34:52.480 --> 01:34:54.790
He cares about no
business as usual.

01:34:54.790 --> 01:34:56.800
He cares about disruption.

01:34:56.800 --> 01:34:58.450
He cares about dissent.

01:34:58.450 --> 01:35:00.460
If he cares about
a dead GI, it's

01:35:00.460 --> 01:35:03.090
because it might
provoke dissent.

01:35:03.090 --> 01:35:03.870
That's all.

01:35:03.870 --> 01:35:06.720
If he cares about
any of these issues,

01:35:06.720 --> 01:35:09.900
it's because it might
provoke dissent.

01:35:09.900 --> 01:35:14.310
That tells us that not
only does organizing work,

01:35:14.310 --> 01:35:16.020
but that the product
of organizing,

01:35:16.020 --> 01:35:21.340
dissent and demonstrations,
also can work.

01:35:21.340 --> 01:35:23.070
They can win.

01:35:23.070 --> 01:35:24.480
They can make change.

01:35:24.480 --> 01:35:25.790
They can change policies.

01:35:25.790 --> 01:35:28.184
That can change basic trends.

01:35:28.184 --> 01:35:30.100
They can change the
consciousness of a nation.

01:35:30.100 --> 01:35:31.724
If you look at this
country now and you

01:35:31.724 --> 01:35:34.390
look at this country at the
start of the Vietnam War,

01:35:34.390 --> 01:35:36.510
you'll see that the
movements in the interim

01:35:36.510 --> 01:35:38.650
have had an unbelievably
powerful effect

01:35:38.650 --> 01:35:40.236
on the psyche of America.

01:35:45.330 --> 01:35:47.684
What we learn from
what they respond to

01:35:47.684 --> 01:35:49.350
is that it's necessary
to have movements

01:35:49.350 --> 01:35:52.590
that have more than one tactic.

01:35:52.590 --> 01:35:55.250
A demonstration in Washington
is a powerful thing.

01:35:55.250 --> 01:35:58.846
It displays large numbers of
people who are dissenting.

01:35:58.846 --> 01:36:00.950
A demonstration in
Washington which

01:36:00.950 --> 01:36:04.180
is accompanied by a civil
disobedience demonstration

01:36:04.180 --> 01:36:06.450
is a more powerful thing.

01:36:06.450 --> 01:36:10.220
It says, not only are
people demonstrating,

01:36:10.220 --> 01:36:15.220
but they are then moving on to
disrupt, to civilly disobey.

01:36:15.220 --> 01:36:18.592
And everybody knows that the
commitment and the involvement

01:36:18.592 --> 01:36:20.050
and the consciousness
change that's

01:36:20.050 --> 01:36:24.250
associated with that leak
means a lasting commitment.

01:36:24.250 --> 01:36:27.310
And so the threat to the
interests that they hold dear

01:36:27.310 --> 01:36:29.730
is that much greater.

01:36:29.730 --> 01:36:33.210
We have to make our dissent
visible and militant.

01:36:33.210 --> 01:36:34.710
We have to have a
movement which has

01:36:34.710 --> 01:36:37.620
a place for people just
moving, just beginning,

01:36:37.620 --> 01:36:40.754
and a place for people whose
anger and whose consciousness

01:36:40.754 --> 01:36:43.170
and whose awareness has gotten
to the point where they are

01:36:43.170 --> 01:36:45.397
ready to do civil disobedience.

01:36:48.905 --> 01:36:50.530
What is it about the
United States that

01:36:50.530 --> 01:36:52.700
makes it so hard to organize?

01:36:52.700 --> 01:36:57.805
I'll pick up the pace, but I
do want to address this a bit.

01:36:57.805 --> 01:37:00.160
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

01:37:00.160 --> 01:37:02.540
ALBERT: It's part of it.

01:37:02.540 --> 01:37:08.050
We live in a country that has
an economy that's capitalist.

01:37:08.050 --> 01:37:09.640
It's profit-oriented.

01:37:09.640 --> 01:37:10.750
And has private ownership.

01:37:10.750 --> 01:37:12.110
It has competitive markets.

01:37:12.110 --> 01:37:14.964
It has 30 billionaires and
31 million poor people.

01:37:18.270 --> 01:37:20.940
We live in a country
that has a culture that's

01:37:20.940 --> 01:37:26.510
racist, ethnocentric,
violent, exclusionary.

01:37:26.510 --> 01:37:31.070
We live in a country that
has kinship arrangements that

01:37:31.070 --> 01:37:34.460
are sexist, patriarchal,
homophobic, political

01:37:34.460 --> 01:37:37.160
arrangements that call
themselves democratic

01:37:37.160 --> 01:37:41.220
but are actually money-driven
and media-dominated.

01:37:41.220 --> 01:37:43.850
And I thought
democratic in the sense

01:37:43.850 --> 01:37:47.060
that elites had a
degree of democracy.

01:37:47.060 --> 01:37:49.700
But I'm even beginning
to wonder about that now,

01:37:49.700 --> 01:37:52.340
given Bush's power
and his capability

01:37:52.340 --> 01:37:56.220
to override elite dissent,
of which there is plenty.

01:37:56.220 --> 01:37:58.770
These institutions create
hierarchies among us--

01:37:58.770 --> 01:38:02.150
class hierarchies, gender
hierarchies, race hierarchies.

01:38:02.150 --> 01:38:03.990
And they create
consciousnesses that

01:38:03.990 --> 01:38:06.940
make it hard for us to act--

01:38:06.940 --> 01:38:09.910
passivity, cynicism, a
belief that nothing better

01:38:09.910 --> 01:38:12.610
is possible.

01:38:12.610 --> 01:38:15.690
They also create
an intelligentsia

01:38:15.690 --> 01:38:19.890
that's bought off, that's more
interested in its well-being,

01:38:19.890 --> 01:38:21.915
financial well-being,
than in the truth.

01:38:21.915 --> 01:38:25.200
A bought media,
powerful police--

01:38:25.200 --> 01:38:28.660
these things make
organizing difficult.

01:38:28.660 --> 01:38:32.594
These sets of institutions breed
the international relations,

01:38:32.594 --> 01:38:34.260
the international
outcomes, I described.

01:38:34.260 --> 01:38:37.210
They also breed
domestic problems.

01:38:37.210 --> 01:38:40.660
We're the richest country
in the world, far and away.

01:38:40.660 --> 01:38:43.420
We have 31 million poor people.

01:38:43.420 --> 01:38:45.850
There are 3 million people
in the United States homeless

01:38:45.850 --> 01:38:48.370
who eat garbage-can dinners
and sleep in alleys.

01:38:48.370 --> 01:38:50.620
The richest country
in the world.

01:38:50.620 --> 01:38:54.070
40% of all Latino children
and 50% of all black children

01:38:54.070 --> 01:38:56.290
grow up poor.

01:38:56.290 --> 01:38:59.630
Half of all black
children grow up poor.

01:38:59.630 --> 01:39:01.280
One person in 20 in
the United States

01:39:01.280 --> 01:39:03.440
suffers some form of
burglary, assault, rape,

01:39:03.440 --> 01:39:06.140
or murder each year.

01:39:06.140 --> 01:39:09.890
There are 250 reported rapes
daily, one every six minutes,

01:39:09.890 --> 01:39:13.400
probably 10 times that
number of unreported rapes.

01:39:13.400 --> 01:39:16.210
There's a brutal beating of
a woman every 19 seconds,

01:39:16.210 --> 01:39:18.930
probably more.

01:39:18.930 --> 01:39:21.480
There's one successful suicide
in the United States every 20

01:39:21.480 --> 01:39:24.540
minutes and 10 times that
number that fail, in the richest

01:39:24.540 --> 01:39:26.230
country in the world.

01:39:26.230 --> 01:39:30.440
That's the product of
institutional structures

01:39:30.440 --> 01:39:33.080
that are based on class
division, gender division, race

01:39:33.080 --> 01:39:35.732
division, and political elitism.

01:39:35.732 --> 01:39:37.190
The reason I became
a revolutionary

01:39:37.190 --> 01:39:41.390
is because I believed that those
problems were not accidents.

01:39:41.390 --> 01:39:42.740
They weren't bad policies.

01:39:42.740 --> 01:39:46.910
They were a natural outgrowth
of those underlying structures.

01:39:46.910 --> 01:39:50.795
One half of 1% of
all US citizens

01:39:50.795 --> 01:39:54.890
hold one third of all US wealth.

01:39:54.890 --> 01:39:58.570
The average CEO, chairman
of a corporation,

01:39:58.570 --> 01:40:04.000
earns as much as 93 factory
workers or 72 teachers.

01:40:04.000 --> 01:40:08.980
And they earn that not counting
the interest that they earn.

01:40:08.980 --> 01:40:12.700
Those 30 billionaires, those
folks are earning $2 million

01:40:12.700 --> 01:40:13.876
a week in interest.

01:40:19.200 --> 01:40:25.820
When I came here, I was
rushed to a fraternity.

01:40:25.820 --> 01:40:27.731
MIT has a fraternity system.

01:40:27.731 --> 01:40:29.480
I want to explain
something about why it's

01:40:29.480 --> 01:40:32.550
hard to organize people myself.

01:40:32.550 --> 01:40:37.094
And the fraternities were sort
of plus, at least at that time.

01:40:37.094 --> 01:40:38.510
Those of us who
went into them, we

01:40:38.510 --> 01:40:42.790
thought they were more
social, better place to live.

01:40:42.790 --> 01:40:45.340
It's arguably not
true, but we felt that.

01:40:45.340 --> 01:40:48.000
And we were rushed.

01:40:48.000 --> 01:40:50.230
And I went to rush week.

01:40:50.230 --> 01:40:52.930
And from my perspective, what
I saw during rush week was

01:40:52.930 --> 01:40:54.730
I was ushered into the building.

01:40:54.730 --> 01:40:56.560
And I got good food,
and people were

01:40:56.560 --> 01:40:58.720
wonderful and nice and friendly.

01:40:58.720 --> 01:41:00.100
And I was catered to.

01:41:00.100 --> 01:41:01.750
And they attempted
to try and get me

01:41:01.750 --> 01:41:03.280
to join their
various fraternities.

01:41:03.280 --> 01:41:05.080
This would last for
a period of time.

01:41:05.080 --> 01:41:09.510
And it's a very active time,
and there's a tremendous effort.

01:41:09.510 --> 01:41:12.504
I joined a fraternity.

01:41:12.504 --> 01:41:14.170
And then we went
through what's called--

01:41:16.920 --> 01:41:19.063
what do you call it when
you get into a fraternity?

01:41:19.063 --> 01:41:19.930
AUDIENCE: Pledging.

01:41:19.930 --> 01:41:21.160
ALBERT: Pledging.

01:41:21.160 --> 01:41:24.210
And that went on for
four months or so.

01:41:24.210 --> 01:41:27.076
MIT's too sophisticated for
the normal kind of hazing.

01:41:27.076 --> 01:41:28.450
Sophisticated--
remember, this is

01:41:28.450 --> 01:41:30.220
the place building smart bombs.

01:41:30.220 --> 01:41:32.896
MIT's too sophisticated for
the normal kind of hazing.

01:41:32.896 --> 01:41:34.270
So instead, what
they did is they

01:41:34.270 --> 01:41:37.480
had the freshmen clean the
place every Friday night,

01:41:37.480 --> 01:41:40.540
from about 8 o'clock until
about 3 o'clock in the morning.

01:41:40.540 --> 01:41:44.350
Now, at MIT, you don't have
that much time to yourself.

01:41:44.350 --> 01:41:46.810
So it's not a small thing
to work from 8 o'clock

01:41:46.810 --> 01:41:49.180
to 3 o'clock in the morning
cleaning the floors,

01:41:49.180 --> 01:41:51.880
and then cleaning them again,
and then cleaning them again

01:41:51.880 --> 01:41:53.350
for four months.

01:41:53.350 --> 01:41:55.360
At the end of that
four months, we

01:41:55.360 --> 01:42:00.469
were inducted as full-fledged
members of the fraternity.

01:42:00.469 --> 01:42:01.510
And we went to a meeting.

01:42:01.510 --> 01:42:03.490
And they told us something
very interesting.

01:42:03.490 --> 01:42:05.620
They told us that
during rush week,

01:42:05.620 --> 01:42:09.340
they had tapped the telephones
in the fraternity where

01:42:09.340 --> 01:42:10.870
we stayed over.

01:42:10.870 --> 01:42:13.860
And they had bugged the rooms.

01:42:13.860 --> 01:42:15.890
Tapped the telephones
and bugged the rooms.

01:42:15.890 --> 01:42:17.500
And we asked why.

01:42:17.500 --> 01:42:21.030
And they said, well, you had to
make a difficult decision very

01:42:21.030 --> 01:42:21.540
quickly--

01:42:21.540 --> 01:42:23.310
where to live.

01:42:23.310 --> 01:42:25.110
And we researched you folks.

01:42:25.110 --> 01:42:28.470
And we were calmer and
older and more mature.

01:42:28.470 --> 01:42:31.910
And we were in a better position
to make that decision than you.

01:42:31.910 --> 01:42:35.720
So what we did is we tapped the
phones and we bugged the rooms

01:42:35.720 --> 01:42:38.630
so that we would hear
what you would say when

01:42:38.630 --> 01:42:41.450
you called your parents or--

01:42:41.450 --> 01:42:44.480
and at that time,
I don't know what--

01:42:44.480 --> 01:42:46.230
at that time, it
was your girlfriend.

01:42:46.230 --> 01:42:47.790
And it was all men--

01:42:47.790 --> 01:42:50.064
or any other friend.

01:42:50.064 --> 01:42:51.980
And you described what
you were interested in.

01:42:51.980 --> 01:42:53.060
So if I called
somebody and said,

01:42:53.060 --> 01:42:54.601
this place is really
nice, but nobody

01:42:54.601 --> 01:42:59.030
plays tennis, 7 o'clock the next
morning, tennis game for me.

01:42:59.030 --> 01:43:00.530
No lie.

01:43:00.530 --> 01:43:03.770
If I said, I thought that
I'd have more intellectual

01:43:03.770 --> 01:43:04.680
stimulation here.

01:43:04.680 --> 01:43:07.138
I thought there'd be more people
who would be into physics,

01:43:07.138 --> 01:43:08.135
and they wanted me--

01:43:08.135 --> 01:43:12.020
oh, I forgot to mention,
this was for the people

01:43:12.020 --> 01:43:14.690
who they wanted.

01:43:14.690 --> 01:43:17.150
The process was a two-tiered
one in the fraternity

01:43:17.150 --> 01:43:18.600
when I went into.

01:43:18.600 --> 01:43:21.902
They had a little chain
because you could go any

01:43:21.902 --> 01:43:23.360
to any living group
that you wanted

01:43:23.360 --> 01:43:24.740
to try and become a part of.

01:43:24.740 --> 01:43:27.300
And they literally researched
all incoming freshmen,

01:43:27.300 --> 01:43:28.550
and they knew who they wanted.

01:43:28.550 --> 01:43:30.050
Everybody who they
didn't want, they just

01:43:30.050 --> 01:43:31.883
funneled through the
building, out the door,

01:43:31.883 --> 01:43:33.512
and into the back alley.

01:43:33.512 --> 01:43:34.970
The ones they wanted
went upstairs.

01:43:34.970 --> 01:43:36.553
And they tried to
get you to stay over

01:43:36.553 --> 01:43:39.890
so they could tap your
phone and bug your room.

01:43:39.890 --> 01:43:42.140
Now, the reason I
bring this story up

01:43:42.140 --> 01:43:46.550
is because if they had told
us the day after rush week--

01:43:46.550 --> 01:43:48.860
if they had told me
the day after rush week

01:43:48.860 --> 01:43:52.840
that they tapped my phone,
I would have gone berserk.

01:43:52.840 --> 01:43:54.990
Berserk.

01:43:54.990 --> 01:43:57.090
But when they told
me after five months

01:43:57.090 --> 01:43:59.440
of hazing that they
tapped my phone--

01:43:59.440 --> 01:44:02.690
in other words, after
I had invested only 20

01:44:02.690 --> 01:44:06.580
Friday nights, but
still, 20 Friday nights,

01:44:06.580 --> 01:44:09.190
and after I had become
friends and become involved

01:44:09.190 --> 01:44:10.622
and gotten some
status associated

01:44:10.622 --> 01:44:12.705
with this fraternity, when
they told me and the 20

01:44:12.705 --> 01:44:16.050
other incoming freshmen,
we all took it.

01:44:16.050 --> 01:44:17.397
We didn't do shit.

01:44:17.397 --> 01:44:18.230
We didn't get upset.

01:44:18.230 --> 01:44:20.330
We were part of the gang.

01:44:20.330 --> 01:44:22.240
We abided by it.

01:44:22.240 --> 01:44:25.740
Well, for some reason
that I don't know,

01:44:25.740 --> 01:44:29.470
something happened in me, and
I had a little trouble with it.

01:44:29.470 --> 01:44:30.310
But I didn't quit.

01:44:30.310 --> 01:44:31.752
I just sort of thought about it.

01:44:31.752 --> 01:44:33.960
And I stay in the fraternity
for the next six months.

01:44:33.960 --> 01:44:38.040
And actually, I was pretty
verbal and outgoing.

01:44:38.040 --> 01:44:40.920
And I was sort of the
fair-haired up-and-comer

01:44:40.920 --> 01:44:41.670
of the fraternity.

01:44:41.670 --> 01:44:44.430
And they even chart your life.

01:44:44.430 --> 01:44:48.240
They charted that I was going to
be their candidate for UAP four

01:44:48.240 --> 01:44:51.180
years later, irony of ironies.

01:44:51.180 --> 01:44:52.640
It's the truth.

01:44:52.640 --> 01:44:54.671
It's the honest truth.

01:44:54.671 --> 01:44:57.110
Well, at the end of the year,
I went home for the summer.

01:44:57.110 --> 01:45:01.400
And I started writing to
some of the freshmen with me.

01:45:01.400 --> 01:45:07.300
And I decided that
I had been a fool.

01:45:07.300 --> 01:45:10.810
And I decided to leave.

01:45:10.810 --> 01:45:11.500
And I came back.

01:45:11.500 --> 01:45:13.270
And the first political
thing I did was I

01:45:13.270 --> 01:45:17.410
sat on the fender of
a car outside API.

01:45:17.410 --> 01:45:20.100
Alpha Epsilon Pi
was the fraternity.

01:45:20.100 --> 01:45:21.740
And I told the freshmen
who were coming

01:45:21.740 --> 01:45:23.220
in rush week what was going on.

01:45:25.750 --> 01:45:26.490
[CHEERING]

01:45:26.490 --> 01:45:32.100
Well, what happened
was very amusing.

01:45:32.100 --> 01:45:34.210
There was a brawl.

01:45:34.210 --> 01:45:36.070
Half of the fraternity
wanted to get me.

01:45:36.070 --> 01:45:38.380
The other half didn't
want the embarrassment

01:45:38.380 --> 01:45:40.740
of their having gotten me.

01:45:40.740 --> 01:45:43.880
And they fought with each other.

01:45:43.880 --> 01:45:44.950
This is amusing.

01:45:44.950 --> 01:45:48.850
But what's really the
point of this story

01:45:48.850 --> 01:45:52.510
is that in subsequent weeks,
the rest of my class--

01:45:52.510 --> 01:45:55.990
or all but five or six of
them-- left that fraternity.

01:45:55.990 --> 01:45:59.020
And that group of
people became the core

01:45:59.020 --> 01:46:01.806
of what was called
Rosa Luxembourg SDS.

01:46:01.806 --> 01:46:04.040
And that group of
people is the group

01:46:04.040 --> 01:46:10.700
of people who did what was done
here at MIT, along with others.

01:46:10.700 --> 01:46:12.800
And there was nothing,
absolutely nothing,

01:46:12.800 --> 01:46:16.250
to distinguish me or any of
the others of those people

01:46:16.250 --> 01:46:18.800
from everybody else
in the freshman class,

01:46:18.800 --> 01:46:24.280
except the trauma and
the mindset change that

01:46:24.280 --> 01:46:28.060
was associated in this case not
with understanding imperialism,

01:46:28.060 --> 01:46:32.770
but with understanding a
form of local feudalism

01:46:32.770 --> 01:46:37.490
in this fraternity and
the opening of ourselves

01:46:37.490 --> 01:46:39.920
that it led to
understanding something

01:46:39.920 --> 01:46:43.270
about the world and the
radicalization that ensued.

01:46:43.270 --> 01:46:45.470
Notice that it only
took 20 Friday nights

01:46:45.470 --> 01:46:50.100
to hold us off for six
months, plus the socialization

01:46:50.100 --> 01:46:51.510
of the gray walls
and the promise

01:46:51.510 --> 01:46:55.310
of being a big physicist
and all the rest of that.

01:46:55.310 --> 01:46:58.580
Everybody in the United
States has some forces

01:46:58.580 --> 01:47:02.540
working on them that make it
very hard to move to the left.

01:47:05.210 --> 01:47:07.530
There's the socialization.

01:47:07.530 --> 01:47:09.670
There's ignorance among many.

01:47:09.670 --> 01:47:13.580
There's literal ignorance,
literal belief in the lies.

01:47:13.580 --> 01:47:16.910
There's the pressures of
maintaining your family

01:47:16.910 --> 01:47:19.730
or of trying to get
yourself in there.

01:47:19.730 --> 01:47:21.950
And there's the awareness
that I think really

01:47:21.950 --> 01:47:27.060
exists deep down in most
people that opposition

01:47:27.060 --> 01:47:30.570
has an implication, that
going to the demonstrations

01:47:30.570 --> 01:47:34.350
once leads to going twice
and that going twice

01:47:34.350 --> 01:47:36.300
leads to going five times.

01:47:36.300 --> 01:47:40.560
And everybody knows that the
first step is the big step.

01:47:40.560 --> 01:47:42.660
The first time
you go [INAUDIBLE]

01:47:42.660 --> 01:47:46.020
is the big time because you
might just get sucked in.

01:47:46.020 --> 01:47:48.060
It might become your life.

01:47:48.060 --> 01:47:51.240
Instead of being the physicist,
you might-- well, whatever.

01:47:53.770 --> 01:47:56.260
And to build a movement
that's going to attract people

01:47:56.260 --> 01:48:01.100
over that hurdle means
building a movement that is not

01:48:01.100 --> 01:48:06.230
so abrasive, but it's
less fun and less pleasant

01:48:06.230 --> 01:48:10.205
and less empowering to be
in than a classroom at MIT.

01:48:10.205 --> 01:48:13.900
It means building a movement
that's not so sexist that women

01:48:13.900 --> 01:48:16.460
literally can't stand it.

01:48:16.460 --> 01:48:19.340
It means building a
movement that is not so

01:48:19.340 --> 01:48:24.570
dominated by white culture that
black people and Latino people

01:48:24.570 --> 01:48:28.050
find it just as abhorrent
as the society outside.

01:48:28.050 --> 01:48:29.700
It means building
a movement that's

01:48:29.700 --> 01:48:34.410
not so hierarchical that
it replicates the Congress.

01:48:34.410 --> 01:48:36.760
[APPLAUSE]

01:48:40.050 --> 01:48:42.000
And it means--
and this, I think,

01:48:42.000 --> 01:48:43.950
although it's the least
talked about thing,

01:48:43.950 --> 01:48:46.390
is probably the hardest thing.

01:48:46.390 --> 01:48:52.700
It means building a movement in
which a few intellectuals don't

01:48:52.700 --> 01:48:56.900
dominate everybody else, in
which we don't replicate class

01:48:56.900 --> 01:49:01.316
structure, not just race
and gender and sexual

01:49:01.316 --> 01:49:02.690
and the other
structures outside,

01:49:02.690 --> 01:49:04.340
but also class structure.

01:49:04.340 --> 01:49:06.980
It means creating a movement
that will welcome people

01:49:06.980 --> 01:49:10.130
and that will empower
people of all backgrounds

01:49:10.130 --> 01:49:12.590
and that will respect the
fact that by and large, they

01:49:12.590 --> 01:49:15.440
know a whole lot more about
what's wrong with the country

01:49:15.440 --> 01:49:17.630
and about what should
be done to the country

01:49:17.630 --> 01:49:20.720
than the people who have
the training to be eloquent

01:49:20.720 --> 01:49:24.770
and to dominate decision-making
and to dominate discussions.

01:49:24.770 --> 01:49:26.413
And we have to be
attuned to that.

01:49:29.299 --> 01:49:31.704
[APPLAUSE]

01:49:36.514 --> 01:49:40.464
I think we should stop, but
I do want to make one plea.

01:49:40.464 --> 01:49:41.380
I heard this evening--

01:49:41.380 --> 01:49:42.666
I don't have the details.

01:49:42.666 --> 01:49:44.290
But I heard this
evening that tomorrow,

01:49:44.290 --> 01:49:46.664
there will be civil disobedience
at the Federal Building.

01:49:46.664 --> 01:49:48.171
[APPLAUSE]

01:49:48.670 --> 01:49:51.880
There will also be some actions
that will be hard for people

01:49:51.880 --> 01:49:56.140
to move into, that will
be by smaller groups,

01:49:56.140 --> 01:49:58.260
I guess, in other places.

01:49:58.260 --> 01:50:00.010
And I can't identify
them because I

01:50:00.010 --> 01:50:00.980
don't know the details.

01:50:00.980 --> 01:50:02.481
But there will be
civil disobedience

01:50:02.481 --> 01:50:03.480
at the Federal Building.

01:50:03.480 --> 01:50:05.800
I'm quite sure there will
also be marches and rallies.

01:50:05.800 --> 01:50:08.560
And I suspect there'll
also be teach-ins.

01:50:08.560 --> 01:50:10.420
Do whatever it is that's
comfortable for you

01:50:10.420 --> 01:50:20.840
to do, but do something because
the people who are for the war

01:50:20.840 --> 01:50:23.230
have an excuse not
to do something.

01:50:23.230 --> 01:50:25.640
But I bet everybody
here is against the war.

01:50:25.640 --> 01:50:28.400
And I'll bet everybody
here is against all

01:50:28.400 --> 01:50:30.140
of the implications of the war.

01:50:30.140 --> 01:50:32.730
To be against the war,
and to be against all

01:50:32.730 --> 01:50:40.610
of the implications of the war,
and to do nothing is hypocrisy.

01:50:40.610 --> 01:50:42.920
And you have to
think about that.

01:50:42.920 --> 01:50:46.090
And it's very important for
people to act in the way

01:50:46.090 --> 01:50:48.460
that you want to, in the
manner that you want to,

01:50:48.460 --> 01:50:51.040
with others who you
choose, but to do it.

01:50:51.040 --> 01:50:55.100
And if we do, I think we
can have a serious impact.

01:50:55.100 --> 01:50:56.686
Let's do the
questions and answers?

01:50:56.686 --> 01:50:59.176
[CHEERING]

01:51:19.096 --> 01:51:21.337
MAN: What do you think
about holding questions

01:51:21.337 --> 01:51:24.362
to about 30 minutes, and then
going over and completing them?

01:51:24.362 --> 01:51:25.570
PRESENTER: Yeah, [INAUDIBLE].

01:51:25.570 --> 01:51:26.566
MAN: OK, great.

01:51:26.566 --> 01:51:27.935
Let's do it.

01:51:27.935 --> 01:51:30.310
Can I try and hand these out
while we're doing questions?

01:51:30.310 --> 01:51:31.518
PRESENTER: Yeah, that's fine.

01:51:31.518 --> 01:51:33.860
OK, just two announcements.

01:51:33.860 --> 01:51:36.140
More information about
the civil disobedience

01:51:36.140 --> 01:51:38.750
that Michael Albert
spoke about--

01:51:38.750 --> 01:51:41.900
it says, put the brakes on war.

01:51:41.900 --> 01:51:44.270
Let's not go to war
for our gasoline.

01:51:44.270 --> 01:51:47.420
And the plan is to block traffic
during rush hour on Boston's

01:51:47.420 --> 01:51:50.540
major arteries, sending a
clear message to Washington

01:51:50.540 --> 01:51:52.940
and the world that we
will not allow business

01:51:52.940 --> 01:51:56.780
to proceed as usual while people
are dying for our gasoline.

01:51:56.780 --> 01:51:59.720
And they plan to block
the JFK Federal Building.

01:51:59.720 --> 01:52:05.450
And it's gathering at
the SEIU local 285, which

01:52:05.450 --> 01:52:08.900
is the seventh floor,
145 Tremont Street,

01:52:08.900 --> 01:52:10.944
across from the Common
at 7:00 PM for those

01:52:10.944 --> 01:52:11.735
who are interested.

01:52:11.735 --> 01:52:12.770
AUDIENCE: What time?

01:52:12.770 --> 01:52:14.840
PRESENTER: At 7:00
PM at the JFK--

01:52:14.840 --> 01:52:15.965
AUDIENCE: That was tonight.

01:52:15.965 --> 01:52:17.706
ALBERT: That was tonight.

01:52:17.706 --> 01:52:18.456
PRESENTER: Oh, OK.

01:52:18.456 --> 01:52:20.180
ALBERT: The thing in the
morning is very early.

01:52:20.180 --> 01:52:20.830
PRESENTER: Sorry about that.

01:52:20.830 --> 01:52:21.455
AUDIENCE: 8:30.

01:52:21.455 --> 01:52:22.820
AUDIENCE: 7:00 AM.

01:52:22.820 --> 01:52:23.570
AUDIENCE: 7:00 AM.

01:52:23.570 --> 01:52:24.630
7:00 AM tomorrow morning.

01:52:24.630 --> 01:52:24.900
PRESENTER: 7:00 AM.

01:52:24.900 --> 01:52:25.150
OK.

01:52:25.150 --> 01:52:27.150
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
tonight's meeting gathered

01:52:27.150 --> 01:52:29.980
at the JFK Federal Building
at Government Center tomorrow

01:52:29.980 --> 01:52:31.927
morning at 7:00 AM.

01:52:31.927 --> 01:52:32.510
PRESENTER: OK.

01:52:32.510 --> 01:52:33.218
Sorry about that.

01:52:33.218 --> 01:52:34.640
Somebody gave me--

01:52:34.640 --> 01:52:36.672
MAN: Can I ask if anyone
hasn't gotten this?

01:52:36.672 --> 01:52:38.380
PRESENTER: Well, we're
gonna ask the Q&amp;A.

01:52:38.380 --> 01:52:39.080
MAN: What?

01:52:39.080 --> 01:52:39.913
PRESENTER: OK, fine.

01:52:39.913 --> 01:52:40.780
MAN: Yeah.

01:52:40.780 --> 01:52:42.510
While we're doing
the Q&amp;A, if you

01:52:42.510 --> 01:52:45.410
haven't gotten a schedule
of the week's activities

01:52:45.410 --> 01:52:50.350
for the Peace Center
and you want one,

01:52:50.350 --> 01:52:53.197
indicate it to me while
we're doing this Q&amp;A.

01:52:53.197 --> 01:52:53.780
PRESENTER: OK.

01:52:53.780 --> 01:52:56.363
And there's just one correction
that needs to be made on that.

01:52:56.363 --> 01:52:58.250
And that's that the
march for the day

01:52:58.250 --> 01:53:00.500
after either the
shooting or the bombing

01:53:00.500 --> 01:53:02.930
begins that's supposed
to take place at Tufts

01:53:02.930 --> 01:53:06.350
does not begin at 1:45 PM,
as indicated on the sheet,

01:53:06.350 --> 01:53:08.330
but rather at 12:30 PM.

01:53:08.330 --> 01:53:10.895
OK, and now we can begin
with question and answers.

01:53:14.615 --> 01:53:15.550
AUDIENCE: Over here?

01:53:15.550 --> 01:53:16.580
PRESENTER: Would
people like to raise--

01:53:16.580 --> 01:53:17.080
OK.

01:53:17.080 --> 01:53:22.070
AUDIENCE: Yeah, my question
is I keep wondering,

01:53:22.070 --> 01:53:25.610
and I'm troubled
by my own inability

01:53:25.610 --> 01:53:37.590
to come up with a convincing
alternative to what Bush

01:53:37.590 --> 01:53:42.000
is apparently bringing us to
the brink of and trying to do.

01:53:42.000 --> 01:53:47.330
And I feel that it's important
to have-- at least for myself--

01:53:47.330 --> 01:53:52.770
a sense of a viable alternative
approach to this situation.

01:53:52.770 --> 01:53:55.710
There are people who say
that, well, the United

01:53:55.710 --> 01:53:57.900
States shouldn't be
involved in this all.

01:53:57.900 --> 01:53:59.780
That's not my feeling.

01:53:59.780 --> 01:54:05.140
In spite of agreeing with
a lot of what Noam said,

01:54:05.140 --> 01:54:08.010
I tend to feel that
the invasion of Kuwait

01:54:08.010 --> 01:54:10.870
was something that
should be opposed.

01:54:10.870 --> 01:54:11.730
CHOMSKY: Of course.

01:54:11.730 --> 01:54:14.000
AUDIENCE: And I would like
to hear either of you,

01:54:14.000 --> 01:54:17.156
or anyone else,
perhaps, speak to what

01:54:17.156 --> 01:54:22.517
would be a viable position,
a credible position,

01:54:22.517 --> 01:54:28.700
an intelligent, sane, position,
for a United States government

01:54:28.700 --> 01:54:31.105
to take in response
to this situation.

01:54:34.490 --> 01:54:37.750
CHOMSKY: Well, I think a
credible, sane position is

01:54:37.750 --> 01:54:40.480
the one that most of
the world is taking--

01:54:40.480 --> 01:54:45.790
namely, rely on sanctions and
pursue the diplomatic track.

01:54:45.790 --> 01:54:48.460
I don't think that sanctions
should involve things

01:54:48.460 --> 01:54:49.525
like food.

01:54:49.525 --> 01:54:50.170
[APPLAUSE]

01:54:50.170 --> 01:54:53.535
I definitely-- there's
nothing specially--

01:54:53.535 --> 01:54:55.660
I mean, that's the position
of the National Council

01:54:55.660 --> 01:54:57.340
of Churches, for example.

01:54:57.340 --> 01:55:00.320
We're not out in left field.

01:55:00.320 --> 01:55:03.400
And it's the position
of most of the world.

01:55:03.400 --> 01:55:08.590
Sanctions in this case will cut
off Iraqi oil exports, which

01:55:08.590 --> 01:55:11.470
is their total export trade.

01:55:11.470 --> 01:55:12.820
And sooner or later--

01:55:12.820 --> 01:55:17.650
and probably sooner-- it will
have the effect of opening up

01:55:17.650 --> 01:55:18.710
a diplomatic track.

01:55:18.710 --> 01:55:21.490
In fact, it may have had
that effect last August.

01:55:21.490 --> 01:55:25.590
And we know what the contours
of a diplomatic settlement are.

01:55:25.590 --> 01:55:27.152
I mentioned a
couple of examples.

01:55:27.152 --> 01:55:28.360
They're perfectly reasonable.

01:55:28.360 --> 01:55:30.100
They've even been
proposed by Iraq,

01:55:30.100 --> 01:55:31.776
according to US officials.

01:55:31.776 --> 01:55:32.900
So there's a viable course.

01:55:32.900 --> 01:55:35.120
I don't understand what
the big problem is.

01:55:35.120 --> 01:55:37.480
A viable course is to
say, OK, let's consider

01:55:37.480 --> 01:55:38.980
the proposal of January 2.

01:55:38.980 --> 01:55:40.480
And let's see if
it's going to work.

01:55:40.480 --> 01:55:42.190
That's a reasonable proposal.

01:55:42.190 --> 01:55:46.480
It's perfectly reasonable to
have a diplomatic settlement

01:55:46.480 --> 01:55:48.004
of the Arab-Israeli conflict.

01:55:48.004 --> 01:55:50.170
The United States has been
blocking it for 20 years,

01:55:50.170 --> 01:55:51.253
and we should oppose that.

01:55:51.253 --> 01:55:53.050
We should have
such a settlement.

01:55:53.050 --> 01:55:55.630
It's perfectly reasonable to
have a diplomatic settlement

01:55:55.630 --> 01:55:59.500
of the very serious problem
of weapons of mass destruction

01:55:59.500 --> 01:56:00.100
in the region.

01:56:00.100 --> 01:56:00.600
Fine.

01:56:00.600 --> 01:56:02.410
Let's stop opposing that.

01:56:02.410 --> 01:56:04.850
Apparently, if we stop
opposing those two things,

01:56:04.850 --> 01:56:06.850
we can arrange for Iraqi
withdrawal from Kuwait.

01:56:06.850 --> 01:56:08.380
So I don't understand
what the problem is.

01:56:08.380 --> 01:56:10.930
The problem-- in fact, I do
understand what the problem is.

01:56:10.930 --> 01:56:13.760
The problem is the United States
won't accept those outcomes.

01:56:13.760 --> 01:56:17.049
But from our point of view,
having a credible answer,

01:56:17.049 --> 01:56:18.340
I don't think that's very hard.

01:56:18.340 --> 01:56:22.813
In fact, most of the
world has that answer.

01:56:22.813 --> 01:56:27.783
AUDIENCE: I'm wonder what's
to be made of reports

01:56:27.783 --> 01:56:31.262
and information about the
green light that was given

01:56:31.262 --> 01:56:34.290
to Hussein to invade Kuwait?

01:56:34.290 --> 01:56:38.164
And there were reports about
meetings, diplomatic meetings,

01:56:38.164 --> 01:56:39.890
immediately preceding
the invasion.

01:56:39.890 --> 01:56:43.070
And what's to be made of these?

01:56:43.070 --> 01:56:46.970
Why isn't there more about
this event [INAUDIBLE]??

01:56:46.970 --> 01:56:49.160
CHOMSKY: Well, the
facts are pretty clear.

01:56:49.160 --> 01:56:52.010
Iraq released
transcripts of meetings.

01:56:52.010 --> 01:56:54.770
And they were
basically agreed to be

01:56:54.770 --> 01:56:56.810
authentic by the United States.

01:56:56.810 --> 01:56:58.550
These were meetings
right on the eve

01:56:58.550 --> 01:57:01.590
of the invasion between
April Glaspie, who

01:57:01.590 --> 01:57:05.570
was the US ambassador, and is,
in fact, one of the big State

01:57:05.570 --> 01:57:07.640
Department experts on Iraq.

01:57:07.640 --> 01:57:09.309
She was the US
ambassador to Iraq.

01:57:09.309 --> 01:57:10.850
She had meetings
with Saddam Hussein.

01:57:10.850 --> 01:57:11.516
I think it was--

01:57:11.516 --> 01:57:13.640
forget-- a day or two
before the invasion.

01:57:13.640 --> 01:57:17.090
And there was also testimony
from the State Department

01:57:17.090 --> 01:57:21.440
here, public testimony, all of
which-- the basic tenor of it

01:57:21.440 --> 01:57:24.410
was that the United States
recognized that Iraq

01:57:24.410 --> 01:57:26.780
had claims against Kuwait.

01:57:26.780 --> 01:57:31.050
And it had no particular
position on the border issues.

01:57:31.050 --> 01:57:34.760
Now, there's basically the two
border issues that I mentioned.

01:57:34.760 --> 01:57:38.180
One has to do with
access to the Gulf.

01:57:38.180 --> 01:57:42.560
And that involves these two
islands, uninhabited islands.

01:57:42.560 --> 01:57:45.350
The other has to do with
this one contested oil field.

01:57:45.350 --> 01:57:48.300
And the US position was, we have
no position on those things.

01:57:48.300 --> 01:57:51.320
Now, it's very
hard to figure out

01:57:51.320 --> 01:57:53.360
what's going on in
Saddam Hussein's head.

01:57:53.360 --> 01:57:57.080
But a fair speculation
is he assumed

01:57:57.080 --> 01:57:59.060
that that was a green light.

01:57:59.060 --> 01:58:02.150
That's possible.

01:58:02.150 --> 01:58:05.130
However, there's a
further speculation here,

01:58:05.130 --> 01:58:07.980
which is not totally without
credibility, I must say.

01:58:07.980 --> 01:58:09.074
But I don't believe it.

01:58:09.074 --> 01:58:10.490
And that is that
the United States

01:58:10.490 --> 01:58:13.970
tried to draw him
in, which does have

01:58:13.970 --> 01:58:15.530
some advantages for the US.

01:58:15.530 --> 01:58:16.790
There's no doubt about it.

01:58:16.790 --> 01:58:22.345
I mean, from the point of view
of this mad strategic logic

01:58:22.345 --> 01:58:23.720
that I was talking
about, there's

01:58:23.720 --> 01:58:27.590
some point to having at least
maybe a war, or at least

01:58:27.590 --> 01:58:28.400
the threat of war.

01:58:28.400 --> 01:58:30.630
It gives the US
basis in the region.

01:58:30.630 --> 01:58:33.170
It establishes the
efficacy of force,

01:58:33.170 --> 01:58:35.600
gives a shot to the
domestic Pentagon system,

01:58:35.600 --> 01:58:37.730
which was in some
trouble, and so on.

01:58:37.730 --> 01:58:38.600
So yeah.

01:58:38.600 --> 01:58:41.030
However, I just think
it's extremely unlikely

01:58:41.030 --> 01:58:43.100
that the attempt
was to draw him in.

01:58:43.100 --> 01:58:45.020
It's much too dangerous.

01:58:45.020 --> 01:58:46.910
I mean, unless people
are really crazy,

01:58:46.910 --> 01:58:48.410
they wouldn't have done that.

01:58:48.410 --> 01:58:50.300
It's not saying it's impossible.

01:58:50.300 --> 01:58:53.990
And the argument
for it is not zero.

01:58:53.990 --> 01:58:56.060
But I don't believe it myself.

01:58:56.060 --> 01:59:00.260
I think what happened is Saddam
Hussein probably interpreted it

01:59:00.260 --> 01:59:02.030
as authorization,
as saying, well,

01:59:02.030 --> 01:59:04.430
the United States doesn't
care, so I'll move in.

01:59:04.430 --> 01:59:07.820
And then when the US perceived
that this guy is not just

01:59:07.820 --> 01:59:12.380
a loyal puppet, but rather an
independent nationalist, then

01:59:12.380 --> 01:59:14.690
all of a sudden he's got to go.

01:59:14.690 --> 01:59:16.550
Notice that the
invasion of Kuwait

01:59:16.550 --> 01:59:21.016
didn't really add very much to
his crimes, didn't add a lot.

01:59:21.016 --> 01:59:22.640
And it didn't bring
him up to the level

01:59:22.640 --> 01:59:25.160
of plenty of people we
support, like General Suharto

01:59:25.160 --> 01:59:26.870
in Indonesia, for example.

01:59:26.870 --> 01:59:29.690
But it did show that he was
an independent nationalist.

01:59:29.690 --> 01:59:31.989
And that's the ultimate crime.

01:59:31.989 --> 01:59:33.530
If you're an
independent nationalist,

01:59:33.530 --> 01:59:37.610
you can be a conservative
parliamentary democrat.

01:59:37.610 --> 01:59:40.070
You can be a right-wing
military figure.

01:59:40.070 --> 01:59:42.064
You can be a
left-wing organizer.

01:59:42.064 --> 01:59:43.230
Doesn't matter what you are.

01:59:43.230 --> 01:59:45.355
You've got to be knocked
off because that's the one

01:59:45.355 --> 01:59:49.130
thing the US will not tolerate.

01:59:49.130 --> 01:59:51.050
ALBERT: Hussein's like Noriega.

01:59:51.050 --> 01:59:53.090
Noriega we ostensibly
removed because we

01:59:53.090 --> 01:59:56.060
were concerned about his
interaction or his involvement

01:59:56.060 --> 01:59:57.490
in international drugs.

01:59:57.490 --> 02:00:00.260
Then we proceeded
to install bankers,

02:00:00.260 --> 02:00:03.400
money launderers
for the drug cartel

02:00:03.400 --> 02:00:05.720
as the government
of Panama, putting

02:00:05.720 --> 02:00:07.340
the lie to the motivation.

02:00:07.340 --> 02:00:10.490
The real motivation was,
again, admittedly a thug,

02:00:10.490 --> 02:00:19.130
admittedly an authoritarian
person, but a nationalist.

02:00:19.130 --> 02:00:21.740
But I want to relay--
although I agree with Noam,

02:00:21.740 --> 02:00:24.734
I want to relay a conversation
that I had in October.

02:00:24.734 --> 02:00:26.150
I had a conversation
with a fellow

02:00:26.150 --> 02:00:28.525
named John Stockwell, who some
of you may know who he is.

02:00:31.070 --> 02:00:35.570
He's an ex-CIA agent
who is now on our side

02:00:35.570 --> 02:00:39.387
and who's done a great deal of
work for peace and for justice.

02:00:39.387 --> 02:00:41.720
And I asked him what he thought
about what was going on.

02:00:41.720 --> 02:00:42.990
And it was much before now.

02:00:42.990 --> 02:00:46.790
It was back in early November.

02:00:46.790 --> 02:00:49.750
And he took me by surprise.

02:00:49.750 --> 02:00:52.660
He said, in the absence of
sufficient domestic dissent,

02:00:52.660 --> 02:00:55.065
there will be war.

02:00:55.065 --> 02:00:56.440
And the reason
there will be war,

02:00:56.440 --> 02:00:59.020
he said, I have to
explain that to you.

02:00:59.020 --> 02:01:01.460
I have to tell you how we
think about it in the CIA.

02:01:01.460 --> 02:01:03.130
And I don't think this is the
best way to think about it,

02:01:03.130 --> 02:01:04.510
but he did tell me how
they teach you to think

02:01:04.510 --> 02:01:06.220
about these things in the CIA.

02:01:06.220 --> 02:01:09.400
And they said, you can't really
look at the immediate context

02:01:09.400 --> 02:01:12.970
for your full understanding
because it's too volatile

02:01:12.970 --> 02:01:16.030
and it's too fraught
with possible confusion.

02:01:16.030 --> 02:01:19.100
You have to look at the
history of the country and ask,

02:01:19.100 --> 02:01:21.250
does it periodically
go to war or not?

02:01:21.250 --> 02:01:23.350
In this case, both sides, yes.

02:01:23.350 --> 02:01:25.210
If it does
periodically go to war,

02:01:25.210 --> 02:01:26.890
what are the indices
which tend to be

02:01:26.890 --> 02:01:29.380
exhibited in the country
when it goes to war?

02:01:29.380 --> 02:01:31.807
And you look at those
indices, he told me.

02:01:31.807 --> 02:01:34.390
And he said, if you look at the
indices for the United States,

02:01:34.390 --> 02:01:35.580
you find war.

02:01:35.580 --> 02:01:39.050
You find the economy decaying.

02:01:39.050 --> 02:01:44.040
You find the allegiance
of the public

02:01:44.040 --> 02:01:46.560
to the policies of
the government being

02:01:46.560 --> 02:01:48.720
undermined, the peace dividend.

02:01:48.720 --> 02:01:52.770
And you find the possibility
of a large-scale war

02:01:52.770 --> 02:01:56.340
being a way to
alleviate that problem

02:01:56.340 --> 02:01:59.280
as well as to pursue the
course of becoming the world's

02:01:59.280 --> 02:02:00.960
mercenary state.

02:02:00.960 --> 02:02:04.050
And he felt-- and I relay
it for what it's worth.

02:02:04.050 --> 02:02:06.510
I don't know what to make of it.

02:02:06.510 --> 02:02:09.510
He felt-- after all,
he was in there--

02:02:09.510 --> 02:02:12.030
that if we weren't in
this position with Hussein

02:02:12.030 --> 02:02:15.180
right now, we would be in
this position with Cuba.

02:02:15.180 --> 02:02:18.090
That literally,
Hussein was a gift.

02:02:18.090 --> 02:02:22.320
Not an arrangement,
not a trap, but a gift,

02:02:22.320 --> 02:02:24.360
that Hussein's
activities provided

02:02:24.360 --> 02:02:26.130
exactly what they wanted.

02:02:26.130 --> 02:02:28.830
And it does sort of go
a long ways to explain

02:02:28.830 --> 02:02:31.230
our absolute unwillingness to
get out of this in anything

02:02:31.230 --> 02:02:34.472
short of what we're facing.

02:02:34.472 --> 02:02:36.060
PRESENTER: I'm gonna
just say, we'll

02:02:36.060 --> 02:02:37.476
just take a couple
more questions,

02:02:37.476 --> 02:02:40.600
and then we will have a question
and answer that will take place

02:02:40.600 --> 02:02:44.020
at the student center where Noam
and Michael Albert have both

02:02:44.020 --> 02:02:46.310
promised to spend a few
more minutes at this Peace

02:02:46.310 --> 02:02:48.040
Center, answering questions.

02:02:48.040 --> 02:02:51.180
So gentleman over there.

02:02:51.180 --> 02:02:54.726
AUDIENCE: What effort are you
aware of that our government

02:02:54.726 --> 02:03:02.662
would take to quiet the dissent
movement against this war,

02:03:02.662 --> 02:03:03.654
domestically?

02:03:03.654 --> 02:03:06.630
[INAUDIBLE]

02:03:06.630 --> 02:03:08.630
AUDIENCE: The press
doesn't report it.

02:03:08.630 --> 02:03:11.630
CHOMSKY: Well, we have
experience on that.

02:03:11.630 --> 02:03:12.620
Back in the '60s--

02:03:12.620 --> 02:03:14.240
ALBERT: A lot of experience.

02:03:14.240 --> 02:03:17.330
CHOMSKY: Yeah,
plenty of experience.

02:03:17.330 --> 02:03:19.040
Every time there
has been dissent,

02:03:19.040 --> 02:03:21.530
there has been repression.

02:03:21.530 --> 02:03:22.930
Some of it got exposed.

02:03:22.930 --> 02:03:24.860
Like, for example,
during the 1960s,

02:03:24.860 --> 02:03:29.000
I don't know how many of you
know about the COINTELPRO

02:03:29.000 --> 02:03:29.890
program.

02:03:29.890 --> 02:03:32.930
That was a big
FBI program, which

02:03:32.930 --> 02:03:35.540
went through whole-- actually,
it started in the '30s.

02:03:35.540 --> 02:03:38.210
But it really picked
up under Kennedy.

02:03:38.210 --> 02:03:41.050
And it targeted first
the Communist Party,

02:03:41.050 --> 02:03:44.090
then the Puerto Rican
nationalist movement, then

02:03:44.090 --> 02:03:48.680
the black movements,
all of them,

02:03:48.680 --> 02:03:51.110
the civil rights movement,
all the peace movements,

02:03:51.110 --> 02:03:54.290
the entire new left, the
American Indian Movement,

02:03:54.290 --> 02:03:55.370
everybody.

02:03:55.370 --> 02:03:59.870
And that reached to the level of
actual political assassination.

02:03:59.870 --> 02:04:03.650
The worst case was the
Gestapo-style assassination

02:04:03.650 --> 02:04:05.780
of a Black Panther
organizer, probably

02:04:05.780 --> 02:04:12.260
drugged in bed by the Chicago
police with an FBI cover.

02:04:12.260 --> 02:04:14.810
It also did things--

02:04:14.810 --> 02:04:19.520
it instigated race riots
and violence in the ghettos

02:04:19.520 --> 02:04:21.302
and took credit for killings.

02:04:21.302 --> 02:04:22.760
I mean, a lot of
this documentation

02:04:22.760 --> 02:04:26.940
came out on court cases
and things like that.

02:04:26.940 --> 02:04:28.670
It also did things
that were like a joke.

02:04:28.670 --> 02:04:33.984
So I mean, I was one of
the targets of it at MIT.

02:04:33.984 --> 02:04:35.150
I mean, this is just a joke.

02:04:35.150 --> 02:04:37.220
But just to give you
the kinds of things

02:04:37.220 --> 02:04:40.460
they do, to give you some
insight into the FBI.

02:04:40.460 --> 02:04:46.069
They were forced under court
order to release documents.

02:04:46.069 --> 02:04:47.735
And here's what's
going to go on at MIT.

02:04:50.590 --> 02:04:52.070
They were forced
under court order

02:04:52.070 --> 02:04:53.690
to release documents
for people who

02:04:53.690 --> 02:04:55.880
were targeted under COINTELPRO.

02:04:55.880 --> 02:04:57.650
And two of the people
who were targeted

02:04:57.650 --> 02:05:01.850
were teaching
assistants in a course

02:05:01.850 --> 02:05:04.700
that Louis Kampf and
I taught that Mike

02:05:04.700 --> 02:05:10.100
was in a couple times, and
other people, back in the '60s.

02:05:10.100 --> 02:05:12.080
The two people who were
teaching assistants

02:05:12.080 --> 02:05:13.460
were guys in the
draft resistance

02:05:13.460 --> 02:05:14.810
movement and other things.

02:05:14.810 --> 02:05:18.680
We sort of brought people
in who could do good things

02:05:18.680 --> 02:05:21.210
and could use the job
and that sort of thing.

02:05:21.210 --> 02:05:25.070
And the sequence
of FBI documents

02:05:25.070 --> 02:05:26.517
got released under court order.

02:05:26.517 --> 02:05:27.350
It starts like this.

02:05:27.350 --> 02:05:31.130
First one, from the Boston
office to J. Edgar Hoover,

02:05:31.130 --> 02:05:32.990
says, we have discovered
that these two guys

02:05:32.990 --> 02:05:34.281
are teaching assistants at MIT.

02:05:36.710 --> 02:05:38.660
It says, our source within MIT--

02:05:38.660 --> 02:05:40.700
and then something's
blacked out.

02:05:40.700 --> 02:05:42.800
And we haven't been able
to figure out who it was.

02:05:42.800 --> 02:05:46.210
Our source within MIT tells
us that these two guys

02:05:46.210 --> 02:05:47.210
are teaching assistants.

02:05:47.210 --> 02:05:51.230
This would be a good target
for a disinformation operation

02:05:51.230 --> 02:05:52.400
to get them kicked out.

02:05:52.400 --> 02:05:54.590
Can we have
authorization for it?

02:05:54.590 --> 02:05:57.170
And then the a thing comes back
from J. Edgar Hoover saying,

02:05:57.170 --> 02:05:58.400
yeah, it sounds
like a good idea.

02:05:58.400 --> 02:05:58.830
Go ahead.

02:05:58.830 --> 02:06:00.538
And then it goes up
and back for a while.

02:06:00.538 --> 02:06:02.810
And finally, at the end,
the last one from the Boston

02:06:02.810 --> 02:06:05.420
office, says, our
source within MIT

02:06:05.420 --> 02:06:07.219
tells us that all
this stuff has worked

02:06:07.219 --> 02:06:09.510
and these two guys are not
going to be hired next year.

02:06:09.510 --> 02:06:10.760
So we've succeeded.

02:06:10.760 --> 02:06:14.150
The only problem with that
story is it was a total fake.

02:06:14.150 --> 02:06:17.012
These two guys were going off
to their next piece of work.

02:06:17.012 --> 02:06:19.220
They never had the slightest
intention of coming back

02:06:19.220 --> 02:06:22.130
to spend another term at MIT.

02:06:22.130 --> 02:06:24.762
And the whole thing had been
cooked up in an FBI office.

02:06:24.762 --> 02:06:26.720
Incidentally, that's one
of the kinds of things

02:06:26.720 --> 02:06:29.570
that goes on in FBI offices.

02:06:29.570 --> 02:06:31.640
An agent has a caseload.

02:06:31.640 --> 02:06:34.890
And it's a lot easier not to
go after guys in the mafia

02:06:34.890 --> 02:06:36.470
because, like, they shoot back.

02:06:36.470 --> 02:06:39.230
But if you fiddle around
with people like us,

02:06:39.230 --> 02:06:42.140
we're not gonna shoot
back, even if they did it.

02:06:42.140 --> 02:06:44.330
And that kind of
stuff will go on.

02:06:44.330 --> 02:06:48.200
So everything from probably
faked and invented harassment

02:06:48.200 --> 02:06:50.600
to really serious
things like organizing

02:06:50.600 --> 02:06:53.810
political assassinations
and ghetto riots.

02:06:53.810 --> 02:06:56.030
That's the kind of
thing that will go on.

02:06:56.030 --> 02:06:58.840
That's been going on all
through American history.

02:06:58.840 --> 02:07:03.394
We've got a very rough history
of repression of dissidents.

02:07:03.394 --> 02:07:04.935
AUDIENCE: And it
already is going on,

02:07:04.935 --> 02:07:05.917
if you read the papers.

02:07:05.917 --> 02:07:10.666
The FBI is going around to our
Americans in systematic ways

02:07:10.666 --> 02:07:12.478
and telling them,
among other things,

02:07:12.478 --> 02:07:17.110
if you receive any threats,
come to us for protection.

02:07:17.110 --> 02:07:19.510
And if you read the front
page of the New York Times,

02:07:19.510 --> 02:07:20.950
there've been a
number of articles

02:07:20.950 --> 02:07:22.390
just in this past week.

02:07:22.390 --> 02:07:25.490
They've already contacted
over 200 Arab Americans.

02:07:25.490 --> 02:07:27.980
ALBERT: Repression is
certainly a real phenomenon.

02:07:27.980 --> 02:07:32.870
But it's not as effective
as it's often led on

02:07:32.870 --> 02:07:34.880
to be unless we let it be.

02:07:34.880 --> 02:07:36.950
There's two things that
I'd like to suggest

02:07:36.950 --> 02:07:39.680
that are critical to having
the kind of activities

02:07:39.680 --> 02:07:42.860
that they engage in be
infinitely less effective

02:07:42.860 --> 02:07:44.750
than they otherwise might be.

02:07:44.750 --> 02:07:46.970
The first is you
have to have support.

02:07:46.970 --> 02:07:50.150
If you have substantial
support, really massive support,

02:07:50.150 --> 02:07:52.520
it's very hard to repress.

02:07:52.520 --> 02:07:54.646
An instance of that
here at MIT was MIT

02:07:54.646 --> 02:07:55.770
thought it was very clever.

02:07:55.770 --> 02:07:59.727
They got an injunction in which
they listed about 10 of us

02:07:59.727 --> 02:08:01.310
that we weren't
allowed on the campus,

02:08:01.310 --> 02:08:03.080
and we were to be
arrested on sight

02:08:03.080 --> 02:08:04.890
on the day of a demonstration.

02:08:04.890 --> 02:08:07.310
So the 10 of us and many
thousands of other people

02:08:07.310 --> 02:08:08.150
came to MIT.

02:08:08.150 --> 02:08:11.800
And we stood on the
steps and ripped it up.

02:08:11.800 --> 02:08:13.400
And with many
thousands of people

02:08:13.400 --> 02:08:15.680
there, and with
many more thousands

02:08:15.680 --> 02:08:17.360
who would have
supported us, it was

02:08:17.360 --> 02:08:20.420
very difficult to do
anything about it.

02:08:20.420 --> 02:08:22.510
That's one answer to repression.

02:08:22.510 --> 02:08:25.270
The second side of repression
is infiltration and the kind

02:08:25.270 --> 02:08:27.130
of thing that was
done to the Panthers

02:08:27.130 --> 02:08:28.600
and to many other groups.

02:08:28.600 --> 02:08:31.060
And while that
activity is effective

02:08:31.060 --> 02:08:33.580
and that activity
is deadly, and it's

02:08:33.580 --> 02:08:35.500
impossible to stop
it entirely, it

02:08:35.500 --> 02:08:39.010
is possible to do
better than we did then.

02:08:39.010 --> 02:08:41.290
What we did then,
often, was to construct

02:08:41.290 --> 02:08:44.380
a movement in which
a policeman was

02:08:44.380 --> 02:08:49.840
at home, in which a policeman
was not immediately obvious.

02:08:49.840 --> 02:08:52.810
If you have a movement
which is really humane

02:08:52.810 --> 02:08:56.620
and which is really democratic
and in which macho behavior

02:08:56.620 --> 02:08:59.800
stands out like a sore
thumb, police agents

02:08:59.800 --> 02:09:02.117
have a very hard
time infiltrating.

02:09:02.117 --> 02:09:03.700
We have never found
one, we have never

02:09:03.700 --> 02:09:06.610
encountered one who can
operate in that kind of context

02:09:06.610 --> 02:09:08.432
in an effective fashion.

02:09:08.432 --> 02:09:09.640
And that's not a small thing.

02:09:09.640 --> 02:09:12.040
That can save lives in
addition to building a movement

02:09:12.040 --> 02:09:15.155
that it's worth being a part of.

02:09:15.155 --> 02:09:15.655
Oh.

02:09:15.655 --> 02:09:16.645
Oh, wait a minute.

02:09:16.645 --> 02:09:17.635
This-- yeah.

02:09:17.635 --> 02:09:20.605
PRESENTER: [INAUDIBLE]

02:09:20.605 --> 02:09:24.317
AUDIENCE: Can you tell me what's
the logic behind the United

02:09:24.317 --> 02:09:31.495
States supporting Israel
[INAUDIBLE] strategically

02:09:31.495 --> 02:09:43.870
[INAUDIBLE]

02:09:43.870 --> 02:09:45.178
CHOMSKY: Three.

02:09:45.178 --> 02:09:55.250
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

02:09:55.250 --> 02:09:56.920
CHOMSKY: There is
logic behind it.

02:09:56.920 --> 02:09:58.600
You could argue about whether--

02:09:58.600 --> 02:10:02.435
just purely cynical, forgetting
anything about morality.

02:10:02.435 --> 02:10:05.200
We even know some of
the background for it.

02:10:05.200 --> 02:10:05.803
In 1948--

02:10:05.803 --> 02:10:07.260
AUDIENCE: What was the question?

02:10:07.260 --> 02:10:07.759
CHOMSKY: Oh.

02:10:07.759 --> 02:10:11.560
The question was, what's
the logic behind US support

02:10:11.560 --> 02:10:12.110
of Israel?

02:10:12.110 --> 02:10:13.693
And notice it's
pretty strong support.

02:10:13.693 --> 02:10:15.460
We're supporting
them to the extent

02:10:15.460 --> 02:10:17.530
of rejecting a
political settlement,

02:10:17.530 --> 02:10:19.330
blocking a political
settlement, using it

02:10:19.330 --> 02:10:21.520
as a basis to go
to war, and so on,

02:10:21.520 --> 02:10:24.580
refusing to arrange
for a reduction of--

02:10:24.580 --> 02:10:27.760
we claim to be concerned
about Iraqi weapons

02:10:27.760 --> 02:10:30.490
of mass destruction, but
not at the cost of getting

02:10:30.490 --> 02:10:34.170
Israel to eliminate its
nuclear weapons, not that cost.

02:10:34.170 --> 02:10:37.450
And the official support is
around $3 billion a year.

02:10:37.450 --> 02:10:39.640
Actually, it comes
to more than that.

02:10:39.640 --> 02:10:42.190
All of that is expensive,
and then there's

02:10:42.190 --> 02:10:44.440
all these Arab countries
around with oil,

02:10:44.440 --> 02:10:45.900
like the Gulf oil
principalities.

02:10:45.900 --> 02:10:47.360
So what's the logic?

02:10:47.360 --> 02:10:49.660
Well, there's a real logic.

02:10:49.660 --> 02:10:53.140
And let me just run
through it quickly.

02:10:53.140 --> 02:10:55.870
Starting in 1948, when
Israel was founded,

02:10:55.870 --> 02:10:59.230
right after Israel
began, at that point,

02:10:59.230 --> 02:11:00.670
they weren't clear what to do.

02:11:00.670 --> 02:11:03.860
But Israel won some pretty big
military victories very fast.

02:11:03.860 --> 02:11:05.860
In fact, even before the
state was established,

02:11:05.860 --> 02:11:09.520
it already expanded quite
considerably in the civil war.

02:11:09.520 --> 02:11:13.870
And the Joint Chiefs of
Staff concluded right then

02:11:13.870 --> 02:11:18.580
that Israeli power would be a
good base for the United States

02:11:18.580 --> 02:11:19.570
in the region.

02:11:19.570 --> 02:11:23.380
They said, after Turkey, Israel
is the major military power

02:11:23.380 --> 02:11:24.370
in the region.

02:11:24.370 --> 02:11:29.180
And it can be an
outpost of US strength,

02:11:29.180 --> 02:11:30.640
especially with
Britain in trouble.

02:11:30.640 --> 02:11:33.400
Our lieutenant was in
trouble in the region.

02:11:33.400 --> 02:11:36.070
And this would be a
basis for using force.

02:11:36.070 --> 02:11:38.730
Ultimately, you have to use
force to control people.

02:11:38.730 --> 02:11:39.950
That continued.

02:11:39.950 --> 02:11:42.940
So in 1958, the National
Security Council

02:11:42.940 --> 02:11:47.290
had a memorandum in which they
concluded that opposition--

02:11:47.290 --> 02:11:50.680
they said a logical
corollary to our opposition

02:11:50.680 --> 02:11:53.980
to radical Arab nationalism--
that means independence.

02:11:53.980 --> 02:11:56.380
A logical corollary
to our opposition

02:11:56.380 --> 02:11:59.920
to independent nationalism
would be support for Israel

02:11:59.920 --> 02:12:04.750
as the one reliable pro-Western
state in the region.

02:12:04.750 --> 02:12:05.860
And it goes on like that.

02:12:05.860 --> 02:12:09.804
In the 1960s, the Defense
Intelligence Agency, we know--

02:12:09.804 --> 02:12:11.470
in fact, the whole
intelligence system--

02:12:11.470 --> 02:12:16.540
regarded Israel as a barrier
against Nasser's pressure

02:12:16.540 --> 02:12:18.580
on the Gulf oil producers.

02:12:18.580 --> 02:12:20.860
And in fact, by that
time, an alliance

02:12:20.860 --> 02:12:23.530
was being set up which was kind
of formalized under the Nixon

02:12:23.530 --> 02:12:27.490
Doctrine around 1970, an
alliance between Israel,

02:12:27.490 --> 02:12:33.420
Iran under the shah, and
Saudi Arabia, in which Iran

02:12:33.420 --> 02:12:38.400
and Israel were the tough guys
who kept anybody under control

02:12:38.400 --> 02:12:42.600
and made sure that the
ruling family of Saudi Arabia

02:12:42.600 --> 02:12:46.200
would stay rich and powerful
and not be threatened and work

02:12:46.200 --> 02:12:47.920
for us.

02:12:47.920 --> 02:12:53.640
Now, the smart guys and the
more intelligent analysts

02:12:53.640 --> 02:12:56.200
in the United States were
entirely aware of this.

02:12:56.200 --> 02:12:58.770
So for example, Senator Henry
Jackson, who at that time

02:12:58.770 --> 02:13:01.716
was the Senate's leading
specialist in energy

02:13:01.716 --> 02:13:03.090
in the Middle East
and so on, was

02:13:03.090 --> 02:13:07.140
a big hawk and very pro-Israel,
he spilled the whole story

02:13:07.140 --> 02:13:07.860
publicly.

02:13:07.860 --> 02:13:10.664
And quite correctly, he said
just what I said, more or less.

02:13:10.664 --> 02:13:12.580
I mean, he put it in a
little different words.

02:13:12.580 --> 02:13:16.380
But basically, he said that
Israel and Iran under the shah

02:13:16.380 --> 02:13:21.060
are reliable, powerful states
which can protect the Gulf oil

02:13:21.060 --> 02:13:24.190
producers from disruption
by radical elements.

02:13:24.190 --> 02:13:26.640
Now, Saudi Arabia
is not a country

02:13:26.640 --> 02:13:28.844
that has documents
that you can find.

02:13:28.844 --> 02:13:30.510
So we'll never have
a documentary record

02:13:30.510 --> 02:13:31.800
from Saudi Arabia.

02:13:31.800 --> 02:13:34.680
But when the US records
come out for that period,

02:13:34.680 --> 02:13:36.340
unless they're
destroyed or censored,

02:13:36.340 --> 02:13:38.310
I predict what
we're going to find.

02:13:38.310 --> 02:13:40.770
What we're going to find
is an actual alliance

02:13:40.770 --> 02:13:44.340
between Saudi Arabia,
Israel, and the shah.

02:13:44.340 --> 02:13:47.875
From the Saudi Arabian elite
point of view, Israel's fine.

02:13:47.875 --> 02:13:50.400
Now, they may yell about
it and this and that.

02:13:50.400 --> 02:13:51.510
But they're happy.

02:13:51.510 --> 02:13:55.997
They want to have
what's called a cop,

02:13:55.997 --> 02:13:58.080
but you have to be pretty
cynical about the police

02:13:58.080 --> 02:14:00.030
to use this phrase rent-a-cop.

02:14:00.030 --> 02:14:03.210
They want a thug around,
a rent-a-thug around who's

02:14:03.210 --> 02:14:07.210
going to suppress
radical tendencies, who

02:14:07.210 --> 02:14:08.750
will be a base for power.

02:14:08.750 --> 02:14:10.800
And that goes right
on to the present.

02:14:10.800 --> 02:14:13.050
Meanwhile, by the
1970s, Israel had

02:14:13.050 --> 02:14:14.880
begun to take on other roles.

02:14:14.880 --> 02:14:17.340
It was becoming a
mercenary state.

02:14:17.340 --> 02:14:21.330
It was used by the US in the
'60s under a big CIA subsidy

02:14:21.330 --> 02:14:23.250
to penetrate black Africa.

02:14:23.250 --> 02:14:24.690
It was used in Asia.

02:14:24.690 --> 02:14:27.180
It was used in Latin America.

02:14:27.180 --> 02:14:27.960
And it's useful.

02:14:27.960 --> 02:14:30.180
For a big terrorist country
like the United States,

02:14:30.180 --> 02:14:32.380
it's useful to have a
mercenary state around.

02:14:32.380 --> 02:14:34.950
See, other countries hire
individual terrorists.

02:14:34.950 --> 02:14:35.970
But we don't.

02:14:35.970 --> 02:14:38.650
We use mercenary states,
terrorist states.

02:14:38.650 --> 02:14:40.110
We're big shots.

02:14:40.110 --> 02:14:41.850
And a little bit
of this came out

02:14:41.850 --> 02:14:43.424
in the Iran-Contra hearings.

02:14:43.424 --> 02:14:45.840
And if you'll notice, there
was an alliance between Israel

02:14:45.840 --> 02:14:48.060
and Saudi Arabia.

02:14:48.060 --> 02:14:49.849
In fact, what
actually happened--

02:14:49.849 --> 02:14:52.140
this is part of the story
suppressed in the Iran-Contra

02:14:52.140 --> 02:14:54.390
hearings, but it's all
in the public record.

02:14:54.390 --> 02:14:56.250
I was writing about
in the early '80s.

02:14:56.250 --> 02:14:58.860
Immediately after the fall
of the shah-- immediately.

02:14:58.860 --> 02:15:01.080
No hostages, nothing
about hostages.

02:15:01.080 --> 02:15:02.786
Years before there
were hostages.

02:15:02.786 --> 02:15:04.410
Immediately after
the fall of the shah,

02:15:04.410 --> 02:15:05.993
the United States
started sending arms

02:15:05.993 --> 02:15:10.110
to Iran via Israel paid
for by Saudi Arabia.

02:15:10.110 --> 02:15:13.470
And the high Israeli officials
who were involved in it

02:15:13.470 --> 02:15:15.330
were completely open and public.

02:15:15.330 --> 02:15:17.940
Same guys who showed up later
in Iran-Contra hearings.

02:15:17.940 --> 02:15:20.130
They said exactly
what was going on.

02:15:20.130 --> 02:15:23.010
What was going on
was the United States

02:15:23.010 --> 02:15:24.660
was doing the standard
thing that you

02:15:24.660 --> 02:15:26.076
do when you're
trying to overthrow

02:15:26.076 --> 02:15:28.200
a government--
support its military.

02:15:28.200 --> 02:15:29.790
You want to overthrow
a government,

02:15:29.790 --> 02:15:32.370
you send arms to its military.

02:15:32.370 --> 02:15:34.450
That's the way you
get-- you make contacts

02:15:34.450 --> 02:15:36.450
with elements in the
military who will overthrow

02:15:36.450 --> 02:15:37.800
the government for you.

02:15:37.800 --> 02:15:40.170
That's the way we overthrew
the government of Indonesia,

02:15:40.170 --> 02:15:42.390
of Chile, case after case.

02:15:42.390 --> 02:15:44.190
And they were just
running the same game.

02:15:44.190 --> 02:15:48.120
And even in the
highly-suppressed story

02:15:48.120 --> 02:15:50.280
of the damage-control
operation that

02:15:50.280 --> 02:15:52.590
was called the Iran-Contra
hearings, you look,

02:15:52.590 --> 02:15:53.670
you can still see it.

02:15:53.670 --> 02:15:57.150
There was an Iran, Israel,
Saudi Arabia connection.

02:15:57.150 --> 02:15:59.160
And that's that old connection.

02:15:59.160 --> 02:16:00.990
Now, those are reasons.

02:16:00.990 --> 02:16:03.600
There's also intelligence
interactions.

02:16:03.600 --> 02:16:06.350
Israel can do things
for the United States.

02:16:06.350 --> 02:16:12.210
Israel tests advanced weapons
in live battlefield conditions

02:16:12.210 --> 02:16:13.590
against defenseless targets.

02:16:13.590 --> 02:16:14.870
That's useful.

02:16:14.870 --> 02:16:18.030
It's part of what went on in
things like the Lebanon War.

02:16:18.030 --> 02:16:20.220
There's common
weapons development.

02:16:20.220 --> 02:16:21.516
There's all kinds of things.

02:16:21.516 --> 02:16:22.890
So from some points
of view-- you

02:16:22.890 --> 02:16:26.700
know, the Israeli lobby, the
Jewish lobby, the pro-Israeli

02:16:26.700 --> 02:16:31.020
lobby here, they claim that
Israel's really a bargain.

02:16:31.020 --> 02:16:32.456
And they've got an argument.

02:16:32.456 --> 02:16:34.830
If these are the things you
want done in the world, yeah,

02:16:34.830 --> 02:16:36.490
it's an argument.

02:16:36.490 --> 02:16:38.070
In fact, I'll just
finally conclude.

02:16:38.070 --> 02:16:41.430
At the same time, 1948,
when the Joint Chiefs

02:16:41.430 --> 02:16:44.100
were noticing that Israel's
the dominant military, one

02:16:44.100 --> 02:16:46.620
of the dominant military
forces in the region,

02:16:46.620 --> 02:16:48.780
the Israeli state
archives, which have since

02:16:48.780 --> 02:16:51.360
been public partially
released, they also

02:16:51.360 --> 02:16:53.320
commented on what was going on.

02:16:53.320 --> 02:16:56.250
And they describe
the Palestinians as--

02:16:56.250 --> 02:16:59.670
what they said-- human
dust and human waste

02:16:59.670 --> 02:17:03.090
who will just become part of
the debris of the Arab world

02:17:03.090 --> 02:17:04.500
as they scatter.

02:17:04.500 --> 02:17:07.410
And that was the
position of the US, too.

02:17:07.410 --> 02:17:10.530
Now, if you've got a choice
between two groups, one of them

02:17:10.530 --> 02:17:14.645
a tough, militarized,
advanced Western state,

02:17:14.645 --> 02:17:16.020
and the other
people who can just

02:17:16.020 --> 02:17:19.237
become more of the human does
that floats around the world,

02:17:19.237 --> 02:17:21.570
you know which one people in
power are going to support.

02:17:21.570 --> 02:17:23.070
And that's still their view.

02:17:23.070 --> 02:17:25.230
That's been their view
from 1948 to today,

02:17:25.230 --> 02:17:27.690
and that explains why
they're doing what they do.

02:17:29.921 --> 02:17:31.879
PRESENTER: I know there
are a lot of questions.

02:17:31.879 --> 02:17:33.420
And what we've
promised to do is have

02:17:33.420 --> 02:17:35.412
an extended
question-and-answer session

02:17:35.412 --> 02:17:37.370
that will now take place
in the student center.

02:17:37.370 --> 02:17:40.261
And we also invite everyone
else to participate

02:17:40.261 --> 02:17:42.260
in other activities that
will be going on there.

02:17:42.260 --> 02:17:45.010
[APPLAUSE]

